1 OCCU Western Roman Emperor (455), Praefectus Urbis Romae (420 CE), Consul of Rome (433, 443 CE), Praetorian Praefect of Italy (439 CE), Patrician (445 CE), and Emperor of Rome (455 CE), cesarz
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Anicius Petronius Maximus Caesar''', Consul of Rome (433, 443 CE), Praetorian Praefect of Italy (439 CE), Patrician (445 CE), and Emperor of Rome (455 CE). He seized power after the death of [http://www.geni.com
2 CONC /people/Valentinian-III-Western-Roman-Emperor/6000000002119493371 Valentinian III], and married Valentinian's widow [http://www.geni.com/people/Lincina-Eudoxia-Augusta-the-Elder/6000000000914324156 Eudoxia] against her will. She i
2 CONC nvited the Vandal king [http://www.geni.com/people/Genseric-the-Lame-King-of-the-Vandals/6000000010556294402 Gaiseric] to come to her rescue. He did, looting the city of Rome. After a reign of 77 days, Maximus was torn to pieces a
2 CONC nd thrown into the Tiber river by palace slaves. He was succeeded by [http://www.geni.com/people/Avitus-Western-Roman-Emperor/6000000000317314011 Avitus]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Petronius Maximus is speculated as the son of [https://www.geni.com/people/Ennodia-Magna/6000000001449960302 Ennodia Magna], and perhaps a descendant of [http://www.geni.com/people/Magnus-Maximus-Emperor-of-the-Western-Roman-Empir
2 CONC e/5365308074870051638 Magnus Maximus]. (Justin Swanström, 2010). 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Foundation for Medieval Genealogy Medlands page on Italy Kings:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ITALY,%20Kings%20to%20962.htm#_Toc203638163
2 CONT
2 CONT PETRONIUS MAXIMUS ([396]-murdered 27 May 455).  
2 CONT
2 CONT Theophanes names "Maximus Maximi nepos" when recording that he murdered Emperor Valentinian[139].  A member of the Roman Anicii family[140].  Iordanes records that Maximus murdered Emperor Valentinian after invading the empire[141
2 CONC ].  
2 CONT
2 CONT He succeeded in 455 as Emperor PETRONIUS MAXIMUS, Emperor in the West.  After his accession, he forced his predecessor's widow to marry him, and her daughter Eudoxia to marry his son, triggering the invasion by Genseric King of th
2 CONC e Vandals to whom the younger Eudoxia had been promised in marriage for his son.  
2 CONT
2 CONT He was killed by the Romans after he attempted to flee[142], although the primary sources on which this is based have not yet been identified.  
2 CONT
2 CONT m firstly ---.  
2 CONT *The first wife of Petronius Maximus was lured to the palace of Emperor Valentinian III who raped her[143], although the primary source on which this is based has not yet been identified.  
2 CONT
2 CONT m secondly ([Mar] 455) as her second husband, EUDOXIA, widow of Emperor VALENTINIAN III, daughter of Emperor THEODOSIUS II, Emperor in the East & his wife Eudoxia --- (422-after 462).  
2 CONT *After the murder of her first husband, she was forced to marry his successor, but was captured during the Vandal invasion and taken to north Africa with her two daughters[144].  Ioannes Malalas records the marriage of "Eudoxia Au
2 CONC gusta, Valentiniani Regis vidua" and "Maximo Tyranno"[145].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Emperor Petronius Maximus & his first (unknown) wife had one child: 
2 CONT
2 CONT i) PALADIUS (-[murdered May 455]).  
2 CONT *The Chronicon of Bishop Idatius records that Maximus installed “filio suo ex priore coniuge Palladio” as cæsar in 455[146].  It is assumed that he was killed at the same time as his father[147].  
2 CONT *m ([Apr] 455) as her first husband, EUDOXIA, daughter of Emperor VALENTINIAN III, Emperor in the West & his wife Eudoxia .  The Chronicon of Bishop Idatius records that Maximus arranged the marriage of “filio suo ex priore coniug
2 CONC e Palladio” and “Valentiniani filiam” in 455[148].  After the death of her father, his successor forced Eudoxia to marry his son, but she was captured by Genseric King of the Vandals during his attack on Rome and taken back to nor
2 CONC th Africa with her mother and sister[149].  The primary source which records her first marriage has not so far been identified.  She married secondly ([455]) as his second wife, Huneric.  The Victoris Tonnennensis Epsicopi Chronic
2 CONC on records that "Hugnericus" married "Valentiniani filiam" who had been abducted from Rome in captivity[150]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT References:
2 CONT
