2 CONT
2 CONT Theophanes names "Maximus Maximi nepos" when recording that he murdered Emperor Valentinian[139]. A member of the Roman Anicii family[140]. Iordanes records that Maximus murdered Emperor Valentinian after invading the empire[141].
2 CONT
2 CONT He succeeded in 455 as Emperor PETRONIUS MAXIMUS, Emperor in the West. After his accession, he forced his predecessor's widow to marry him, and her daughter Eudoxia to marry his son, triggering the invasion by Genseric King of the
2 CONC  Vandals to whom the younger Eudoxia had been promised in marriage for his son.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was killed by the Romans after he attempted to flee[142], although the primary sources on which this is based have not yet been identified.
2 CONT
2 CONT m firstly ---.
2 CONT
2 CONT     The first wife of Petronius Maximus was lured to the palace of Emperor Valentinian III who raped her[143], although the primary source on which this is based has not yet been identified.
2 CONT
2 CONT m secondly ([Mar] 455) as her second husband, EUDOXIA, widow of Emperor VALENTINIAN III, daughter of Emperor THEODOSIUS II, Emperor in the East & his wife Eudoxia --- (422-after 462).
2 CONT
2 CONT     After the murder of her first husband, she was forced to marry his successor, but was captured during the Vandal invasion and taken to north Africa with her two daughters[144]. Ioannes Malalas records the marriage of "Eudoxia 
2 CONC Augusta, Valentiniani Regis vidua" and "Maximo Tyranno"[145].
2 CONT
2 CONT Emperor Petronius Maximus & his first (unknown) wife had one child:
2 CONT
2 CONT i) PALADIUS (-[murdered May 455]).
2 CONT
2 CONT     The Chronicon of Bishop Idatius records that Maximus installed “filio suo ex priore coniuge Palladio” as cæsar in 455[146]. It is assumed that he was killed at the same time as his father[147].
2 CONT     m ([Apr] 455) as her first husband, EUDOXIA, daughter of Emperor VALENTINIAN III, Emperor in the West & his wife Eudoxia . The Chronicon of Bishop Idatius records that Maximus arranged the marriage of “filio suo ex priore coni
2 CONC uge Palladio” and “Valentiniani filiam” in 455[148]. After the death of her father, his successor forced Eudoxia to marry his son, but she was captured by Genseric King of the Vandals during his attack on Rome and taken back to no
2 CONC rth Africa with her mother and sister[149]. The primary source which records her first marriage has not so far been identified. She married secondly ([455]) as his second wife, Huneric. The Victoris Tonnennensis Epsicopi Chronicon
2 CONC  records that "Hugnericus" married "Valentiniani filiam" who had been abducted from Rome in captivity[150].
2 CONT
