1 NOTE Original individual @P3960234250@ (@MS_TREE2.GED0_15GM2@) merged with @P3960391567@ (@MS_TREE2.GED0_15GM2@)
1 NOTE Radegund was born to King Berthar, one of the three kings of Thuringia (a kingdom located in present day Germany), some time in the first half of the 6th century.
2 CONT Radegund's uncle, Hermanfrid, killed Berthar in battle, orphaning her. Then, after allying with the Frankish King Theuderic, Hermanfrid defeated his other brother Baderic. However, having crushed his brothers and seized control of
2 CONC  Thuringia, Hermanfrid reneged on his deal with Theuderic to share sovereignty.
2 CONT In 531 Theuderic returned to Thuringia with his brother Clotaire I. Together they defeated Hermanfrid and conquered his kingdom. Clotaire I also took charge of Radegund, taking her back to Merovingian Gaul with him and making her 
2 CONC his wife.
2 CONT Radegund was one of Clotaire Is four wives (the other three being Chunsina, Ingund and Ingunds sister Aregund). She bore him no children, and, after Clotaire I had her brother assassinated, she turned to God, founding a nunnery 
2 CONC in Poitiers.
2 CONT She was a friend of the poet Venantius Fortunatus and a contemporary of Gregory of Tours. She died on 13 August 586 and her funeral, which both men attended, was three days later.
2 CONT She was canonized as a saint by the church
1 NOTE RESEARCH NOTES:
2 CONT Various sites have different wives of Clotaire I as daughters of Berthar,
2 CONT King of Thuringia, while others have them as daughters of Balderic, King
2 CONT of Thuringia.  Some sites merge several of the wives (different names for
2 CONT same woman).  mariah site discusses the wives individually.  On the assumption
2 CONT that Balderic and Berthar are different, this analysis accepts Radegund as
2 CONT the daughter of Berthar and Ingonde and Aregund as daughters of Balderic.
