1 NOTE Clothar I
2 CONT http://trees.ancestry.com/rd?f=image&guid=feb4a4fa-a4cc-48b8-8280-7b46496b9e62&tid=8764362&pid=-687683575
1 NOTE Served after Clovis from 511-  517, Known as the old, Le Vieux.
1 NOTE Även Chlothaire, Klotar
2 CONT Enade frankerriket
1 NOTE Chlothar I[1] (c. 497 - 29 November 561), called the Old (le Vieux), King of the Franks, was one of the four sons of Clovis. He was born about 497 in Soissons (now in Aisne département, Picardie, France).
2 CONT
2 CONT On the death of his father in 511, he received, as his share of the kingdom, the town of Soissons, which he made his capital; the cities of Laon, Noyon, Cambrai, and Maastricht; and the lower course of the Meuse River. But he was 
2 CONC very ambitious, and sought to extend his domain.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the chief instigator of the murder of his brother Chlodomer's children in 524, and his share of the spoils consisted of the cities of Tours and Poitiers. He took part in various expeditions against Burgundy and, after the d
2 CONC estruction of that kingdom in 534, obtained Grenoble, Die, and some of the neighbouring cities.
2 CONT
2 CONT When the Ostrogoths ceded Provence to the Franks, he received the cities of Orange, Carpentras, and Gap. In 531, he marched against the Thuringii with his nephew Theudebert I and in 542, with his brother Childebert I against the V
2 CONC isigoths of Spain. On the death of his great-nephew Theodebald in 555, Chlothar annexed his territories. On Childebert's death in 558 he became sole king of the Franks.
2 CONT
2 CONT He also ruled over the greater part of Germany, made expeditions into Saxony, and for some time exacted from the Saxons an annual tribute of 500 cows. The end of his reign was troubled by internal dissensions, his son Chram rising
2 CONC  against him on several occasions. Following Chram into Brittany, where the rebel had taken refuge, Chlothar shut him up with his wife and children in a cottage, which he set on fire. Overwhelmed with remorse, he went to Tours to 
2 CONC implore forgiveness at the tomb of St Martin, and died shortly afterwards at the royal palace at Compiègne.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT Chlothar's first marriage was to Guntheuc, widow of his own brother Chlodomer, sometime around 524. They had no children. His second marriage, which occurred around 532, was to Radegund, daughter of Bertachar, King of Thuringia, w
2 CONC hom he and his brother Theuderic defeated. She was later canonized. They also had no children. His third and most successful marriage was to Ingund, by whom he had five sons and two daughters:
2 CONT
2 CONT Gunthar, predeceased father 
2 CONT Childeric, predeceased father 
2 CONT Charibert, King of Paris 
2 CONT Guntram, King of Burgundy 
2 CONT Sigebert, King of Austrasia 
2 CONT Chlothsind, married Alboin, King of the Lombards 
2 CONT His next marriage was to a sister of Ingund, Aregund, with whom he had a son, Chilperic, King of Soissons. His last wife was Chunsina (or Chunsine), with whom he had one son, Chram, who became his father's enemy and predeceased hi
2 CONC m. Chlothar may have married and repudiated Waldrada
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Further reading
2 CONT Bachrach, Bernard S. (1972). Merovingian Military Organization, 481-751. Minneapolis: University of Minnesota Press, ISBN 0 81660 621 8. 
2 CONT Geary, Patrick J. (1988). Before France and Germany: The Creation and Transformation of the Merovingian World. Oxford: Oxford University Press, ISBN 0 19504 458 4. 
2 CONT James, Edward (1991). The Franks. London: Blackwell, ISBN 0 63114 872 8. 
2 CONT Oman, Charles (1914). The Dark Ages, 476-918. London: Rivingtons. 
2 CONT Wallace-Hadrill, J. M. (1962). The Long-Haired Kings, and Other Studies in Frankish History. London: Methuen. 
2 CONT Wood, Ian N. (1994). The Merovingian Kingdoms, 450-751. London: Longman, ISBN 0 58221 878 0. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes
2 CONT ^ Also spelled Chlothachar, Chlotar, Clothar, Clotaire, Chlotochar, or Hlothar, giving rise to Lothair. 
