2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopediaJump to: navigation, search 
2 CONT
2 CONT Flavius Magnus Magnentius 
2 CONT
2 CONT usurper of the Roman Empire 
2 CONT
2 CONT Although Magnentius restored certain rights to the Pagans, the reverse of this coin bears a prominent Christian chi-rho. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Reign January 18, 350 – August 11, 353 
2 CONT
2 CONT Born 303 
2 CONT
2 CONT Birthplace Samarobriva, Gaul 
2 CONT
2 CONT Died August 11, 353 (aged 50) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Place of death Mons Seleucus 
2 CONT
2 CONT Predecessor Constans 
2 CONT
2 CONT Successor Constantius II 
2 CONT
2 CONT Wife Justina 
2 CONT
2 CONT Flavius Magnus Magnentius (303–August 11, 353) was a usurper of the Roman Empire (January 18, 350 – August 11, 353).
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents [hide]
2 CONT
2 CONT 1 Early life and career
2 CONT
2 CONT 2 Usurper
2 CONT
2 CONT 3 Demise
2 CONT
2 CONT 4 Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT 5 References
2 CONT
2 CONT 6 External links
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Early life and career
2 CONT
2 CONT Born in Samarobriva (Amiens), Gaul, Magnentius was the commander of the Herculians and Iovians, the imperial guard units[1]. When the army grew dissatisfied with the behaviour of Roman Emperor Constans, it elevated Magnentius at A
2 CONC utun on January 18, 350. Constans was abandoned by all except a handful of retainers, and he was slain shortly afterwards by a troop of light cavalry near the Pyrenees.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Usurper
2 CONT
2 CONT Magnentius quickly attracted the loyalty of the provinces in Britannia, Gaul, and Hispania, in part because he proved to be far more tolerant towards both Christians and Pagans. His control on Italia and Africa was applied through
2 CONC  the election of his men to the most important offices. However, the short-lived revolt of Nepotianus, a member of the Constantinian dynasty, showed Magnentius that his status of Emperor was to be consolidated against the members 
2 CONC of that dynasty.
2 CONT
2 CONT The self-proclaimed emperor tried to strengthen his grasp on the territories previously controlled by Constans, moving towards the Danube. Vetranio, commander of the Pannonian army, had been elected Augustus by his troops in Mursa
2 CONC  on 1 March. This revolt had a loyalist mark, since Vetranio was supported by Constantina, and Constantius II himself recognized Vetranio, sending him the imperial diadem.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Demise
2 CONT
2 CONT The remaining emperor of the family of Constantine I, Constantius II broke off his war in Syria with Persia, and marched west. Despite Magnentius' efforts to gain Vetranio to his cause, the old general reached Constantius with his
2 CONC  army, and resigned the crown.
2 CONT
2 CONT After electing Magnus Decentius (probably his brother) to Caesar and gathering as many troops as possible, the armies of Magnentius and Constantius met in the Battle of Mursa Major in 351; Magnentius led his troops into battle, wh
2 CONC ile Constantius spent the day of battle praying in a nearby church. Despite Magnentius' heroism, his troops were defeated and forced to retreat back to Gaul.
2 CONT
2 CONT As a result of Magnentius' defeat, Italy ejected his garrisons and rejoined the loyalist cause. Magnentius made a final stand in 353 in the Battle of Mons Seleucus, after which he committed suicide by falling on his sword.
2 CONT
2 CONT Following the suppression of Magnentius' rebellion, Constantius commanded an investigation be made to find his followers. The most notorious agent in this search was the primicerius notariorum Paulus Catena.
2 CONT
2 CONT Some sources state that Magnentius' father was a Briton and his mother a Frank.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magnentius
