2 CONT King Childeric I 's Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse: Basina Andovera of Thuringia (Married) Children: Andelfieda of Merovinga Franks, King Clovis I "the Great" "the Riparian" of Franks
2 CONT
2 CONT Page 212 in "Dawn of European Civilization" has Merovingian chart. It differs a little from some others; Cloderic is same as Childeric.
2 CONT
2 CONT From "Rulers of the World" by R. F. Tapsell: "Childeric I became the third King of the Franks in 458. He fought with the Roman Aegidius against the Wisigoths. When Aegidius died in 464, he was succeeded by Count Paul. Childeric I'
2 CONC s army helped Count Paul push back a Saxon advance from Angers around 466. Count Paul died in 470. They had to repeat this feat around 475, since Count Paul had died and the Saxons once again had attempted to expand into Angers.
2 CONT
2 CONT (Andre Castelot, Histoire de La France, Pages, 176, 200). Born: before 433, son of Merovee=Merovech, Prince des Francs and N?, Childeric I is presumed to have been at least 15 years of age by the time he succeeded his father in 44
2 CONC 8. Between 480 and 481 Childeric I's tomb in Tournai was discovered in 1653 and contained magnificent weapons. Buried: circa 481 in Tournai, Bigorre, France.
2 CONT
2 CONT King of the Salian Franks 456-481, Childeric succeeded his semi-legendary father Merovech as king of the Salian Franks of northern Gaul in 456, during Roman times. He became infatuated with the daughters of his subjects, who were 
2 CONC so incensed about this that they forced him to give up the throne. He discovered that they intended to assassinate him, and he fled to Thuringia, leaving a close friend and telling him to send him a message when Childeric could re
2 CONC turn to his kingdom.
2 CONT
2 CONT Childeric took refuge with Bisinus, King of the Thuringian Franks, and his wife Basina. The king elected by the Franks was cruel, and soon after Childeric was re-called to his kingdom by his friend, and was restored to the throne.
2 CONC  Once Bisinus and Childeric were both kings, Basina deserted her husband and went to live with Childeric, who married her and had a son Clovis.
2 CONT
2 CONT After a battle with Odoacer, King of the Saxons (and conquerer of the Western Roman Empire, 476), at Orleans, Childeric and the Saxon king made a peace treaty and together subdued the Alamanni, who had invaded a part of Italy. In 
2 CONC 481 Childeric died and was succeeded by Clovis, his son by Basina.
2 CONT
2 CONT Childeric I began his rule in 458. He deposed in 459 and restored in 463. He continued to rule until 481.
2 CONT
2 CONT Reign He succeeded his father Merovech (Latinised as Meroveus or Merovius) as king, traditionally in 457 or 458. With his Frankish warband he was established with his capital at Tournai, on lands which he had received as a foedera
2 CONC tus of the Romans, and for some time he kept the peace with his allies.
2 CONT
2 CONT In about 463 in Orléans, in conjunction with the Roman General Aegidius, who was based in Soissons, he defeated the Visigoths, who hoped to extend their dominion along the banks of the Loire River. After the death of Aegidius, he 
2 CONC first assisted Comes ("count") Paul of Angers, together with a mixed band of Gallo-Romans and Franks, in defeating the Goths and taking booty. Odoacer reached Angers but Childeric arrived the next day and a battle ensued. Count Pa
2 CONC ul was killed and Childeric took the city. Childeric, having delivered Angers, followed a Saxon warband to the islands on the Atlantic mouth of the Loire, and massacred them there. In a change of alliances, he also joined forces w
2 CONC ith Odoacer, according to Gregory of Tours, to stop a band of the Alamanni who wished to invade Italy.
2 CONT
2 CONT These are all the facts known about him, and they are not secure. The stories of his expulsion by the Franks, whose women he was taking; of his eight-year stay in Thuringia with King Basin and his wife Basina; of his return when a
2 CONC  faithful servant advised him that he could safely do so by sending to him half of a piece of gold which he had broken with him; and of the arrival in Tournai of Queen Basine, whom he married, are entirely legendary and come from 
2 CONC Gregory of Tours' Libri Historiarum (Book ii.12).
2 CONT
2 CONT He died in 481 and was buried in Tournai, leaving a son Clovis, afterwards king of the Franks.
2 CONT
2 CONT His Tomb Childeric's tomb was discovered in 1653 by a mason doing repairs in the church of Saint-Brice in Tournai where numerous precious objects were found, including a richly ornamented sword, a torse-like bracelet, jewels of go
2 CONC ld and cloisonné enamel with garnets, gold coins, a gold bull's head and a ring with the inscription CHILDERICI REGIS ("of Childeric the king"), which identified the tomb. Some 300 golden bees were also found. Archduke Leopold Wil
2 CONC liam, Spanish governor of the Netherlands, had the find published in Latin, and the treasure went first to the Habsburgs in Vienna, then as a gift to Louis XIV, who was not impressed with them and stored them in the royal library,
2 CONC  which became the Bibliothèque Nationale de France during the Revolution. Napoleon was more impressed with Childeric's bees when he was looking for a heraldic symbol to trump the Bourbon fleur-de-lys. He settled on Childeric's bee
2 CONC s as symbols of the French Empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT On the night of November 5-6, 1831, the treasure of Childeric was among 80 kilos of treasure stolen from the Library and melted down for the gold. A few pieces were retrieved where they had been hidden in the Seine, including two 
2 CONC of the bees. The record of the treasure, however, now exists only in the fine engravings made at the time of its discovery, and in some reproductions made for the Habsburgs.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1 2 3 4 5 6 Birth: 436 in North Rhine-Westphalia, Preussen 7 8 6 2 Death: 26 NOV 481 in Tournai, Hainaut, Wallonie, Belgium 2 Burial: DEC 481 St. Vincent Abbey, Paris, Ville-de-Paris, France 7 8 9 6 2
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Childeric I From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Childeric I (c. 437– c. 481) was the Merovingian king of the Salian Franks from 457 until his death, and the father of Clovis.
2 CONT
2 CONT He succeeded his father Merovech (Latinised as Meroveus or Merovius) as king, traditionally in 457 or 458. With his Frankish warband he was established with his capital at Tournai, on lands which he had received as a foederatus of
2 CONC  the Romans, and for some time he kept the peace with his allies.
2 CONT
2 CONT In about 463 in Orléans, in conjunction with the Roman General Aegidius, who was based in Soissons, he defeated the Visigoths, who hoped to extend their dominion along the banks of the Loire River. After the death of Aegidius, he 
2 CONC first assisted Comes ("count") Paul of Angers, together with a mixed band of Gallo-Romans and Franks, in defeating the Goths and taking booty. Odoacer reached Angers but Childeric arrived the next day and a battle ensued. Count Pa
2 CONC ul was killed and Childeric took the city. Childeric, having delivered Angers, followed a Saxon warband to the islands on the Atlantic mouth of the Loire, and massacred them there. In a change of alliances, he also joined forces w
2 CONC ith Odoacer, according to Gregory of Tours, to stop a band of the Alamanni who wished to invade Italy.
2 CONT
2 CONT The stories of his expulsion by the Franks, whose women he was taking; of his eight-year stay in Thuringia with King Basin and his wife Basina; of his return when a faithful servant advised him that he could safely do so by sendin
2 CONC g to him half of a piece of gold which he had broken with him; and of the arrival in Tournai of Queen Basina, whom he married come from Gregory of Tours' Libri Historiarum (Book ii.12).
2 CONT
2 CONT He died in 481 and was buried in Tournai, leaving a son Clovis, afterwards king of the Franks.
2 CONT
2 CONT His tomb
2 CONT
2 CONT Childeric's tomb was discovered in 1653 (May 27) by a mason doing repairs in the church of Saint-Brice in Tournai, a city in modern Belgium, where numerous precious objects were found, including a richly ornamented sword, a torse-
2 CONC like bracelet, jewels of gold and cloisonné enamel with garnets, gold coins, a gold bull's head and a ring with the inscription CHILDERICI REGIS ("of Childeric the king"), which identified the tomb. Some 300 golden bees were also 
2 CONC found. Archduke Leopold William, governor of the Southern Netherlands (today's Belgium), had the find published in Latin, and the treasure went first to the Habsburgs in Vienna, then as a gift to Louis XIV, who was not impressed w
2 CONC ith them and stored them in the royal library, which became the Bibliothèque Nationale de France during the Revolution. Napoleon was more impressed with Childeric's bees when he was looking for a heraldic symbol to trump the Bourb
2 CONC on fleur-de-lys. He settled on Childeric's bees as symbols of the French Empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT On the night of November 5-6, 1831, the treasure of Childeric was among 80 kilos of treasure stolen from the Library and melted down for the gold. A few pieces were retrieved where they had been hidden in the Seine, including two 
2 CONC of the bees. The record of the treasure, however, now exists only in the fine engravings made at the time of its discovery, and in some reproductions made for the Habsburgs.
2 CONT
2 CONT External links Northvegr webite: Gregory of Tours, Historia Francorum "A note on Childeric's bees": the discovery of his tomb: follow the links for the engravings of Childeric's treasure and the two remaining gold bees.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Childeric I (c. 440– c. 481) was the Merovingian king of the Salian Franks from 457 until his death, and the father of Clovis.
2 CONT
2 CONT He succeeded his father Merovech as king, traditionally in 457 or 458. With his Frankish warband he was established with his capital at Tournai, on lands which he had received as a foederatus of the Romans, and for some time he ke
2 CONC pt the peace with his allies.
2 CONT
2 CONT Childeric's tomb was discovered in 1653 (May 27) by a mason doing repairs in the church of Saint-Brice in Tournai, a city in modern Belgium
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Traditionally called king of the Franks (Roi des Francs) Reign: 456/457 - 481/482 (traditional dates) End of reign: 481/482 (traditional date), died
2 CONT
2 CONT Childéric, named the son of Mérovée by Gregory of Tours (1), was the chief of the Salian Franks, who settled in Belgica Secunda, between the Meuse and Somme rivers. He was exiled to Thuringia for eight years by his own subjects, i
2 CONC rritated by his violence, and replaced with the Roman military commander Ægidius. On his return, Childéric played an important role as an ally of the Romans. He helped the Romans to repel the Visigoths near Orléans, then took Ange
2 CONC rs (c. 463 or c. 469) and allied with Odoacer against the Aleman invasion (476/480).
2 CONT ------------------------
2 CONT From: http://www.fluckers.com/family/daspit/merovingian.html
2 CONT
2 CONT Childeric married his cousin Basina, daughter of Basin, King of Thuringia. Basin was the grandson of Widelphrus and first cousin to Merovius. In 465 A.D. a son was born of this marriage: Clovis, b. 465 A.D. 
2 CONT --------------------
