1 NOTE At about the time that Alaric I and his Gothic warriors were invading Italy, another Teutonic people, the Francs, were living near the mouth of the Rhine River. Of all the Teutonic tribes the Francs were the most powerful and the 
2 CONC strongest. The Francs laid the foundation for two nations, France and Germany. The Francs remained powerful because they didn't break off ties with their homeland. When they invaded the Roman Empire they had kept what territory th
2 CONC ey had and expaned it until they controlled the Roman province of Gaul. Of all the early rulers of the Francs, Clovis was the greatest. Five years after the Teutons had driven the last Roman emperor from his throne, Clovis became 
2 CONC king of the Francs. At that time he was only 15 years old. With Clovis at the head of their army the Francs defeated the Roman governor of Gaul. Before the Francs could lay claim to all of Gaul they had to subdue all the other tri
2 CONC bes that had settled there. By conquering all of the Teutonic tribes one after the other Clovis finally made himself king over all of his country. Clovis worshipped all the old gods of Teuton. His wife, Clotilde was a Christian. S
2 CONC he tried again and again to convert Clovis. One day when Clovis was doing battle and it seemed he would lose, he prayed to the Christian god and  made a promise to become a Christian if he won the day. His army won and Clovis kept
2 CONC  good on his promise. On the following Christmas day he and 300 of his followers were baptised.
2 CONT Source: A World Background For The United States. McLure Scheck Wright: Laidlaw Brothers, Published Chicago, NY, San Francisco, Dallas, Atlanta 1941.
