2 CONT https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Erelieva
2 CONT
2 CONT Ereleuva (before 440 - ca. 500?), concubine of Theodemir and mother of Theodoric the Great.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ben M. Angel notes: The name for this person is spelled in various ways:
2 CONT
2 CONT Ereliva: From the "Anonymus Valesianus," published by Henri Valois ("Valesianus") in the 17th century, based on two documents: "The Lineage of Emperor Constantine" from 390, and "Ex libris Chronicorum inter cetera" on the life of 
2 CONC Theodoric the Great, supposedly written by Bishop Maximianus of Ravenna.
2 CONT
2 CONT Erelieva: From Jordanes in his work Getica
2 CONT
2 CONT Ereleuva: From correspondence between this person and Pope Gelasius I.
2 CONT
2 CONT Eusebia: Her baptismal name (when she became Catholic - she later supposedly converted to Arianism, in accordance with her husband's faith).
2 CONT
2 CONT The following variation I've yet to find a source for other than various online family tree profiles, indicating to me it's probably a mistaken spelling passed along repeated by people copying and pasting on the internet:
2 CONT
2 CONT Erelicia
2 CONT
2 CONT The accepted form for her name among modern historians is Ereleuva, and for that reason, she is named here as such.
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Foundation for Medieval Genealogy page on Italy Kings:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ITALY,%20Kings%20to%20962.htm#Theodemirdied474B
2 CONT
2 CONT THEODEMIR [Thiudimir], son of VANDALARIUS (-Kyrrhos 474).
2 CONT
2 CONT Iordanes names "Thiudemer et Valamir et Vidimir" as the sons of Vandilarius[231].
2 CONT
2 CONT King of the Ostrogoths in Pannonia, under his brother Valamir, he ruled over the western part of their domain which covered the county of Somogy and northeastern Croatia. He succeeded his brother in [468/49] as King of all the Pan
2 CONC nonian Ostrogoths.
2 CONT
2 CONT When the Ostrogoths left Pannonia in [473], Theodemir and his contingent went towards Constantinople. They were settled in Macedonia, based in the city of Kyrrhos[232].
2 CONT
2 CONT m ---. The name of Theodemir's wife is not known.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT Concubine: ERELEUVA [Erelieva].
2 CONT
2 CONT She was baptised a Catholic as EUSEBIA[233]. Iordanes names "Erelieva concubina" as mother of Theodoric[234]. She went with her son to Italy.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT Theodemir & his wife had one child:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. AMALAFRIDA (-murdered [523/25]).
2 CONT
2 CONT Iordanes names "Amalfridam germanam suam [Theoderici]" as the mother of "Theodehadi" and wife of "Africa regi Vandalorum…Thrasamundo"[235].
2 CONT
2 CONT Emperor Zeno used her as ambassador to her half-brother in 487 to thwart his attack on Constantinople[236].
2 CONT
2 CONT Her second marriage was arranged by her half-brother, Theodoric King of Italy, as part of his efforts to foster the support of the Vandals. Amalafrida's dowry was Lilybæum in western Sicily[237].
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of her husband, she unsuccessfully protested his successor's withdrawal of support from her brother, but she was outmanœuvred and killed[238].
2 CONT
2 CONT m firstly [HUGO ---] (-before 500). The Widukindi Res Gestæ Saxonicæ names "Huga rex Francorum…unicam filiam Amalbergam" who married "Irminfredo regi Thuringorum"[239], but there is no indication to whom "Huga rex Francorum" could
2 CONC  refer.
2 CONT
2 CONT m secondly ([500]) THRASAMUND, King of the Vandals, son of [GENTO the Vandal or GELIMER the Vandal] (before 460-523).
2 CONT
2 CONT Amalafrida & her first husband had two children.
2 CONT
2 CONT Theodemir had three illegitimate children by his concubine (Ereleuva):
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. THEODORIC ([451][258]-30 Aug 526).
2 CONT
2 CONT Iordanes names "Theodericum" as son of Theodemir, in a later passage naming his mother "Erelieva concubina"[259].
2 CONT
2 CONT He was proclaimed THEODORIC "the Great" King of Italy in Mar 493 after defeating King Odovacar.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. THEODIMUND. The primary source which names him has not yet been identified.
2 CONT
2 CONT He marched westwards to Durazzo with his brother in 479, leading one of the three marching columns[260].
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. daughter (-[479]). The primary source which records her existence has not yet been identified.
2 CONT
2 CONT She died about the time her half-brother marched westwards to Durazzo[261].
2 CONT
