1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Flavius Richomeres''' ('''Ricimer''' or '''Richomer''') (c405-472), Magister Militum (456 CE), Patricius (457 CE), and Consul of Rome (459 CE). He was Commander of the Suevian army. He rebelled against the Roman
2 CONC  Empire in 456 at Ravenna, deposing the Emperor Avitus. The next four emperors were his nominees and puppets: Majorian (457-461), Severus III (461- 465), Anthemius (467-472), and Olybrius (472).
2 CONT
2 CONT His parentage is highly speculative. Some modern sources suggest that he might have been a son of '''[http://www.geni.com/people/Pakimund/6000000010060043805 Pakimund]'''. However, Sir Anthony Wagner calls him the son of an unknow
2 CONC n Suevic chieftain. He is said to have been a brother of '''[http://www.geni.com/people/Agrippina/6000000011162991104 Agrippina]''' who married Chilperic of Burgundy. No known children.
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Ricimer (c. 405 – August 18, 472; pronounced /ˈrikimer/) was a Germanic general who achieved effective control of the remaining parts of the Western Roman Empire, during the middle of the fifth century A.D. His power was based pri
2 CONC marily on military strength, and he exercised political control through a series of "puppet emperors", whom he enthroned and eliminated.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT Ricimer was married to Alypia, a daughter of Anthemius and Marcia Euphemia. They had no known children.
2 CONT
2 CONT Forrás / Source:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ricimer
