1 NAME Titus Flavius Titianus Praefect of Egypt
2 GIVN Titus Flavius Titianus
2 NSFX Praefect of Egypt
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 95
2 PLAC Rome, Roma, Italy
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 133
1 OCCU Praefect of Egypt 126-133
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Titus Flavius Titianus,n en Roma hacia el 95 Ad.  fue  Prefecto de Egipto, perteneciente al Gens Flavio
2 CONT  ( Rubio)  El Flavius  Titiani descendiente de los Flavios Sabini .
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fabpedigree.com/s008/f744382.htm [???]
2 CONT
2 CONT “Fab Pedigrees" provide no sources of historical merit. Here are two sources, though the second one isn’t utterly convincing:
2 CONT
2 CONT '''From a preview snippet''' from "Rocznik Papirologii Prawniczej", ''The Journal of Jusristic Papyrology'' Volume 35 page 102:
2 CONT "Though the name of the praefect was not preserved, there is no doubt that is was Titus Flavius Titianus . Titus Flavius titianus was Praefecuts Aegypti for an unusually long time in the middle part of Hadrian's reign. The earlies
2 CONC t attestation of him in office is his inscription on the memnon Colossus, IColosse 24, from 20 March, AD 126 (10th year of Hadrian), the latest one -- p.Berol. 1164, from 27 March, AD AD 133 (17th year of Hadrian). For Titus Flavi
2 CONC us Titianus, sse A. Stein "Die Präfekten von Ägypten in der römishen Kiserzeit [=Dessertationes Bermenses. Ser. i. Fasc. I]...." [https://www.google.com/books/edition/Rocznik_Papirologii_Prawniczej/5jhJAAAAYAAJ?hl=en&gbpv=1&bsq=Ti
2 CONC tus+Flavius+Titianus&dq=Titus+Flavius+Titianus&printsec=frontcover]
2 CONT
2 CONT Lady M. R. M Amherst of Hackney's ''A Sketch of Egyptian History'' provides a pithy appendix entry: 
2 CONT "Titus Flavius Titianus -- March 126-August 131."  
2 CONT The book  is not a grand historical book of merit. [https://www.google.com/books/edition/A_Sketch_of_Egyptian_History_from_the_Ea/wltFAAAAYAAJ?hl=en&gbpv=1&dq=Titus+Flavius+Titianus&pg=PA423&printsec=frontcover]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===List of governors of Roman Egypt===
2 CONT Showing on a Wikipedia webpage is this: "126–133: Titus Flavius Titianus". The hyperlink of his name on the page shows little more: "Titus Flavius Titianus, governor of Egypt from AD 126 to 133."
2 CONT # [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_governors_of_Roman_Egypt#Prefects_during_the_Principate]
2 CONT # [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flavia_gens#Flavii_Titiani]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Other sources found in internet and Google Books searches''' for a Titus Flavius Titianus are these, but they do '''not''' mesh with dates and relationships:
2 CONT * https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Titus_Flavius_Titianus_(consul)
2 CONT * https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Titus_Flavius_Postumius_Titianus
2 CONT * "The Histories" by Tacitu. Entering ‘Titus Flavius’ brings up one paragraph, which does not apply. See Book I, paragraph 9; January - March, A.D. 69.  http://classics.mit.edu/Tacitus/histories.1.i.html
2 CONT * The book “Empire X: The Scorpion’s Strike” by Anthony Riches is historical fiction. With the story is set in 189 AD, the Titus Flavius Titianus in the book doesn’t fit.
