1 NOTE OR "ATAULFUS"; KING OF THE VISIGOTHS; BROTHER OF ANOTHER WIFE OF ALARIC I
1 NOTE RESEARCH NOTES:
2 CONT King of the Visigoths in Gaul 410-415; also wed cousin, daughter of Rocesthes
2 CONT of the Visigoths; cousin gives father as "Modares"
1 NOTE _P_CCINFO 89-2
1 NOTE Visigoth King and leader Ataulphus, (Atawulf or Ataulf) died in 415 AD after he had led his army across the Pyrenees in an invasion of the Iberian Peninsula of Spain.  He died after his army had reached Barcelona.  Leaving his bri
2 CONC de, the twice-widowed Galla Placidia (formerly wife of General Constantius who died in 421AD.).
2 CONT
2 CONT Shortly after he became King of the Visigoths, Ataulphus or Atawulf, withdrew the Goths from Italy into Gaul.  He made an alliance with the Roman Emperor Honorius, and then married the Emperor's half sister, Galla Placidia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Abt 415 AD Atawulf of the Visigoths led his army into Spain, crossing the Pyrenees, from Gaul in Southern France.  The Visigoths (also called just the Goths) invaded the Iberian Peninsula taking territory once claimed by the Vanda
2 CONC ls.
2 CONT
2 CONT Shortly after Alaric King of the Visigoths conquered Rome in 410, and died shortly after, his brother-in-law Ataulphus or Atawulf (Ataulf) succeeded Alaric as King and leader of the Visigoths.
1 NOTE _P_CCINFO 2-2438
1 NOTE Also known as, Ataulphis, Atawulf, Alaric was brother in law and cousin of Honorius. Fought on behalf of Honorius.
2 CONT Invaded Spain,
2 CONT Defeated Jovinus.
2 CONT Galla bore him a son who died as an infant. The child was buried in Hispania in a silver plated coffin. Athaulf was forced into Hispania by emperor, Constanius III. Athaulf Succeeded Alaric. He was assassinated while taking a bath
2 CONC .
2 CONT The name Athal Wulf means: Noble Wolf.
2 CONT Sources:
2 CONT Patrick J. Geary, ed., Readings in Medieval History (Ontario: Broadview Press Ltd., 2003), 97.Henry Bradley, The Goths: from the Earliest Times to the End of the Gothic Dominion in Spain (New York: G.P. Putnam's Sons, Second editi
2 CONC on, 1883), chapter 11. Orosius, Historiae adversum paganos (vii.43.4-6), translated in Stephen Williams, Diocletian and the Roman Recovery, Routledge, 1985, 2000, p. 218) Antonio Marchetta, Orosio e Ataulfo nell'ideologia dei rapp
2 CONC orti romano-barbarici (Rome: Istituto Isorico per il Medio Evo) 1987. The first chapter deals with the doubts raised by previous historians as to the authenticity of the discourse. Considering all that Athaulf had accomplished in 
2 CONC his life, he was a genius. Genius likes to write things that have meaning and describe sentiments.
2 CONT "It was at first my wish to destroy the Roman name and erect in its place a Gothic empire. But when experience taught me that the untameable barbarism of the Goths would not suffer then to live under the sawy of the law, I chose t
2 CONC he glory of renewing and maintaining by Gothic strength the fame of Rome."
