2 NOTE “ 30 January 680 ... the death of Balthild, the Anglo-Saxon slave girl turned queen of the Franks.
3 CONT
3 CONT The background to Balthild’s enslavement is a mystery, but it seems she was a native of East Anglia and perhaps an innocent victim of dynastic fighting over the throne. 
3 CONT
3 CONT Her biographer, writing a decade after Balthild’s death, says she taken when very young 'from across the sea' (i.e., from Britain). 
3 CONT In Francia, she became the protégé of  Erchinoald, the top official in the royal palace and the protector of the young king Clovis II. (Of Erchinoald, a chronicler said, “he lined his own pockets, to be sure, but quite moderately”
3 CONC .)
3 CONT
3 CONT In 648, king Clovis II chose her as his bride. When he died nine years later,  Balthild acted as regent for their son Chlothar III until he too came of age.  She spent the last 15 years of her life in retirement (possibly forced) 
3 CONC in the nunnery at Chelles, which is now a suburb of Paris.
3 CONT
3 CONT Within a decade of Balthild’s death, she was on her way to sainthood. 
3 CONT The nuns of Chelles opened the grave and Balthild’s long plaits of hair (greying strawberry-blonde and tied with red, yellow and green silk ribbons) her bones and her burial clothing were all bundled up and placed in a reliquary c
3 CONC asket.  The nuns, thinking ahead, even left authentication slips for future generations!
3 CONT
3 CONT What’s extraordinary is that these relics survived the French Revolution (when they were already 1,100 years old). That’s because Chelles sent its entire medieval collection into hiding before the Revolutionaries got there. 
3 CONT
3 CONT The caskets stayed in a local church until 1983, when a local historian persuaded the Church authorities to open them.
3 CONT
3 CONT To their astonishment, they found the relics that Chelles had been hoarding since its foundation in the 7th-Century. 
3 CONT These included the bones of Queen Balthild. It turns out she was just over 5 feet tall.
3 CONT
3 CONT The fragments of textile show the queen went to the grave in a brown silk dress and a silk girdle woven with animal motifs; a yellow silk veil; and a fringed mantle of red and yellow silk fastened with a small gold brooch. 
3 CONT
3 CONT Surviving in much better condition is a white linen pinafore apron embroidered at the neckline with necklaces, pendants and the cross.   
3 CONT
3 CONT The story goes that the retired queen used to attend mass decked out in all her royal jewels until a visiting holy man rebuked her for her lack of humility in church. 
3 CONT Balthild took off the trappings of wealth, kept one pair of gold bracelets and gave away the rest.  After that, she wore the linen pinafore with the ghostly outline of the jewels she no longer owned.  
3 CONT
3 CONT This way, she advertised her piety to the nuns inside the convent walls ... while at the same, subtly reminding them of the royal power she had given up to join them.
3 CONT
3 CONT For photos by Genevra Kornbluth:
3 CONT https://www.kornbluthphoto.com/TunicBalthild.html” Facebook - The Early Middle Ages, January 30, 2024
