2 CONT King Pharamond b. 370
2 CONT
2 CONT Pharamond or Faramund (c. 370-427) is a legendary early king of the Franks, first referred to in the anonymous 8th century Carolingian text Liber Historiae Francorum, also known as the Gesta regnum Francorum. In this work, which i
2 CONC s customarily dated to 727, the anonymous author begins by writing of a mythical Trojan origin for the Franks. The emphasis of the Liber was upon "constructing a specific past for a particular group of people."
2 CONT
2 CONT Legend
2 CONT
2 CONT The story is told of the election of the first Frankish king. It says that after the death of Sunno, his brother Marcomer, leader of the Ampsivarii andChatti, proposed to the Franks that they should have one single king, contrary 
2 CONC to their tradition. The Liber adds that Pharamond, named as Marchomir's son, was chosen as this first king (thus beginning the tradition of long-haired kings of the Franks), and then states that when he died, his son Chlodiowas ra
2 CONC ised up as the next king. The work says no more of him.
2 CONT
2 CONT Because there is no reference in any source prior to this work to this figure named Pharamond, who is placed prior to Chlodio (that is, before ca. 428), scholars consider him a legendary rather than historical figure. As a matter 
2 CONC of fact in several sources, for example Gregory of Tours, multiple kings are attested to rule simultaneously in later times.[citation needed] It is thus a dubious matter to assume that, even if Pharamond existed, he was ever recog
2 CONC nized as sole king. The first king of the Franks who may have been close to this position was Clovis I, but after his death his empire was divided again amongst his sons, who ruled again simultaneously.
2 CONT
2 CONT The myth of Pharamond has led to new legends and romances in later times. In past times this has led to attempts to falsely write Pharamond into Prosper Tiro. Martin Bouquet at a much later date invented an entire history of Phara
2 CONC mond.
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.themorrisclan.com/GENEALOGY/PHARAMOND.html PHARAMOND King of the Franks HUSBAND:- PHARAMOND. (Faramond, Faramund, Faramundus). First King of all the Franks. King of Westphalia. First King of the Merovingians. Born in 37
2 CONC 0 in Westphalia, Germany; son of MARCOMIR, Duke of the Franks. Pharamond is known as the first King of France after the departure of the Romans from Gaul. He is not mentioned by Roman historians of the 4-5th centuries, or by Grego
2 CONC ry of Tours, so most modern historians disregard him as a historical person, but regard him as an invention to establish a dubious dynastic continuity.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Liber Historiae Francorum, also known as the Gesta regnum Francorum, which is customarily dated to 727 AD, gives a different father for Pharamond. It is noted though that this work is incorrect in other historical datings.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married (1) Ymbergide (Imberltrude). They did not have children.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married (2) Argotta in 394 AD.
2 CONT
2 CONT The story is told of the election of the first Frankish king. It says that after the death of Sunno, Marcomir proposed to the Franks that they should have one single king, contrary to their tradition. The Liber adds that Pharamond
2 CONC , named as Marcomir's son, was chosen as this first king, and then states that when he died, his son Chlodio was raised up as the next king. The work says no more of him.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was Duke of the East Franks in 404, and then also Duke of the West Franks in 419, and King of Westphalia.
2 CONT
2 CONT In Prosper of Aquitaine, we read, under the year 417, the twenty-sixth year of Honorius: Faramundus regnat in Francia. (S12).
2 CONT
2 CONT In several sources, for example Gregory of Tours, who wrote Historia Francorum, multiple kings are attested to rule simultaneously in later times, so it was not always the rule to have only one king over the Franks.
2 CONT
2 CONT He is believed to have led his people across the Rhine River heading west in 420 AD, separating his people from the Ripuarian Franks settlement near Cologne.
2 CONT
2 CONT He died in (427-S1)(428-S2)(430-S4,S5).
2 CONT
2 CONT NOTE: David Hughes makes the following statement, without citing and sources:
2 CONT
2 CONT The genealogy of the early Frankish kings was confused by medieval clerics who had few sources available to them, but modern scholarship has come along to untangle the mess using multiple sources that have come to be available ove
2 CONC r the years from discoveries made in the libraries of old monasteries. For example, the 1st, 2nd, & 3rd Frankish kings, namely, Faramond [Pharamond], Chlodio, and Merovech, are NOT father, son, and grandson, as they appear in the 
2 CONC traditional genealogy of the Merovingians, but rather each came from entirely different families. Faramond's mother and both of his wives were Frankish princesses. His successor, Chlodio, murdered Faramond's only son upon Faramond
2 CONC 's death to clear his own succession. Chlodio's wife, Argotta [aka Siegse], was one of Faramond's two daughters. Argotta brought into the marriage a son, Merovech, from a previous marriage to Quintus Tarus, Prefect of Provence. It
2 CONC  was the civil war between Merovech and his half-brother, Cloderic, that was the pretext for the invasion of Gaul by Attila "The Hun". And, the confusion of the parentage of King Clovis "The Great", who was not the son of the Fran
2 CONC kish King Childeric I, is due to the fact that the wife of Prince Clovis [King Childeric I's son], namely, Dochilt, married [the future] King Clovis "The Great" upon the premature death of her husband, Prince Clovis [King Childeri
2 CONC c I's son], thus, the misidentification of Prince Clovis [King Childeric I's son] with King Clovis "The Great" by medieval clerics is understandable. (S10).
2 CONT
2 CONT WIFE (1): Ymbergide (Imberltrude). She married Pharamond, King of the Franks. They did not have children.
2 CONT
2 CONT WIFE (2): ARGOTTA. Queen Of France. (Princess of the SALIC FRANKS, Argotta De Cimbres)(Siegse)). Born in 376; daughter of GENEBALD (Genbald), Duke Of the Franks. Argotta is sometimes listed as the daughter of the King of the Cimbr
2 CONC i (Cambray).
2 CONT
2 CONT CHILDREN of PHARAMOND King of the Franks, and ARGOTTA:
2 CONT
2 CONT     CLODIUS, Long Haired. (Le Chevelu). (Clodius V, King of Westphalia, Clodio, Clodian, Le Chevelu). King of France, King of Cologne. Born (in 395)(about 352). He married Basina of Thuringia. He died in 447 (430)(449). Note that 
2 CONC David Hughes (S10) says he was not the son of Pharamond, but the son-in-law.
2 CONT     Fredemundus DESPOSYNI.
2 CONT     Adalbert (Alberico, Adelbertus). Duke of MOSEL. Born in 405 AD. He died in 491 AD.
2 CONT     (Frotmund?). Ancestor of Viviane del Acqs. (S2). She would have been named after her grandmother. 
2 CONT
