2 CONT Tonantius Ferreolus (also called Tonance Ferréol in modern French) (between about 440 and 450 – between 511 to after 517), vir clarissimus between 507 and 511, was a Gallo-Roman Senator who lived in Narbonne, then called Narbo, be
2 CONC tween about 479 and 517. He was also present and seen at Rome in 469 and 475 and was known to be a friend and relative of Sidonius Apollinaris. He was the son of Tonantius Ferreolus and wife Papianilla. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Papianilla is generally regarded as belonging to the Arvernian family of the Aviti, though in a generation senior to Sidonius' wife of the same name.[1] The younger Tonantius' wife's name was thought to have been lost to the ages 
2 CONC but, according to the reference below she very likely was an Industria who lived at Narbonne, born ca 450 to 460, whom he married after 475, daughter, possibly, of Flavius Probus [2], Gallo-Roman Senator, and his wife Eulalia, cou
2 CONC sin-german (first cousin) of Sidonius Apollinaris[3]). No civil or church offices are known for the younger Tonantius Ferreolus [1] He had several siblings whose names are not preserved. There is some argument as to whether Ferreo
2 CONC lus of Narbo referred to as husband of Industria and father of Firminus is Tonantius Ferreolus or a brother
2 CONT
2 CONT Issue: Ferreolus, Senator of Narbonne, father of the Gallo-Roman Senator Ansbertus
2 CONT
2 CONT Ruricius (d. 506, 507 or shortly after 507), Bishop of Uzès 
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Firminus, Bishop of Uzès in 507 (ca 490 – 538, 551 or October 11, 553), Feast Day October 11[16], [17]
2 CONT     * Fidentius, the father of St. Ferreolus, Bishop of Uzès (553 ; January 4, 581), Feast Day January 4, and Saint Tarsicia of Rodez (died ca 600), Feast Day January 15 Mommarets and Kelley [18] that Ferreolus Bishop of Uzes was 
2 CONC son of a Ferreolus, presumably Ferreolus, Senator of Narbonne above.
