2 CONC d Jane Bethune, " The Proelium Cibalense et Proelium Campi Ardiensis : The First Civil War of Constantine I and Licinius I," AncW, 21[1990] 90).
2 CONT
2 CONT 11Moreau, as in n. 10; Kienast, as in n.10.
2 CONT
2 CONT 12Jones-Martindale-Morris, PLRE I 222 and 409-10; Barnes, New Empire 43.
2 CONT
2 CONT 13Mattingly-Sydenham, RIC VII 26 and 493; On the use of the title nobilissimus/nobilissima see Maria R. Alföldi, "Helena nobilissima femina : Zur Deutung der Trierer Deckengemälde," Jahrbuch für Numismatik 10 (1959/60) 79-90 at 80
2 CONC -81.
2 CONT
2 CONT 14Maria R. Alföldi, Die constantinische Goldprägung (Mainz, 1963) 95 and 144; Mattingly-Sydenham, as in n.1; R. A. G. Carson, Coins of the Roman Empire (New York, 1990) 170 and pl. 45, nos. 669 and 670, not quite correctly. That t
2 CONC his occurred exactly on 8 November 324, when Constantius II was elevated to the rank of Caesar, is an attractive conjecture but not supported by our sources.
2 CONT
2 CONT 15 Euseb. Vita Const. 3.47; Sozomen, Hist. Eccl . 2.2.4.
2 CONT
2 CONT 16 Eutropius 10.6.3 ( MGH, AA II 174; ed. Santini 67; ed. Friedhelm L. Müller [Stuttgart 1995] 142).
2 CONT
2 CONT 17Aur. Vict. Epit . 41.11-12; We need give little credence to John Chrysostom, In Ep. ad Phil. Comm . 4.15.5 (Migne, PG LXII 295), who claims to know that Fausta was bound naked to the mountains and exposed to the wild beasts. Joh
2 CONC n Holland Smith, Constantine the Great (London, 1971) 215, misreading John Chrysostom, transfers the scene to the amphitheater.
2 CONT
2 CONT 18 Zosimus 2.29.1-2; Zonaras 13.2.38-41.
2 CONT
2 CONT 19Drijvers, "Flavia Maxima Fausta" 505-506, concludes that we shall never know.
2 CONT
2 CONT 20Paschoud, Cinq études 37-38, and others before him; more recently Drijvers, "Flavia Maxima Fausta" 504 with n. 27.
2 CONT
2 CONT 21 CIL X 678 = Dessau, ILS 710.
2 CONT
2 CONT 222.29.3-4.
2 CONT
2 CONT 23 Caes. 336A-B.
2 CONT
2 CONT 24 Hist. Franc. 1.34 in Migne, PL LXXI 179; 1.36 in MGH, SRM 1st ed. I 51; ibid. 2nd ed. I.1 26-27.
2 CONT
2 CONT 25Chris Scarre, Chronicle of the Roman Emperors (London, 1995) 216.
2 CONT
2 CONT 26It has been supposed by some that it was in Trier: Ramsay MacMullen, Constantine (New York, 1969) 50 and 187; Rougé, "Fausta, femme de Constantin" 4 and 13; W. H. C, Frend, The Rise of Christianity (Philadelphia, 1984) 496 and 5
2 CONC 01; Drijvers, "Flavia Maxima Fausta" 506, rightly deems Rome the more likely place; Scarre (see previous note) also seems to think that it was in Rome.
2 CONT
2 CONT 27 Or. 1.9B-C.
