1 OCCU Clarissima Femina, Nicht van Avitus
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Papianilla''', ''clarissima femina''. According to [http://www.geni.com/people/Saint-Sidonius-Apollinarius-Bishop-of-Clermont/6000000009437556787 Sidonius], a contemporary source, Ferreolus' wife was a partner t
2 CONC o her husband and shared his troubles. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Her ancestry is uncertain. She was probably a relative of another [http://www.geni.com/people/Papianilla/6000000001353843878 Papianilla], wife of [http://www.geni.com/people/Saint-Sidonius-Apollinarius-Bishop-of-Clermont/600000000
2 CONC 9437556787 Sidonius Apollinaris]. Therefore, according to Settipani she was most probably a niece of Avitus (1989 and 1991, pp. 198-9). However, Harries says she was a generation older, perhaps a sister or cousin of Avitus (1994, 
2 CONC p.34, n. 30), while Mommaerts and Kelley suggest she might have been a generation younger (1992, pp. 112-13).
2 CONT
2 CONT In Settipani's reconstruction she was a granddaughter of [http://www.geni.com/people/Pontius-Anicilus/6000000000316672378 Pontius Anicilus], either through his son [http://www.geni.com/people/Adelphius-Bishop-of-Limoges/6000000000
2 CONC 316609610 Adelphius, bishop of Limoges] or his son [http://www.geni.com/people/Hermogenianus/6000000000316685236 Hermogenianus], one of whom married a sister of Avitus whose name is reconstructed as [http://www.geni.com/people/Epa
2 CONC rchia/6000000003827918217 Eparchia].
2 CONT
2 CONT According to some, she was the daughter of [https://www.geni.com/people/Zeno-Eastern-Roman-Emperor/6000000002106208023 Zénon l'Isaurien], Commander of the Army of the Eastern Empire and later Eastern emperor. Her marriage, in whic
2 CONC h Emperor Avitus had served as an intermediary, was celebrated in Constantinople and everyone tried to hide the family of his new wife from Tonance. By bringing Papinianille to Narbonne and Arles, Tonance had introduced into the h
2 CONC ouse of his father a Nestorian heretic.
2 CONT
2 CONT == Sources ==
2 CONT
2 CONT * Christian Settipani, "Continuite Gentilice et Continuite Familiale Dans Les Familles Senatoriales Romaines A L'epoque Imperiale, Mythe et Realite, Addenda I - III" (juillet 2000- octobre 2002) (n.p.: ''Prosopographica et Genealo
2 CONC gica'', 2002).
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the Wikipedia page of her husband, Tonantius Ferreolus I (Prefect):
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tonantius_Ferreolus_(prefect)
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Papianilla, clarissima femina, born ca 415, a niece of Emperor Avitus (and as such would be the granddaughter of Flavius Julius Agricola, b. c365, Consul of Rome in 421, but uncertain if he is a paternal or maternal gra
2 CONC ndfather) and the first cousin of another Papianilla, wife of Sidonius Apollinaris, and they had many children, among whom Tonantius Ferreolus. She was a partner who shared his troubles, according to Sidonius.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to her Wikipedia page:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Papianilla_(wife_of_Tonantius_Ferreolus)
2 CONT
2 CONT Papianilla was a Roman noblewoman
2 CONT
2 CONT She was the wife of Tonantius Ferreolus,[1] another Papianilla, the wife of the poet Sidonius Apollinaris, was a relative of hers.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT She had Tonantius Ferreolus and other sons.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.^ Sidonius Apollinaris, Carmina, XXIV 34-38.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.^ Sidonius Apollinaris, Epistles, II 9.3; VII 12.1.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.^ Sidonius Apollinaris, Carmina, XXIV 34-38; Epistles, II 9.7.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT "Papianilla 1", Prosopography of the Later Roman Empire, Volume 2, p. 830.
2 CONT
2 CONT Les ancêtres de Charlemagne, 1989, Christian Settipani
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the Wikipedia page of her son, Tonantius Ferreolus II (Senator):
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tonantius_Ferreolus_(senator)
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the son of Tonantius Ferreolus and wife Papianilla. Papianilla is generally regarded as belonging to the Arvernian family of the Aviti, though in a generation senior to Sidonius' wife of the same name.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the Wikipedia page of her possible grandfather, Agricola (Consul 421):
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flavius_Julius_Agricola
2 CONT
2 CONT Agricola, full name possibly Julius Agricola (ca. 365 – after 421) was a West Roman statesman who served twice as praetorian prefect and became consul for 421.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT He was possibly from Gaul, specifically Narbo.[1] 
2 CONT
2 CONT His relations are unclear: his exact parentage is unknown, as are his immediate descendants. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the grandfather of Flavius Magnus, consul in 460. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He may have had a son named Nymphidius. He was also a relative, and perhaps even the father, of the Emperor Avitus (r. 455–456).[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT He served twice as praetorian prefect. The first tenure was sometime before 418, but the exact circumscription is unknown; it was most probably in the Western half of the Empire however.[1] The second time he served as praetorian 
2 CONC prefect of Gaul in 418. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was then appointed to the consulship for 421, with Fl. Eustathius as his colleague.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.^ a b c d Martindale & Morris (1980), p. 36
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.^ Martindale & Morris (1980), p. 37
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography
2 CONT
2 CONT Martindale, John R.; Morris, John (1980), The Prosopography of the Later Roman Empire - Volume II, AD 395–527, Cambridge University Press, ISBN 978-0521201599
