1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grimoald_III_of_Benevento
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Grimoald III '''(c. 760 – 806) was the Lombard Prince of Benevento from 788 until his own death. He was the second son of Arechis II and Adelperga. In 787, he and his elder brother Romoald were sent as hostages to Charlemagne w
2 CONC ho had descended the Italian peninsula as far as Salerno to receive the submission of Benevento. In return for peace, Arechis recognised Charlemagne's suzerainty and handed Grimoald over as a hostage.
2 CONT
2 CONT When his father and brother both died in 788, Grimoald was allowed to return to Italy. He recognised Frankish overlordship, but was permitted practical independence in return for defending Italy from the Eastern Romans. In 788 he 
2 CONC faced a Roman invasion commanded by Adelchis, the son of the last Lombard king, Desiderius. A Frankish army under Winigis and Hildebrand, Duke of Spoleto, joined Grimoald and defeated Adelchis on the coast soon after his landing.
2 CONT
2 CONT Later, Grimoald tried to throw off Frankish suzerainty, but [https://www.geni.com/people/Charlemagne/6000000002457013227 Charlemagne]'s sons, [https://www.geni.com/people/Pipino-re-d-Italia/6000000003962328571 Pepin of Italy] and 
2 CONC [https://www.geni.com/people/Charles-the-Younger-King-of-the-Franks/6000000010615374324 Charles the Younger], forced him to submit in 792.
2 CONT
2 CONT -------
2 CONT
2 CONT == MedLands ==
2 CONT
2 CONT *http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/SOUTHERN%20ITALY,%20PRE-NORMAN.htm#Arichisdied787
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT   Duke Arichis & his wife had five children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT b. '''GRIMOALD''' (-Apr 806, bur Salerno Cathedral).  "Rumoldum et Grimoldum" are named as sons of "Aragisus" by Einhard[116].  The Chronicon Salernitanum names (in order) "Romoald, Grimoald et Gisifum, Theoderadam et Adelchisam" 
2 CONC as children of Duke Arichis and Adalperga[117].  Grimoald was taken by Charles I King of the Franks as a hostage for his father's good behaviour, but after the death of his father his mother persuaded the king to release him[118].
2 CONC   The Annales Beneventani record that he was installed as '''GRIMOALD III Prince of Benevento''' in succession to his father[119], in return for recognising Carolingian suzerainty.  Einhard records the deaths (in 786) of "Aragisus
2 CONC  dux Beneventanorum…Rumoldo maiore filio suo" and the accession of "minore ducis filio Grimoldo"[120].  The Annales Regni Francorum record that, in 788, he won an overwhelming victory over Byzantine forces in Calabria[121].  The d
2 CONC ating clause of a charter dated Oct 792, under which "Melonianus" donated property to Cava monastery, refers to the fifth year of "Grimoald…dux gentis Langobardorum"[122].  The dating clause of a charter under which "Trasemundus f
2 CONC i[lius] Quodescalci" donated property "in Borfoniana finibus Apulie" to Montecassino refers to "temporibus domni Grimualdi Longobardorum gentis principis, anno septimo principatus eius, men Martio, quintadecima indict" (Mar [792])
2 CONC [123].  Grimoald revolted against the Franks in 792, from which time he issued coins in his own name.  He also refused to shave his beard despite the Carolingian order to do so[124].  Einhard records that "Grimaldus Beneventanorum
2 CONC  dux" captured "Winigisum comitem Spoletii" at "Luceria" and released him the following year[125].  The Annales Fuldenses record that "Winigisus dux Spolitanus" was besieged and captured by "Grimoldo" in 802, but released in the f
2 CONC ollowing year[126].  The Annales Regni Francorum also record that, in 802, Grimoald captured Winichis dux of Spoleto at Lucera, and released him the following year[127].  The dating clause of a charter under which "Lupo fi[lius] q
2 CONC [uon]dd[am] Gavioli" sold property "in vico Bonelle" to Montecassino refers to "tempori dom…Grimoalt summo magno princ Langobardorum genti, quinto decimo anno, men Aprile per indict undecima" (Apr [803])[128].  The dating clause o
2 CONC f a charter under which "Vitaliano fi[lius] Vitali" sold property "in Prata, loco…Vico Bonelle" to Montecassino refers to "temporibus dom Grimoaldi summo magni princ Langobardorum ientis, octabo decimo anno princ eius, men Ianuari
2 CONC o per indict quarta decima" (Jan [806])[129].  The Annales Beneventani record the death of Prince Grimoald in 806[130].  The Chronicon Salernitanum records that "Grimoald" was buried "in…ecclesia sedis Salernitanæ iuxta sepulchra 
2 CONC patris fratrisque"[131].  The Epitaphium Grimoaldi records that "vixisti septem [ter binis floridus annis]"[132].  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' ( [789/91], repudiated [795] ) EUANTHIA, daughter of --- & his wife Hypatia.  Erchempert records that "Grimoalt neptem augusti Achivorum in coniugium sumpsit nomine Wautiam"[133].  The Vita Philareti by Niketas of Amnia re
2 CONC cords the marriage of Euanthia, granddaughter of [https://www.geni.com/people/Saint-Philaretos-of-Phaplagonien/4039830539610027513 Philaretos] (and sister of Maria, first wife of [https://www.geni.com/people/Konstantinos-VI-Porphy
2 CONC rog%C3%A9nete-Byzantine-Emperor/6000000000417201595 Emperor Konstantinos VI]), to "un roi des Lombards nommé Argousis"[134].  Her repudiation is referred to in a letter from Theodore the Studite to Emperor Konstantinos VI reproach
2 CONC ing him of his licentious lifestyle alleging that it provoked "la répudiation d´une épouse légitime dans une contrée lointaine"[135].  The dating of the repudiation is presumably around the same time as Emperor Konstantinos repudi
2 CONC ated his first wife [dated to Jan 895], which is recorded by Theophanes[136]. 
2 CONT
