1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Herod of Chalcis (d. 48 AD), also known as Herod V, was a son of Aristobulus IV, and the grandson of Herod the Great''', Roman client king of Judaea. He was the brother of Herod Agrippa I and Herodias. He was ve
2 CONC sted the rulership of Chalkis, a kingdom north of Judaea, as tetrarch. After the death of his brother, he was also given responsibility for the Temple in Jerusalem, as well as the appointment of the Temple's High Priest. Ananias w
2 CONC as appointed by him.[citation needed]
2 CONT
2 CONT His first wife was his cousin, Mariamne.[1] She bore him a son, who was also named Aristobulus, and who eventually became ruler of Chalcis.
2 CONT
2 CONT After Mariamne's death, he married his niece Berenice, with whom he had two sons, Berenicianus and Hyrcanus.
2 CONT
2 CONT After his death the kingdom was given to Herod Agrippa II.
2 CONT
2 CONT ^1 This Mariamne was the daughter of Joseph ben Joseph (nephew of Herod) and Herod's daughter Olympias, who was the sister of Herod 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Herod_of_Chalcis
2 CONT
2 CONT >>><nowiki>--------------</nowiki>
2 CONT
2 CONT Amongst the ward hostages to Antonia Minor in Rome were the four children of Aristobulus IV and his cousin, Berenice, daughter of Costobarus and Salome. He was the son of Herod the Great and his second wife, Mariamne I, the last o
2 CONC f the Hasmoneans:
2 CONT
2 CONT #Aristobulus Minor, grandson to Herod the Great and youngest son of prince Aristobulus IV and princess Berenice of Judea.
2 CONT #'''Herod of Chalcis, who became responsible for the Temple in Jerusalem and the appointment of the High Priest. He married (i) his cousin, Mariamne and after her death, (ii) his niece Berenice, with whom he had two sons, Berenici
2 CONC anus and Hyrcanus.'''
2 CONT #Herodias, who married (i) her uncle, Herod II, son of Herod the Great and Mariamne II, daughter of the high priest Simon Boethus; (ii) another uncle, Herod Antipas, tetrarch of Galilee and Peraea.
2 CONT #Mariamne III, who married Antipater III, her uncle and eldest son of Herod; after Antipater’s execution in 4 BCE, she may have been the first wife of another uncle, Herod Archelaus, ethnarch of Judea.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://historyhuntersinternational.org/2010/06/05/chrestians-and-the-lost-history-for-classical-antiquity/
2 CONT
2 CONT >>><nowiki>-------------------------------------</nowiki>
2 CONT
