1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''
2 CONT =Tiberius Claudius Balbilus=
2 CONT '''
2 CONT
2 CONT Tiberius Claudius Balbilus, his full name Tiberius Claudius Balbillus Modestus,[1] also known as Tiberius Claudius Balbillus, Barbillus, Babilus, Balbillus and Balbillus the Wise (Tiberius Claudius Balbillus Modestus in Greek: Τιβ
2 CONC έριος Κλαύδιος Βαλβίλλος Μόδεστος, 3-79) was a distinguished Ancient Roman Scholar, Politician and a court Astrologer to the Roman emperors Claudius, Nero and Vespasian in the 1st century.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Descent, Family Background & Early Life'''
2 CONT
2 CONT The assuming birthplace of Balbilus has been Alexandria, in the Roman Province of Egypt.[9] Balbilus was a Roman Egyptian nobleman mostly of Greek and partly of Armenian and Median descent. Balbilus was the son and the younger chi
2 CONC ld born to Tiberius Claudius Thrasyllus also known as Thrasyllus of Mendes a Grammarian, Literary Commentator, Court Astrologer who became the personal friend of the Roman emperor Tiberius and Aka II of Commagene, a Princess from 
2 CONC the Kingdom of Commagene being a great, granddaughter of Antiochus I Theos of Commagene and his wife, Isias Philostorgos.
2 CONT
2 CONT Balbilus had one known sibling, an elder unnamed sister. His sister married the Eques Lucius Ennius. Through her, Balbilus was the maternal uncle of Ennia Thrasylla who married the Praetorian prefect of the Praetorian Guard Naeviu
2 CONC s Sutorius Macro and perhaps, Lucius Ennius who was the father of Lucius Ennius Ferox, a Roman Soldier who served during the reign of the Roman emperor Vespasian from 69 until 79. Although, Balbilus was born and raised in Egypt, h
2 CONC e was of the Roman equestrian order. Balbilus was a friend of Tiberius’ nephew, Claudius, whom Balbilus knew from when they were children and had met at his father’s house.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to a surviving Papyrus found in Theogonis Egypt, dated August 26, 34, mentions Balbilus as one of the owners of a bathhouse located in the city and the Papyrus mentions the Lease of the Bathhouse and taxation paid from i
2 CONC ts revenue. In the reign of Roman emperor Caligula 37-41, Balbilus left Rome and returned to Alexandria.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Political career'''
2 CONT
2 CONT After the assassination of Caligula on January 24, 41, his paternal uncle Claudius succeeded him as Roman emperor. The political career of Balbilus started in the reign of Claudius. Balbilus left Alexandria to go to Rome, to suppo
2 CONC rt Claudius as a friend when he became emperor.[26] Balbilus accompanied Claudius on his expedition to Britain in 43 in a military capacity, serving as an officer in the 20th legion (Legio XX Valeria Victrix). He served in the Rom
2 CONC an legion, as a Military Tribune and the Commander of the Military Engineers, thus being of the first Greek people to be in Britain. When Claudius with the Roman Legions returned from Britain to Rome, Balbilus was awarded a crown 
2 CONC of honor.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Suetonius (Claud.28,1), Claudius rewarded Balbilus a Hasta Pura and perhaps a corona aurea during the Triumph to celebrate the conquest of Britain in 44. As Balbilus was a part of his retinue, it seems likely that his
2 CONC  awards, as much as his military rank, were honorary.
2 CONT
2 CONT Balbilus was one of the highest ranking Equestrian Magistrates that served in Rome. After Balbilus returned to Rome from the Roman conquest of Britain, he received an important post in Egypt. In Alexandria, Balbilus was appointed 
2 CONC High Priest at the Temple of Hermes and Director of the Library, he split his time between Alexandria and Rome. Sometime after he served as a Procurator of the Asia province.
2 CONT
2 CONT In October 54, Claudius died and was succeeded by Nero as Roman emperor. Under Nero, Balbilus was appointed Prefect of Egypt where he served from 55 until 59. After his Prefecture ended in Egypt, Balbilus continued to live in Alex
2 CONC andria.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Astrology'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Balbilus succeeded to his father’s skill in astrology. He was a leading astrologer of his time in Rome. He remained in Rome during Claudius’ reign as his advisor, after he had passed an edict expelling all astrologers from the cit
2 CONC y and foretold an eclipse which fell on one of the emperor’s birthdays.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the reign of Nero 54 til 68, Balbilus served as an astrological adviser to him and his mother, Agrippina the Younger. A comet had passed across the sky in either 60 or 64, signalling the death of a great personage. Balbilus
2 CONC  tried to calm Nero’s fears by noting that the usual solution was to murder prominent citizens, thus appeasing the Gods and Nero agreed, killing many nobles. As Balbilus proved to be a distinct Astrologer, he avoided the fatal end
2 CONC  of many astrologers under Nero.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the reign of Roman emperor Vespasian from 69 until 79, Balbilus returned to Rome from Alexandria. In Rome, Balbilus served as an Astrologer to Vespasian and found favor with the emperor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Balbilus was a learned man. Seneca the Younger describes him as ‘an excellent man of most rare learning in every branch of studies’. He wrote an astrology treatise, titled Astrologumena which fragments from the book have survived.
2 CONC  The book was addressed to Hermogenes.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Family & Issue'''
2 CONT
2 CONT The identity of the wife of Balbilus is unknown and most probably she was a Greek noblewoman perhaps from the aristocracy of the Roman Near East. There is a possibility that the wife of Balbilus may have been royalty regardless, i
2 CONC f she was Commagenian or other. From the union with his wife, Balbilus had a daughter called Claudia Capitolina. Claudia Capitolina is his only known child. The nomen Claudia she inherited from her father’s family while the Cognom
2 CONC en Capitolina, she may have inherited from her mother’s family.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 64, Capitolina married her second paternal cousin the Prince from Commagene, Gaius Julius Archelaus Antiochus Epiphanes, son of King Antiochus IV of Commagene, and his sister-wife, Queen Iotapa. Capitolina bore Epiphanes one so
2 CONC n, called Gaius Julius Antiochus Epiphanes Philopappos and one daughter, called Julia Balbilla.
2 CONT
2 CONT Balbilus has two further namesakes the Emesene Priests of the cult of El-Gebal in Rome, Tiberius Julius Balbillus and his relative, Titus Julius Balbillus who lived in the second half of the 2nd century and the first half of the 3
2 CONC rd century. Like Balbilus, Tiberius Julius Balbillus and Titus Julius Balbillus were descendants of the King Antiochus I Theos of Commagene, through Balbilus’ maternal uncle Iotapa who married into the Emesani dynasty.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Posthumous Honors'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Although Balbilus died in 79, he may have died in June of that year around the same time that Vespasian did. In his later years Balbilus lived in Ephesus. Vespasian granted privileges to him and his city of Ephesus because of his 
2 CONC proficiency in the art.
2 CONT
2 CONT As Vespasian thought very highly of him and favored him to the extent, that he dedicated and allowed Ephesians to institute games held in his honor. The games held in his honor became a sporting festival called the Balbillean Game
2 CONC s. Named after him, the event was held at Ephesus from 79 well into the 3rd century. An inscription in Ephesus honors Balbilus and his daughter.
2 CONT
2 CONT Balbilus is honored by his granddaughter Julia Balbilla in two epigrams in Aeolic Greek which is dated in 130. The two epigrams are a part of four epigrams recorded which are inscribed and are preserved on lower parts on one of th
2 CONC e Colossi of Memnon. The Colossi of Memnon are two massive stone statues built by the Egyptian Pharaoh Amenhotep III (flourished 14th century BC), to stand guard at the entrance of Amenhotep’s memorial temple. Balbilla had travell
2 CONC ed to Egypt and was the escort to the Roman emperor Hadrian with his wife, Vibia Sabina in the country. The inscriptions that Balbilla commissioned commemorated their visit to the country.
2 CONT
2 CONT Balbilla by inscribing her name on the Colossi of Memnon, she is acknowledging and making reference to her royal and aristocratic descent. In the last two lines of the second epigram, she honors her family including Balbilus:
2 CONT
2 CONT ''For my parents were noble, and my grandfathers,
2 CONT The wise Balbillus and Antiochus the king.''
2 CONT
2 CONT The fourth and final epigram, Balbilla dedicates to her parents and grandfathers. This epigram is dedicated also to her noble and aristocratic blood. In the epigram, Balbilla mentions that Balbilus has royal lineage.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''For pious were my parents and grandfathers: Balbillus the Wise and King Antiochus; Balbillus, the father of my mother of royal blood and king Antiochus, the father of my father. From their line I too draw my noble blood, and the
2 CONC se verses are mine, pious Balbilla.''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Balbilus in Fiction'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * The Hasta Pura of Balbilus is mentioned in the second part of the novel series, written by Robert Graves, I, Claudius and Claudius the God. Graves calls the decoration an "Arrow without a head" and refers to its award to Balbilu
2 CONC s.
2 CONT * Balbilus's somewhat dubious role as court astrologer is referred to throughout the fourth to sixth parts of the novel series Romanike by Codex Regius (2006-2014)
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source : '''
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tiberius_Claudius_Balbilus
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Tiberius Claudius Balbillus or Balbilus, also known as ‘Balbillus the Wise‘ (Greek: Τιβερίος Κλαύδιος Βαλβίλλος, 3 – 79)''' was an Egyptian Greek astrologer and a learned scholar. Balbillus was the son of astrologer Tiberius Cl
2 CONC audius Thrasyllus, also known as Thrasyllus of Mendes and Princess Aka II of Commagene, who was either a granddaughter or great granddaughter of King Antiochus I Theos of Commagene. His sister was Eunia, who married the Praetorian
2 CONC  Prefect Naevius Sutorius Macro.
2 CONT
2 CONT Balbillus was of [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Equestrian_order   Equestrian rank.] He was born and raised in Alexandria, Egypt. During the reign of Roman Emperor Caligula he fled from Rome to Alexandria, but returned to Rome unde
2 CONC r Roman Emperor Claudius, with whom he had been friendly when they were children and had met at his father's house. He accompanied Claudius on his expedition to Britain as an officer in the 20th legion (Legio XX Valeria Victrix) a
2 CONC nd when they returned was awarded a crown of honor. Other sources mention his Hasta Pura.[1] Balbillus was one of the highest magistrates of Equestrian rank that served in Rome.
2 CONT
2 CONT Appointed high priest at the Temple of Hermes in Alexandria and director of the Library, he split his time between there and Rome. He foretold an eclipse which fell on one of the emperor’s birthdays, and remained as advisor to Cla
2 CONC udius even after the emperor had passed an edict expelling all astrologers from Rome. He avoided the fatal end of many astrologers under Roman Emperor Nero, who in 56 appointed him Prefect of Egypt, where he stayed until 59. Balbi
2 CONC llus returned to Rome under Roman Emperor Vespasian. When he died, his friend Vespasian thought very highly of him and dedicated a sporting festival called the Balbillean Games, named and held in honor of his memory which was held
2 CONC  at Ephesus from 79 until into the 3rd century.
2 CONT
2 CONT Balbillus married an unnamed Greek woman and they had an only child, a daughter called Claudia Capitolina. Capitolina married a Greek Prince from the Kingdom of Commagene called Gaius Julius Archelaus Antiochus Epiphanes. Through 
2 CONC his daughter, Balbillus would become the maternal grandfather of Capitolina’s children with Epiphanes who were: Prince Gaius Julius Antiochus Epiphanes Philopappos and Princess Julia Balbilla.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===References===
2 CONT
