1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Many family trees have Aka of Commagene as the wife of Tiberius Claudius Balbillus
2 CONT
2 CONT The reason for her identity is from an engraving left by Julia Balbilla a Roman poet on the legs of the Colossi of Memnon.
2 CONT
2 CONT The relevant passage:
2 CONT "For pious were my parents and grandfathers:
2 CONT Balbillus the Wise and King Antiochus;
2 CONT Balbillus, the father of my mother of royal blood and King Antiochus, the father of my father. From their line I too draw my noble blood,
2 CONT and these verses are mine, pious Balbilla."
2 CONT
2 CONT Notice that there is no mention of Aka anywhere here. What happened was a transcription error. Various scholars have read different things at the end of verse 15. Some scholars read it as AKA or a variation of it and decided that 
2 CONC it must be a reference to her grandmother's name. Andre and Etienne Bernand argue in Les inscriptions grecques et latines du colosse de Memnon (1960) that if you look at the photograph of the inscription they are using it clearly 
2 CONC says αμμας, which simply means mother in Latin, which makes a lot more sense in the context of the verse.
2 CONT
2 CONT {{https://media.geni.com/p14/df/6f/d7/cb/53444862fa4905f1/screenshot_2023-04-07_232453_original.png?hash=0cfd7223766e9a5112768bd86a3f419752a6b71972f0015fddbc581897798c5a.1681023599}}
