1 NOTE Flavius Gratian received the title of Augustus from his father on August 4 367, but upon the death of his father, Valentinianus I the troops in pannonia proclaimed his half brother, son of Justina, Valentiianus II, Augustus. Flavi
2 CONC us Gratian settled for the choice without a fight over the throne.
2 CONT During his rein Orthodox christianity became dominant.
2 CONT At some point in his career he took into service a body of Alans and began appearing in public in the costume of a Scythian warrior. After the desaster of the battle of Adrianopole he aroused the contempt of his Roman troops. A Ro
2 CONC man general, Magnus Maximus took advantage of this falling out of his troops and revolted in Britain and invaded Gaul. Gratian fled from Paris after being deserted by his troops, to Lyon. Gratian was captured and given to Andragat
2 CONC hius and assassinated on August 25 383.
