1 NOTE {geni:about_me} He was a Pannonia rope-maker, who rose to become a general. He was Comes Britanniarum, and Comes Africae. His parents are unknown. His fake ancestry comes from the ''Historia Augusta'', which is largely fiction.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Graciano_el_Viejo
2 CONT
2 CONT Graciano el Viejo era el padre de los emperadores romanos, Valentiniano I y Valente y el abuelo de Graciano.
2 CONT
2 CONT Graciano comenzó su carrera como un oficial de personal, pero ascendió más tarde a tribuno, y posteriormente a Comes Africae ( gobernador de África). Le enviaron a Britania en torno 340 por lo que recibió el cargo de Comes Britann
2 CONC iarum, al mando del ejército (comitatus). La crisis que precipitó esto no se sabe con certeza. Después de su retiro, el emperador Constancio II confiscó todas las propiedades de Graciano, debido a sospechas de ayudar a Magnencio.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Gratianus Funarius, also known as Gratianus Major (Major Latin for the elder), also known as Gratian the Elder, was a soldier of the Roman Empire who flourished in the 4th century. He is called Gratian the Elder, to distinguish hi
2 CONC mself from his grandson the Roman Emperor Gratian.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Origins
2 CONT
2 CONT Gratianus originated from an obscure Christian family from the town of Cibalae or Cibalis, Southern Pannonia (modern Vinkovci, Croatia). There Gratianus seems to have married a local woman, and they had two sons Flavius Valentinia
2 CONC nus (321-375) and Flavius Iulius (Julius) Valens (328-378). His first son would become Roman Emperor of the West and his second son would become Roman Emperor of the East.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gratianus obtained the cognomen Funarius, which means ‘the rope-man’, because prior to his military career, Gratianus was a rope salesman. On one occasion, he successfully resisted the efforts of five soldiers to wrestle him. That
2 CONC  occasion inspired Gratianus to enlist in the Roman Army.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Military & Political Career
2 CONT
2 CONT Gratianus was renowned for his strength and his wrestling skills, and had an illustrious army career. He rose from Staff Officer to be promoted to Military Tribune and became a Comes Africae. In the 340s, Gratianus was sent to Bri
2 CONC tannia and was given the title Comes Britanniarum. He commanded a field army (comitatenses). The crisis that precipitated this is not known, however.
2 CONT
2 CONT After his army career ended, Gratianus returned to his birthplace and lived as a private citizen who had earned a good reputation from his military career. Gratianus could have earned the praetorship.
2 CONT
2 CONT In Gratianus’ retirement, Roman Emperor Constantius II (reigned 337-360) confiscated all of his estates because of his suspected support of Roman usurper Magnentius. Gratianus was kept in high regard with fellow soldiers due to hi
2 CONC s popularity within the army; this could have contributed to the elevation of his sons and his descendants in becoming Roman Emperors. When Valens became Emperor, the Roman Senate in Constantinople decreed a brass statue of him.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT     * http://www.roman-emperors.org/vali.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT     * http://www.ancientlibrary.com/smith-bio/1409.html
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Rodgers,N.,The History and Conquests of Ancient Rome,Hermes House,2005.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Föddes 290 i Pannonien , Imperio Romano, och dog 367 i Imperio Romano. 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
