2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Marcus Aemilius Lepidus (c. 30 BC-33) was a Roman senator, politician and general, praised by the historian Tacitus.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents [hide]
2 CONT
2 CONT 1 Origin and early career
2 CONT
2 CONT 2 Activities during the reign of Tiberius
2 CONT
2 CONT 3 Descendants
2 CONT
2 CONT 4 References
2 CONT
2 CONT 5 External links
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Origin and early career
2 CONT
2 CONT Lepidus was the son of Cornelia Scipio and Lucius Aemilius Paullus (who served as a censor) and brother-in-law to Augustus' granddaughter Julia the Younger. Thus, he was a descendant of one of the oldest patrician families, the Ae
2 CONC milii.
2 CONT
2 CONT He became consul in 6. He then distinguished himself as legate in charge of an army during the Illyrian War (6-9) under the command of Tiberius, the later emperor. After the end of the war he served as governor of Dalmatia (modern
2 CONC  day Croatia and Bosnia) or Pannonia (modern day Hungary).
2 CONT
2 CONT At the time of Augustus' death, in 14, he was governor of Northern Spain in charge of an army of three legions.[citation needed] Interestingly, while there were serious riots in the armies in Germany and Pannonia after Augustus' d
2 CONC eath, Lepidus army gave no trouble.
2 CONT
2 CONT Descendants
2 CONT
2 CONT His daughter Aemilia Lepida married Drusus Caesar, a son of Germanicus and Agrippina the Elder. She committed suicide after being accused of adultery.
2 CONT
2 CONT His paternal nephew and adopted son Marcus Aemilius Lepidus became Caligula’s brother-in-law and there were rumors that Caligula had indicated him as a possible successor. However, he was involved in a conspiracy in 39** and was m
2 CONC urdered.
2 CONT
2 CONT Forrás / Source:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marcus_Aemilius_Lepidus_(consul_6)
2 CONT
2 CONT **Note (from FARKAS Mihály László):
2 CONT
2 CONT After he died?
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT '''
2 CONT =Marcus Aemilius Lepidus=
2 CONT '''
2 CONT
2 CONT Marcus Aemilius Lepidus (c. 30 BC-33 AD) was a Roman senator, politician and general, praised by the historian Tacitus.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Origin and early career'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Lepidus was the son of Cornelia and Lucius Aemilius Lepidus Paullus (who served as a censor) and brother-in-law to Augustus' granddaughter Julia the Younger. Thus, he was a descendant of one of the oldest patrician families, the A
2 CONC emilii.
2 CONT
2 CONT He became consul in 6 AD. He then distinguished himself as legate in charge of an army during the Illyrian War (6-9 AD) under the command of Tiberius, the later emperor. After the end of the war he served as governor of Dalmatia (
2 CONC modern day Croatia and Bosnia) or Pannonia (modern day Hungary).
2 CONT
2 CONT At the time of Augustus' death, in 14 AD, he was governor of Northern Spain in charge of an army of three legions. Interestingly, while there were serious riots in the armies in Germany and Pannonia after Augustus' death, Lepidus 
2 CONC army gave no trouble.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Activities during the reign of Tiberius'''
2 CONT
2 CONT He defended Gnaeus Calpurnius Piso (a friend of Tiberius, but also a relative of Lepidus) at his trial for the alleged poisoning of Germanicus.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 21 AD, Tiberius offered him the governorship of Africa Province. He rejected the offer, however, because of ill-health and his children, but more likely to leave the position to Quintus Junius Blaesus, uncle of Lucius Aelius Se
2 CONC janus the powerful pretorian prefect. Although these episodes may seem a flattery towards the emperor Tiberius, Lepidus' activities in the senate show an independent mind. In 21 AD he made a strong speech against the death penalty
2 CONC  for an irreverent poet. Nevertheless, the poet was executed by order of the senate: this allowed Tiberius to praise Lepidus' moderation (as well as the senate's zeal in persecuting any offence against the emperor).
2 CONT
2 CONT Modern scholars have suggested that Marcus Lepidus also restored the Basilica Aemilia in the Roman Forum in 22 AD and served as governor of Asia in 26. Apparently, Lepidus was one of the few aristocrats obtaining high positions (i
2 CONC ncluding command of large armies) in this troubled time without ever being accused of plotting against the emperor. Although in 32 an important senator (Cotta Messalinus) openly attacked him because of his excessive influence in t
2 CONC he senate, this accusation had no consequences. Lepidus died in 33 AD. Tacitus described him as "wise and noble" for his actions as a senator. According to Tacitus his actions could be taken as an example for independent aristocra
2 CONC ts living under tyranny.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Descendants'''
2 CONT
2 CONT His daughter Aemilia Lepida married Drusus Caesar, a son of Germanicus and Agrippina the Elder. She committed suicide after being accused of adultery.
2 CONT
2 CONT Although it is uncertain, some historians believe that he was also father to that Marcus Aemilius Lepidus who became Caligula’s lover and brother-in-law, husband to Julia Drusilla, and about whom there were rumors that Caligula ha
2 CONC d indicated him as his successor. However, the younger Lepidus was probably involved in a conspiracy in 39 AD, along with the emperor's sisters Agrippina the Younger and Julia Livilla. Whether the conspiracy was real or not, Calig
2 CONC ula so accused him and Lepidus was executed in the same year.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source : '''
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marcus_Aemilius_Lepidus_(consul_6)
