1 NOTE Claudius (Tiberius Drusus Nero Claudius Caesar) was the great uncle andstepfather of Nero. When Caligula was murdered, in 41 A.D., thereremained this Claudius, his uncle, who was now 51 years of age but who,as the butt of the fami
2 CONC ly, had been excluded from the functions of thegovernment, neglected, ill-treated, and allowed to divide his timebetween low company and literary studies. He was known as "Claudius, theIdiot or the Stutterer." No one had considere
2 CONC d him a serious candidatesave the shrewd Herod Agrippa II., who, having successfully schemed forthe elevation of Caligula and reaped a rich reward, was silentlymeditating a second coup. Perhaps instead of being weak-minded, Claudi
2 CONC usmerely feigned madness in order to escape poisoning. On his father's sidehe was descended from Appius Claudius, a Roman decemivir in 450 B.C.,whose name survives in the Appian Way. Born in Lyons (Lugdunum), 10 B.C.,Claudius in 4
2 CONC 3 A.D. determined to carry out the conquest of Britain whichAugustus had meditated, but decided to postpone, if not to forego. Senecarecords with a sneer that Claudius "had determined to see every German,Gaul , and Briton in a tog
2 CONC a." He sent Aulus Platius against Caractacus,in 43 A.D., and himself soon joined his victorious army in time to seethe crossing of the Thames and the fall of Colchester, Cymbeline'scapital, and to receive the "submission of the el
2 CONC even British kings."These successes, gained only with the hardest fighting, led him to maketreaties with the British chiefs (See Wurts, pp. 155-156). After butsixteen days in the island he returned to celebrate his triumph, leavin
2 CONC ghis generals to carry on. This was the most notable achievement of thereign of Claudius, who was also the builder of the conduit Aqua Claudiusand other public works. He married four times: (1) Plautia Urgulanilla,who died on her 
2 CONC wedding day, (2) Aelia Paetina, whom he divorced, and (3)Valeria Messalina, aged sixteen, an exceedingly wicked woman, mother oflittle Octavia, had the title of Augusta conferred upon her, whom he alsodivorced. Then at age forty-e
2 CONC ight, he married (4) Agrippina the Younger,his niece, who was already twice a widow, daughter of Agrippina the Elderand Germanicus. Claudius conferred the title of Augusta upon her. By herfirst husband, Cnaeus Domitius Ahenobarbus
2 CONC , she had a son Nero. She wasalso married the second time to Caius Crispus. Her problem was to becomethe wife of Claudius, to get rid of his son, Britannicus, and make Nero,by adoption, heir to the Empire. The fact that she was Cl
2 CONC audius' niecedid not deter her, but gave her opportunities for fond intimacies thatstirred the old ruler in no avuncular manner. She, at the age ofthirty-two, while Claudius was fifty-seven, became the 4th wife of heruncle. The Se
2 CONC nate approved, the Praetorians laughed, and Agrippinareached the throne. Claudius, to whom she gave poison and caused hisdeath on October 13, 54 A.D. On that day her son, Nero, was proclaimedEmperor. See details of the life of Cla
2 CONC udius in Durant, pp. 268-274.Also, the PBS TV Series "I, Claudius," available in the Carnegie PublicLibrary in Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania. It is based on the popular novel bythe same name.
2 CONT
2 CONT [Editor's note: According to Wurts, in his "Magna Charta," Claudius had adaughter, Venissa (Venus Julia), but there is no record as to which ofClaudius' wives was her mother. This connection has not been confirmedelsewhere and is 
2 CONC highly questionable.]
2 CONT
