1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Galba
2 CONT
2 CONT * 6th Emperor of the Roman Empire
2 CONT * Reign	9 June 68 – 15 January 69
2 CONT * (7 months)
2 CONT * Predecessor	Nero
2 CONT * Successor	Otho
2 CONT * Born	24 December 3 BC
2 CONT * Near Terracina, Italy
2 CONT * Died	15 January 69 (aged 70)
2 CONT * Rome
2 CONT * Spouse	Aemilia Lepida
2 CONT * Full name
2 CONT * Servius Sulpicius Galba
2 CONT * (at birth);
2 CONT * Lucius Livius Ocella Sulpicius Galba
2 CONT * (until ascension);
2 CONT * Servius Sulpicius Galba Caesar Augustus
2 CONT * (as emperor);
2 CONT * Imperator Servius Galba Caesar Augustus
2 CONT * (imperial name)[1]
2 CONT * Dynasty	None
2 CONT * Mother	Mummia Achaica
2 CONT
2 CONT Galba (Latin: Servius Sulpicius Galba Caesar Augustus;[2] 24 December 3 BC – 15 January 69), pron.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was born as Servius Sulpicius Galba near Terracina, "on the left as you go towards Fundi" in the words of Suetonius.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT His mother was Mummia Achaica, the granddaughter of Lutatius Catulus (cos. 78) and great-granddaughter of Lucius Mummius Achaicus. They only had one other child, an elder son called Gaius who left Rome after squandering the greate
2 CONC r part of his estate, and committed suicide because Tiberius dishonored him by preventing him from taking part in the allotment of the provinces in his year. His father married a second wife, Livia Ocellina, a distant kinswoman of
2 CONC  the empress Livia. She later adopted Galba, so he took her names, remaining Lucius Livius Ocella Sulpicius Galba until becoming emperor.
2 CONT
2 CONT His wife, Aemilia Lepida, however, was connected by the marriages of some of her relatives to some of the Julii-Claudii. They had two sons, probably Gaius and Servius (most likely Livius Ocella Galba), who died during their father
2 CONC 's life. The elder son was born circa 25 AD. Hardly anything is known about his life as he died young. He was engaged to his step-sister Antonia Postuma, but they never wed, which leads modern historians to believe that he died du
2 CONC ring this time. Their engagement is dated to 48, and that is generally believed to be his time of death.
2 CONT
2 CONT The date of birth of the younger son occurred later than 25 but before 30. This Galba outlived his older brother. 
