1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * from Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Quintus Volusius'''[1] also known as''' Q. Volusius Saturninus''' and '''Volusius Saturninus'''[2] was a politician of the Roman Republic who lived in the 1st century BC.
2 CONT
2 CONT Little is known on his origins however, Volusius was originally from the city of Feronia, also known as Lucus Feroniae in Etruria.[3] He came from an ancient and distinguished Senatorial family, that never rose above the Praetorsh
2 CONC ip. Volusius was of eques status.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Volusius was a pupil of the Roman statesman Cicero in oratory.[5] He accompanied Cicero to Cilicia, where he held office under him.[6] While in Cilicia, Volusius served as a Prefect under Cicero in 51 BC to 50 BC.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT During his Prefectship in Cilicia, Cicero had sent to Volusius to Cyprus.[8] When Volusius arrived on the island, he had heard and settled civil cases to a small group of Roman citizens that lived on the island. Cicero describes V
2 CONC olusius as a ‘man both trustworthy and extraordinarily moderate’.[9]
2 CONT
2 CONT Volusius married a Roman noblewoman called Claudia, the daughter of Pompey’s officer, Drusus Claudius Nero, sister of Praetor Tiberius Claudius Nero who was the aunt of the Roman emperor Tiberius.[10] Claudia bore Volusius one son
2 CONC , Lucius Volusius Saturninus[11] and a daughter, Volusia Saturnina.[12]
2 CONT
2 CONT =In fiction=
2 CONT
2 CONT Volusius is a character in the novel titled Respublica: A Novel of Cicero's Roman Republic written by Richard Braccia which was released by AuthorHouse in 2009.
2 CONT
2 CONT =References & Sources=
2 CONT
2 CONT * for a full list of sources, please see: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quintus_Volusius_(prefect) 
