2 CONT
2 CONT -----------------
2 CONT
2 CONT In Arthurian legend he had a fictitious wife [http://www.geni.com/people/Ivoire-Fictional/6000000006290723846 Ivoire].
2 CONT
2 CONT Emperor Constantine
2 CONT
2 CONT (c.AD 375 - 411)
2 CONT
2 CONT (Welsh: Custennin; Latin: Constantinus; English: Constantine)
2 CONT
2 CONT After the gradual withdrawal of Roman troops from Britain in the last decades of the 4th century, several generals rose to power as Emperors over the mainland British. Legend, particularly perpetrated by Geoffrey of Monmouth, stat
2 CONC es that the British people looked to their cousins in Brittany for such leadership. [http://www.geni.com/people/Aldrien-Brittany/6000000004089183711 King Aldrien of Brittany] declined the British High-Throne, but offered up his yo
2 CONC ung brother, Constantine in his stead. Constantine landed in Britain with 2000 men and overcame the barbarians already invading the Island's shores to become High-King as Constantine Waredwr (the Deliverer).
2 CONT
2 CONT Constantine has been variously identified with one of several near contemporary men of this name appearing in ancient Welsh Royal pedigrees: the Galfridian brother of King Aldrien of Brittany, a son of the Emperor [http://www.geni
2 CONC .com/people/Constans-I-Flavius/6000000003645859911 Magnus Maximus] or a King of Dumnonia in South-Western Britain. The latter is perhaps the most popular, yet also the most unlikely and based on his grandson [http://www.geni.com/p
2 CONC eople/Arthwyr-ap-Uthyr/6000000007329382612 Arthur]'s associations with Somerset and Cornwall. His alternative name of Constantine Fendigaid (the Blessed) may itself have appeared through confusion with one of these men.
2 CONT
2 CONT It seems likely that this man has arisen to take his place in British Mythology from memories of an historical British Emperor who was raised to the Imperial throne by his own troops in 407. Disenchanted with the rule of the legit
2 CONC imate Emperor Honorius, the Roman army remaining in Britain had already got through two self appointed Emperors in a single year. Worried about renewed barbarian invasions on the continent, they decided to promote the usurpation o
2 CONC f a third in the shape of the Emperor Constantine III. From his base in Britain, Constantine was able to take perhaps the last of the Roman troops in that country to Gaul. Here he strengthened defences along the Rhine and was able
2 CONC  to head off the barabrians who, two years later, turned south for Spain. Constantine sent his lieutenant, named Gerontius, to conteract their move; but Gerontius rebelled against his Emperor and encouraged the barbarians in their
2 CONC  efforts. Despite this renewed attack, Constantine gained Imperial recognition from the Emperor Honorius himself. However, his position was soon discredited and Zosimus tells us how the British were forced to throw off the shackle
2 CONC s of Imperial rule and look to their own defence against invading Saxons. Constantine tried to assert his authority once more by invading Italy in 411. He was captured by Honorius in Arles, taken back to Ravenna and executed..
2 CONT
2 CONT It is possible that while all this was going on on the continent, Constantine was deposed in Britain by his treacherous advisor, Vortigern, as related by Geoffrey. However, it would appear that he has pushed Constantine forward in
2 CONC  history some fifteen years or more. If the Emperor's descendancy is to be believed, his grandson, King Arthur, was flourishing almost a hundred years after his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT Geoffrey Ashe (1990) Mythology of the British Isles.
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter C. Bartrum (1993) A Welsh Classical Dictionary.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ronan Coghlan (1991) The Encyclopaedia of Arthurian Legends.
2 CONT
2 CONT David Day (1995) The Quest for King Arthur.
2 CONT
2 CONT Phyllis Ann Karr (1997) The Arthurian Companion.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Malory (1485) Le Morte D'Arthur.
2 CONT
2 CONT John Matthews (1994) The Arthurian Tradtion.
2 CONT
2 CONT Geoffrey of Monmouth (1136) The History of the Kings of Britain.
2 CONT
