1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Caecina Paetus From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia Caecina Paetus was condemned to death on a charge of disloyalty by the emperor Claudius in 42 AD. He chose to commit suicide rather than face the emperor's wrath
2 CONC . His wife Arria stabbed herself first in order to give him the courage to do this and handed him the dagger saying "Non dolet, Paete!" ("It doesn't hurt, Paetus"). Contents  [hide]  1 References 1.1 Primary 1.2 Secondary 2 Extern
2 CONC al links References[edit]
2 CONT
2 CONT Primary[edit] Pliny, Letters III.xvi.6 Secondary[edit] Georges Duby, Michelle Perrot, Pauline Schmitt. A History of Women in the West, Volume I. Belknap Press.2000 External links[edit]
