1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Arria From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Arria et Paetus, sculpture by Pierre Lepautre and Jean-Baptiste Théodon, Musée du Louvre Arria (also Arria Major) was a woman in ancient Rome. Her husband Caecina Paetus was ordered by the emperor Claudius to commit suicide for hi
2 CONC s part in a rebellion but was not capable of forcing himself to do so. Arria wrenched the dagger from him and stabbed herself, then returned it to her husband, telling him that it didn't hurt ("Non dolet, Paete!"). Her story was r
2 CONC ecorded in the letters of Pliny the Younger, who obtained his information from Arria's granddaughter, Fannia. Pliny records that Arria's son died at the same time as Caecina Paetus was quite ill. She apparently arranged and planne
2 CONC d the child's funeral without her husband even knowing of his death. Every time she visited her husband Arria told him that the boy was improving. If emotion threatened to get the better of her she excused herself from the room an
2 CONC d would, in Pliny's words, "give herself to sorrow," then return to her husband with a calm demeanor. After the rebellion against Claudius led by Lucius Arruntius Camillus Scribonianus in 42. AD, Scribonianus was killed and Caecin
2 CONC a was taken to Rome as a prisoner for conspiring with him. Arria begged the captain of the ship to allow her to join him on board. She claimed that if a consular Roman man was allowed slaves to take care of him, then she should sa
2 CONC ve them the trouble and look after him herself. The captain refused, so Arria followed the great ship in a small fishing boat all the way to Rome. Arria openly attacked the wife of the rebellion leader Scribonianus for giving evid
2 CONC ence to the prosecution, crying: "Am I to listen to you who could go on living after Scribonianus died in your arms?" [1] It was this sentence which alerted everyone to her intention of dying alongside Paetus. Her son-in-law, Thra
2 CONC sea, attempted to persuade her to live, asking her if she would want her own daughter to kill herself if he were sentenced to death. Arria insisted that she would if her daughter (also called Arria) had lived as long and happily w
2 CONC ith Thrasea as she herself had with Caecina. She was watched very closely from that point onwards but, realising this, Arria said that they could not stop her from dying. Having pointed this out she ran, head first, in o a wall an
2 CONC d knocked herself out cold. When she came to, she cried: "I told you I would do it the hard way if you stopped me from doing it the easy way."[2] Arria was eventually permitted to join her husband in a "noble death" (falling on on
2 CONC e's own sword/dagger). 	Wikimedia Commons has media related to Arria. References[edit]
2 CONT
2 CONT Jump up ^ Pliny the Younger, Letters 3.16. AD 97/107. Jump up ^ Pliny the Younger, Letters 3.16. AD 97/107. L Categories: 42 deathsAncient Roman women1st-century RomansFemale suicides
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arria
