2 CONT
2 CONT From the English Wikipedia page on Robert of Hesbaye (with an awful lot of conjecture thrown in):
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_of_Hesbaye
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert II, Rodbert or Chrodobert (770-807) was a Frank, count of Worms and Rheingau and duke of Hesbaye around the year 800. 
2 CONT
2 CONT His family is known as Robertians. His son is Robert of Worms and his grandson is Robert the Strong, the namegiver of the Robertians. Robert of Hesbaye is the oldest known ancestor in the line of Robertians that is thought to be c
2 CONC ertain. He is the great-grandfather of two Frankish kings, Odo and Robert, that ruled in the kingdom of Western Francia. 
2 CONT
2 CONT One of his descendants is Hugh Capet, who was the "last Frankish king" and the "first king of France". The descendants of Hugh Capet ruled France until the French Revolution, with a junior line having ruled Spain since 1700.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ingerman and Cancor are possibly his brothers. Landrada, mother of Saint Chrodogang, archbishop of Metz, is likely his sister. His father may have been named Rodbert or, Chrodobert. He was likely the son of Thuringbert of Worms an
2 CONC d Rheingau and grandson of Robert I of Worms and Rheingau, who died in 764.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ermengarde, wife of emperor Louis the Pious was most likely his niece.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT Riché, Pierre. The Carolingians, a family who forged Europe.
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From the English Wikipedia page on Wormsgau: 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wormsgau
2 CONT
2 CONT The Wormsgau or pago wormatiensi was in the Middle Ages a county, which extended itself not only in the surroundings of the city of Worms, Germany, but concurrently along the Rhine widely in northern direction until short of reach
2 CONC ing Coblenz. The city of Mainz belonged likewise to it as to the start of the 9th century also Boppard, which in reality ca. 825 was already lost again. In the 10th century the Wormsgau lost wide middlerhenish zones...
2 CONT
2 CONT 2nd Robertian Count in Wormsgau: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert II (Rutbert II o. Hruodbertus) (770 attested, † 12 July 807), Robert/Rupter I's grandson, 795/807 Count in Worms- and Oberrheingau, 795 Lord to Dienheim, ∞ I Theoderata (Tiedrada) (766/777 attested, † before 789), ∞ II Isen
2 CONC garde, 789
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert of Hesbaye
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia 
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert, Rodbert or Chrodobert was a Frank and duke of Hesbaye. His family is known as Robertians. His son is Robert of Worms and his grandson is Robert the Strong, the namegiver of the Robertians. Robert of Hesbaye is the oldest k
2 CONC nown ancestor in the line of Robertians that is thought to be certain. He is the great-grandfather of two Frankish kings, Odo and Robert, that ruled in the kingdom of Western Francia. One of his descendants is Hugh Capet, who was 
2 CONC the "last Frankish king" and the "first king of France". The descendants of Hugh Capet ruled France until the French Revolution.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ingerman and Cancor are possibly his brothers. Landrada, mother of Saint Chrodogang, archbishop of Metz, is likely his sister. His father may have been named Rodbert or, Chrodobert.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ermengarde, wife of emperor Louis the Pious was most likely his niece.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT Riché, Pierre. The Carolingians, a family who forged Europe.
2 CONT
