2 CONT
2 CONT Eunoë, who was descended from Moors, was the wife of Bogudes, King of Mauretania, and a mistress of Julius Caesar, according to Suetonius. She may have replaced Cleopatra in Caesar's affections, when he arrived in North Africa pri
2 CONC or to the Battle of Thapsus on April 6, 46 BC. Only a brief romance for the Roman, both Eunoe and Bogudes profited through gifts bestowed on them by Caesar.
2 CONT
2 CONT Eunoe and Cleopatra were among several queens courted by Caesar. Caesar departed from Africa in June 46 BC, five and a half months after he landed.
2 CONT
