2 CONT =Decimus Valerius Asiaticus=
2 CONT '''
2 CONT
2 CONT Decimus Valerius Asiaticus (35-after 69 AD) was a Roman Senator who served as a Legatus of Gallia Belgica.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Family Background & Early Life'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Asiaticus was of Praetorian rank. He was the son born to the Roman Senator, consul Decimus Valerius Asiaticus and the Roman noblewoman Lollia Saturnina. There is a possibility he may have had other siblings. He and along with his 
2 CONC family were originated from Vienna, Gallia Narbonensis.
2 CONT
2 CONT The father of Asiaticus was of Allobrogian origin and his political career was a contemporary of the rule of the Roman emperors Tiberius, Caligula and Claudius. His father was a respected, wealthy, and prominent Roman Senator. The
2 CONC  elder Asiaticus in 35 served as a suffect consul and again in 46, served as an ordinary consul. He was the first citizen from Vienna to become consul, the first man from Gaul to attain the consulship and became the first man from
2 CONC  Gaul to be admitted into the Roman Senate. The elder Asiaticus had a brother who served as a Roman Senator until 47.
2 CONT
2 CONT The mother of Asiaticus was the social, beautiful woman Lollia Saturnina, whose younger sister Lollia Paulina was a Roman empress and the third wife of the Roman emperor Caligula. Saturnina was the first daughter of the suffect co
2 CONC nsul Marcus Lollius and the noble woman Volusia Saturnina, as she was a first cousin to Roman emperor Tiberius and his brother, the General Nero Claudius Drusus.
2 CONT
2 CONT Although Asiaticus was the namesake of his father and through Roman citizenship he was a member of the gens Valeria, through his mother he also had relations to that gens. His maternal grandfather Marcus Lollius, was the son born 
2 CONC to the Roman Politician and Military Officer Marcus Lollius from his wife Valeria. Valeria was one of the daughters of the literary patron, consul Marcus Valerius Messalla Corvinus and a sister to the Roman Senators Marcus Valeriu
2 CONC s Messalla Messallinus and Marcus Aurelius Cotta Maximus Messalinus.[19] His father was murdered on the orders of the Roman empress Valeria Messalina in 47 and was later raised by his mother. Little is known on his early life.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Political career'''
2 CONT
2 CONT After serving as a Legatus of Gallia Belgica, he later became the Roman governor of that province[22] in the reign of the Roman emperor Nero. In early 69 when Aulus Vitellius became Roman emperor, he was finding governors and comm
2 CONC anders in the provinces as supporters for his emperorship who were genuinely enthusiastic. Among those supporters was Asiaticus. When Vitellius was in the city of Lugdunum Gaul, proclaiming his son and daughter as heirs from his w
2 CONC ife Galeria Fundana, Vitellius betrothed his daughter Vitellia to Asiaticus.[28] Asiaticus and Vitellia had married in the reign of Vitellius.
2 CONT
2 CONT He had become powerful through wealth and the skilful exploitation of imperial patronage. The alliance with Asiaticus and Vitellius was perhaps an attempt to reconcile the communities in Gaul with the Roman state. Asiaticus was un
2 CONC able to give Vitellius much support when the Roman army commanders appointed, Vespasian as an alternative successor to Vitellius.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Vespasian became Roman emperor in second half of 69, Asiaticus was appointment for a consulship in 70. Before he could serve his consulship in early 70, Asiaticus died.
2 CONT
2 CONT Asiaticus was survived by Vitellia and their son Marcus Lollius Paulinus Decimus Valerius Asiaticus Saturninus. Later in 70, Vespasian arranged Vitellia to remarry another unnamed groom. Her second marriage was a splendid match fo
2 CONC r her and Vespasian provided for her, the dowry and clothing.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source : '''
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Decimus_Valerius_Asiaticus_(Legatus)
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT * from Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Decimus Valerius Asiaticus''' (35-after 69 AD) was a Roman Senator who served as a Legatus of Gallia Belgica.[1]
