1 NOTE Livia Drusilla (58 B.C. - A.D. 29), who took the name Julia Augustafollowing the death of her husband Augustus in A.D. 14, served as empresswith her husband and unwanted advisor to her son Tiberius.
2 CONT -------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: http://www.mmdtkw.org/VLivia.html
2 CONT
2 CONT Livia Drusilla, the First First Lady: There has to be a first ofeverything, and this lady was it. She certainly was not the firstpowerful woman in the ancient world: the Bible and other ancient tomeswere full of predecessors, and 
2 CONC Cleopatra was barely out of the picturewhen Livia bloomed in Rome. Livia was not even the first wife of Octavian(later called Caesar Augustus): that would have been Claudia, the childstepdaughter of Marc Antony, whom Octavian left
2 CONC  untouched and whom hedivorced when he and Antony fell out again. Not even his second:Scribonia, an "older woman" and already (by a previous husband) themother of the later notorious Julia the Elder and grandmother of the evenmore
2 CONC  odious Julia the Younger (Julietta), filled that role. LiviaDrusilla, who was willed the title of Julia Augusta when her CaesarAugustus died many years later, was, in fact, Augustus' third wife.
2 CONT
2 CONT Livia was born in 58 BC, the daughter of a Roman Patrician, Marcus LiviusDrusus Claudius, and the granddaughter of Marcus Livius Drusus, a greatman who had been tribune in 91 BC. At the age of 15 or 16, she wasmarried to Tiberius 
2 CONC Claudius Nero, who was an opponent of the youngOctavian. Livia gave birth to there first son, Tiberius in 42 BC and by40 BC, the young family had fled Rome to the protection of Mark Antony.They soon returned to Rome and, by 38 BC,
2 CONC  a very pregnant Livia divorcedTiberius Claudius Nero to marry Octavian who had himself just divorcedScribonia, newly the mother of Julia the Elder.
2 CONT
2 CONT The events leading up to this marriage aren't clear, but it appears tohave been done with the approval of her husband, but he could hardly havesafely objected. It is certain that both Livia and Octavian wanted thisunion, because t
2 CONC hey were willing to endure a great deal of scandal andRoman gossip. But to make the marriage acceptable to Roman society, astory of divine omens was put about. The tale was that, while travelingto her country estate, an eagle, the
2 CONC  sacred bird of Jupiter, had droppedin her lap a laying hen holding a sprig of laurel in its mouth -- threepotent omens occurring simultaneously obviously marked Livia for aspecial job. Three months after her marriage to Octavian,
2 CONC  Livia's secondson, Drusus, was born. In accordance with Roman custom, Livia's sons wentto live with their father. Julia, the daughter of Scribonia, stayed withOctavian and Livia. When Tiberius Claudius Nero died in 32 BCE, Livia'
2 CONC ssons joined Octavian's household. This may seem complicated, but suchfamily arrangements were not uncommon among the ancient Roman elites.
2 CONT
2 CONT The role of wife to rich and a famous urban Roman man was clearly definedby Livia's time: she should stay at home, produce as many children as shecould, maybe do a little spinning and weaving, supervise the servants,and know (for 
2 CONC ceremonial purposes only) how to thresh grain and start afire. The "stay at home" part was the most important. A good Roman wifenever upstaged her husband -- in fact, she should not be on the stage atall. Many upper class women un
2 CONC derstandably found this incredibly boringand sought solace in the arms of men other than their husbands. Livia didnot appear to have had a wandering heart and found other outlets whichclosely match our modern ideas of the role of 
2 CONC a "First lady."
2 CONT
2 CONT Livia was given the right to sit with the Vestal Virgins during publicperformances, she received large numbers of clients, commissioned anddedicated public buildings, sponsored charities and interceded on behalfof provincial emiss
2 CONC aries. Her entire public persona and all her imagesand actions were connected with marriage, the family and traditionalRoman family values. She endowed the "Portico of Livia" between the Opianand Cispian Hills in Rome as a public 
2 CONC recreational area consisting of alarge courtyard with a shrine to Concordia, dedicated to marital concordand harmony. At the time of its dedication, she gave a large banquet forthe respectable matrons of Rome. Her actions were dec
2 CONC idedly untraditionalbecause they were undertaken in public, but they were still directedtoward traditional ends. She was a very intelligent woman who was able tohelp her husband consolidate his power while maintaining the appearan
2 CONC ceof not doing so.
2 CONT
2 CONT Like some later first ladies, Livia had to put up with the rowdiness andrandiness of her husband. The Historian Suetonius noted that Augustus'"various acts of adultery" were "not denied even by his friends". Hisearly enemies (espe
2 CONC cially Mark Antony and his brother Lucius Antony, whenthey were still alive) were free with accusations, which he apparentlyanswered only with the famous "and so is your mother." Such carryings onwere expected of manly men in thos
2 CONC e days, and Livia stood by her Augustusthroughout -- and he did always come home. They stayed married for almost53 years until his death in 14 AD.
2 CONT
2 CONT Livia continued to exert her good influence over Augustus' successor, herson Tiberius, for fifteen years after Augustus' peaceful death. She diedin 29 AD at the age of 85. It was probably because of her politicalacumen and ability
2 CONC  to watch out for Tiberius that his problems with thepraetorian prefect Sejanus, a bureaucrat/pretender, did not occur untiltwo years after her death.
2 CONT
2 CONT Livia's was the exemplar for later Roman and other "First Ladies", butthe later Roman ones, all of whom also carried the honorific title "JuliaAugusta", just did not measure up because all seemed to have forgottenone qualification
2 CONC  that Livia always possessed: Livia was never accused orever even suspected of any kind of moral or sexual misconduct. LiviaDrusilla was so revered by later ages that she was claimed as anancestress of all European royal houses (t
2 CONC hrough Charlemagne andFarabert, King of Franks from 166 to 186). British colonials and Americanfounding fathers, steeped as they were in the classics, named theirdaughters Livia Drusilla in the hope that they would be good and fai
2 CONC thfulwives as was their namesake, and her combination name survives in asurprising number of American academic and plutocratic families.
2 CONT
2 CONT For more on the personal lives of Caesar Augustus and Livia, go to:
2 CONT http://www.fordham.edu/halsall/ancient/suetonius-augustus.html and scrolldown to Chapter 61.
2 CONT
2 CONT PS: You too can, with mathematical if not biological certainty, traceyour ancestry back to Livia. If you assume four generations per centuryand you know that in each previous generation you had double the numberof ancestors that y
2 CONC ou had in the next generation, it's easy to work outthe geometric progression. Just multiply by two for each generation:yourself, two parents, four grandparents, eight great grandparents, 16great-greats, etc. Using this method, yo
2 CONC u can see that you would have hadabout a trillion ancestors (one with 12 zeroes behind it) alive in 1000AD and about a septillion (24 zeroes) in Livia's time. Even the trillionis more folks than ever lived, so you can see that the
2 CONC re must have beensome, if distant, incest involved in your ancestry ( -- We won't tell).Since intercontinental travel was already possible in Livia's time andsince race-mixing wasn't much of a taboo in most places and interveningt
2 CONC imes, it's obvious that everyone alive in Livia's time was yourancestor, and inevitably though several different lines.
2 CONT ------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT A Woman for the Ages
2 CONT In ancient Rome, in an age when women were valued for little more thantheir family connections, property, or the proper public image they wereable to display, Livia Drusilla emerged as one of the most significantwomen in Roman his
2 CONC tory. Livia's stature in the Roman world wasunsurpassed. Most scholars believed that Livia conformed to the accepted,traditional role of women and gained influence in an acceptable way;(Huntsman par.1) her reputation as one of the
2 CONC  world's most powerful andinfluential women has remained unparalleled.
2 CONT
2 CONT Livia's Early Years
2 CONT Livia was...
2 CONT
2 CONT born in 58 BCE.
2 CONT the daughter of a Roman noble named Marcus Livius Drusus Claudius
2 CONT depicted in statues as having "a commanding presence, features of perfectregularity, enormous eyes and thick, wavy hair" (Payne 161).
2 CONT At the age of 15 or 16, Livia was married to Tiberius Claudius Nero, hercousin and rival to Octavian. Livia had her first child, Tiberius, in 42BCE. While Livia was pregnant with the couple's second child, Octavianbecame attracted
2 CONC  to the beautiful Livia. Octavian forced TiberiusClaudius Nero to divorce Livia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Livia and Octavian
2 CONT Octavian married Livia Drusilla with "indecent haste" (Earl 39) onJanuary 17, 38 BCE. This marriage was important for the followingreasons...
2 CONT
2 CONT ...it was politically astute.
2 CONT By marrying a woman whose lineage (Claudian family) was so important,Octavian would attract the support of ambitious aristocrats who judgedthat he might be able to carry them to power (Earl 39).
2 CONT ...it was "perhaps the most important single event in all the long riseof Octavian to supreme power" (Earl 39) but it was also "for love and forlife" (Hooper 345).
2 CONT A scandal arose over the union of Octavian and the pregnant, divorcedLivia. In order to lessen the effects of the scandal, Octavianmanufactured an omen designed to gain support for his marriage to Livia.
2 CONT
2 CONT It was said that as Livia rode to her country estate, she recieved adivine gift. An eagle, messenger of Jupiter, dropped a pregnant hen witha laurel sprig in her mouth into Livia's Lap.
2 CONT
2 CONT This symbol was to indicate that "the new race of triumphatores"(generals who added to the wealth of Rome) would result from his marriageto Livia (Flory).
2 CONT
2 CONT Livia proved to be a suitable wife, practicing "the virtues of chastity,obedience and silence" (Payne 161). But although she was silent, Livia'sinfluence on Octavian was great. He relied on her "critical intelligence"(Payne 161), 
2 CONC and it is said that Livia's "greatest service to the Romanslay in her devotion to Octavian and in the civilizing influence shebrought to bear on him" (Payne 160).
2 CONT
2 CONT Later, with Livia's counsel, Octavian completed his quest for control ofthe Roman state and won the battle of Actium. Then, in 27 BCE, Octavianrecieved the honorary title "Augustus," which acknowledged his positionof supreme prest
2 CONC ige and his protection by the gods.
2 CONT
2 CONT While exercising restraint in creating lavish tributes for himself,Augustus "carefully crafted the public display, honors and status givento his wife" (McManus)...
2 CONT
2 CONT Public statues honored Livia.
2 CONT State protection (sacrosanctity) gave her the right to sit in a specialplace with the vestal virgins during public performances.
2 CONT She dedicated public buildings and sponsored charities.
2 CONT All of her "public images and actions were closely connected withmarriage, the family and traditional Roman morality" (McManus).
2 CONT Livia's public image was carefully cultivated by Augustus.
2 CONT
2 CONT Livia After Octavian
2 CONT In 23 BCE, Augustus became gravely ill. Because he feared that he wasdying, "Augustus made no secret of his intention that the descendants ofJulia [his daughter from his previous marriage to Scribonia] were to behis political heir
2 CONC s" (Africa 26). After the health crisis passed,Augustus adopted Gaius and Lucuis (Julia's sons) as his legal heirs,thwarting Livia's desire for Tiberius to succeed Augustus.
2 CONT
2 CONT But by 4 AD, both Gaius and Lucius died unexpectedly so Augustus soughtto preserve the direct line by looking to Germanicus, son of Tiberius'brother Drusus. However, Germanicus was "too young and inexperienced torule" and Augustus
2 CONC  "reluctantly" turned to Tiberuis (Seager 36), who was"compelled to adopt his nephew germanicus, who would thus, when Augustusadopted Tiberius, become the princeps' grandson" (Seager 37). Livia's sonTiberius was adopted as Augustu
2 CONC s' heir, and her plans for the successionof her son were complete (Africa 28).
2 CONT
2 CONT On August 19, 14 AD, Augustus died, and Tiberius assumed power. "In hiswill, Augustus adopted Livia into his own Lineage, giving her the nameJulia Augusta, and the title Augusta was to become a special honorificfor subsequent empr
2 CONC esses" (McManus).
2 CONT
2 CONT As widow of Augustus anf mother of Tiberius, Livia enjoyed unprecedentedstatus and was honored with statues associating her with:
2 CONT
2 CONT Ceres, goddess of fertility and abundance, and Cybele, the Great Mother(McManus).
2 CONT
2 CONT Livia and Tiberius
2 CONT Tiberius' reign was characterized by peace and prosperity, but hesuffered personally. His adopted son Germanicus caused him troublethrough "unruliness and ambitious seeking of popularity" (Payne 195).
2 CONT
2 CONT Both Livia and Tiberius were jealous of the popularity enjoyed byGermanicus; they feared he would come to power and restore the oldrepublican constitution (Africa 168).
2 CONT
2 CONT Germanicus soon met a mysterious, untimely death. It was rumored thatLivia and Tiberius plotted to poison Germanicus, but the rumor was neverproven. It was no secret, though, that both Livia and Tiberius were"delighted" by Germani
2 CONC cus' death (Africa 172).
2 CONT
2 CONT Livia Livia continued to have a strong influence in the reign ofTiberius...
2 CONT
2 CONT "Tiberius did not like to cross her [Livia] in matters where she hadstrong feelings..."
2 CONT Livia "...never ceased to remind him what he owed her" (Hooper 367).
2 CONT In a possible attempt to escape Livia, Tiberius left Rome, never toreturn.
2 CONT Livia's power and ambitions were so embarrassing to Tiberius that herefused to attend her funeral in 29 AD and failed to carry out the termsof her will (Bonenkamp, et al.).
2 CONT
2 CONT Livia's Legacy
2 CONT
2 CONT Despite Tiberius' negative feelings for his mother, Livia's generallypopular reputation survived, and she was deified by her grandson Claudiusin 41 AD.
2 CONT
2 CONT Livia's reputation has remained largely untainted. In her nearly seventyyears as a public figure she...
2 CONT
2 CONT quietly shaped the rule of two Roman emperors
2 CONT and served as a model for Roman decorum.
2 CONT Livia's prominence in her lifetime was unmatched, and she has remained animportant, admired figure through 2000 years of history.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
