1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Julia Livilla''' (Classical Latin: IVLIA•LIVILLA, also called IVLIA•GERMANICI•CAESARIS•FILIA or LIVILLA•GERMANICI•CAESARIS•FILIA) born AD 18 - late AD 41 or early AD 42 and was the youngest child of Germanicus a
2 CONC nd Agrippina the Elder and the youngest sister of the Emperor Caligula.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Life===
2 CONT
2 CONT Livilla was the youngest great granddaughter of Emperor Augustus, great-niece and adoptive granddaughter of the Emperor Tiberius, sister of the Emperor Caligula, niece of the Emperor Claudius, and through her eldest sister Agrippi
2 CONC na the Younger, maternal aunt of the Emperor Nero. In most ancient literary sources, on inscriptions and on coins, she is simply called 'Julia'. It is possible that she dropped the use of her cognomen after the damnatio memoriae o
2 CONC f her paternal aunt Livilla (sister of Germanicus and Claudius), after whom she was named. However, on her sepulchral inscription, she is explicitly named Livilla, daughter of Germanicus, which leads to believe that in her times, 
2 CONC she was called either 'Julia' or 'Livilla'.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was born on Lesbos Island, one of the many Greek islands, during her parents' grand tour of the eastern Mediterranean, leading Germanicus to his command base in the imperial province of Syria for the maius imperium given to hi
2 CONC m by Tiberius over the territory east of the Adriatic Sea. As a young baby, she was with her mother and brother Caligula when they returned to Rome after Germanicus' untimely death in Antioch, on the 10th October 19.
2 CONT
2 CONT Julia Livilla grew up in the household of her great-grandmother Livia Drusilla and later under the care of her paternal grandmother Antonia Minor. She was first betrothed to a distant cousin, Quinctilius Varus, son of the ill-fate
2 CONC d governor of Germania, Publius Quinctilius Varus, and of Claudia Pulchra, granddaughter of Octavia Minor, sister of Augustus, but after Quinctillius was charged of maiestas in 27, the marriage did not occur. In 33, she married Ma
2 CONC rcus Vinicius. Vinicius' family came from a small town outside of Rome. He descended from a family of equestrian knights and his father and grandfather had served as consuls. Her husband was mild in character and was an elaborate 
2 CONC orator. Vinicius was appointed by Tiberius as a commissioner in early 37. He was also consul in 30 and proconsul of Asia in 38/39. According to an inscription, Julia Livilla may have accompanied her husband in Asia during his proc
2 CONC onsulship.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the first years of Caligula's reign, Livilla, along with her elder sisters Agrippina the Younger and Drusilla, received considerable honours and striking privileges, such as the rights of the Vestal Virgins (like the freedo
2 CONC m to view public games from the upper seats in the stadium), the inclusion of her name in the oath of loyalty to the emperor and her depiction on coins. She seemed to have enjoyed a rather wild life at the court of Caligula and ac
2 CONC cording to Suetonius, she, along with Agrippina, allowed herself to be prostituted by her brother to his catamites. Ancient writers even report gossip of incestuous relationships between Caligula and his sisters, including Livilla
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT In 39, Livilla was involved in an unsuccessful conspiracy (led perhaps by Agrippina) to overthrow Caligula and to replace him by his brother-in-law Marcus Aemilius Lepidus (Drusilla's widower, but also lover of Agrippina and Livil
2 CONC la). Livilla and her sister Agrippina the Younger were banished to the Pontine Islands (they were most likely separated in their exile and each one sent to a different island). After the deaths of Caligula, his fourth wife Milonia
2 CONC  Caesonia, and their daughter Julia Drusilla, she returned from exile on the orders of the new emperor, Livilla's paternal uncle Claudius. Later in 41, she fell out of favour with Valeria Messalina (Claudius's third wife) and was 
2 CONC charged by her paternal uncle Claudius for having adultery with Seneca the Younger. Both were exiled. She was most likely sent to Ventotene. Political considerations may have played a role in Julia Livilla's fate, more than just m
2 CONC oral or domestic preoccupations as inferred in the ancient sources. In late 41 or early 42, her uncle ordered her execution, apparently by starvation, without a defense and on unsupported charges. Her remains were later brought ba
2 CONC ck to Rome, probably when Agrippina became Empress; they were laid to rest in the Mausoleum of Augustus.
2 CONT
2 CONT The sepulchral inscription found on her cippus reads: "Livilla, daughter of Germanicus, lies here" (LIVILLA GERMANICI CAESARIS FILIA HIC SITA EST).
2 CONT
2 CONT A rich and precious vase found near this cippus is believed to have contained Livilla's ashes.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Sources===
2 CONT
2 CONT * English Wikipedia: [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Julia_Livilla Julia Livilla]
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT '''
2 CONT =Julia Livilla=
2 CONT ''' Julia Livilla (Classical Latin: IVLIA•LIVILLA, also called IVLIA•GERMANICI•CAESARIS•FILIA or LIVILLA•GERMANICI•CAESARIS•FILIA) (early AD 18 - late AD 41 or early AD 42) was the youngest child of Germanicus and Agrippina the El
2 CONC der and the youngest sister of the Emperor Caligula.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Life'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Julia Livilla was the youngest great-granddaughter of Emperor Augustus, great-niece and adoptive granddaughter of the Emperor Tiberius, sister of the Emperor Caligula, niece of the Emperor Claudius, and through her eldest sister A
2 CONC grippina the Younger, maternal aunt of the Emperor Nero. In most ancient literary sources, on inscriptions and on coins, she is simply called "Julia". It is possible that she dropped the use of her cognomen after the damnatio memo
2 CONC riae of her paternal aunt Livilla (sister of Germanicus and Claudius) after whom she was named. However, on her sepulchral inscription, she is explicitly named "Livilla, daughter of Germanicus", which suggests that in her time she
2 CONC  was called either "Julia" or "Livilla".
2 CONT
2 CONT She was born on Lesbos, one of the many Greek islands, during her parents' grand tour of the eastern Mediterranean, leading Germanicus to his command base in the imperial province of Syria for the maius imperium given to him by Ti
2 CONC berius over the territory east of the Adriatic Sea. As a young child, she was with her mother and brother Caligula when they returned to Rome after Germanicus' untimely death in Antioch in 19 CE.
2 CONT
2 CONT Julia Livilla grew up in the household of her great-grandmother Livia and later under the care of her paternal grandmother Antonia Minor. She was first betrothed to a distant cousin, Quinctilius Varus, son of the ill-fated governo
2 CONC r of Germania, Publius Quinctilius Varus, and of Claudia Pulchra, granddaughter of Octavia Minor, sister of Augustus, but after Quinctillius was charged of maiestas in 27, the marriage did not occur. In 33, she married Marcus Vini
2 CONC cius. Vinicius' family came from a small town outside of Rome. He descended from a family of equestrian knights and his father and grandfather had served as consuls. Her husband was mild in character and was an elaborate orator. V
2 CONC inicius was appointed by Tiberius as a commissioner in early 37. He was also consul in 30 and proconsul of Asia in 38/39. According to an inscription, Julia Livilla may have accompanied her husband in Asia during his proconsulship
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT During the first years of Caligula's reign, Livilla, along with her elder sisters Agrippina the Younger and Drusilla, received considerable honours and striking privileges, such as the rights of the Vestal Virgins (like the freedo
2 CONC m to view public games from the upper seats in the stadium), the inclusion of her name in the oath of loyalty to the emperor and her depiction on coins. She seemed to have enjoyed a rather wild life at the court of Caligula and ac
2 CONC cording to Suetonius, she, along with Agrippina, allowed herself to be prostituted by her brother to his catamites. Ancient writers even report gossip of incestuous relationships between Caligula and his sisters, including Livilla
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT In 39, Livilla was involved in an unsuccessful conspiracy (led perhaps by Agrippina) to overthrow Caligula and to replace him by his brother-in-law Marcus Aemilius Lepidus (Drusilla's widower, but also lover of Agrippina and Livil
2 CONC la). Livilla and her sister Agrippina the Younger were banished to the Pontine Islands (they were most likely separated in their exile and each one sent to a different island). After the deaths of Caligula, his fourth wife Milonia
2 CONC  Caesonia, and their daughter Julia Drusilla, she returned from exile on the orders of the new emperor, Livilla's paternal uncle Claudius. Later in 41, she fell out of favour with Messalina (Claudius's third wife) and was charged 
2 CONC by her paternal uncle Claudius for having adultery with Seneca the Younger. Both were exiled. She was most likely sent to Ventotene. Political considerations may have played a role in Julia Livilla's fate, more than just moral or 
2 CONC domestic preoccupations as inferred in the ancient sources. In late 41 or early 42, her uncle ordered her execution, apparently by starvation, without a defense and on unsupported charges. She was executed around the same time as 
2 CONC her cousin Julia, the daughter of her aunt Livilla. Her remains were later brought back to Rome, probably when Agrippina became Empress; they were laid to rest in the Mausoleum of Augustus.
2 CONT
2 CONT The sepulchral inscription found on her cippus reads: "Livilla, daughter of Germanicus, lies here" (LIVILLA GERMANICI CAESARIS FILIA HIC SITA EST). A rich and precious vase found near this cippus is believed to have contained Livi
2 CONC lla's ashes.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Portraiture'''
2 CONT
2 CONT A series of portrait heads, usually known as the Leptis-Malta type, has been identified as Livilla.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''In literature'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * In I, Claudius and Claudius the God, the novels by Robert Graves, Livilla is called 'Lesbia', a name alluding to the island where she was born. She was almost omitted from the television adaptation, there was just a brief mentio
2 CONC n of Caligula having three sisters at one point, and another brief mention of two sisters just before his assassination.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Appearance in media'''
2 CONT
2 CONT In the 1968 British television series The Caesars Julia Livilla was portrayed by Jenny White. In the film Caligula she was played by Mirella D'Angelo.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source : '''
