2 CONT
2 CONT Flavius Valerius Leo (401–18 January 474), known in English as Leo the Thracian or Leo I, was a Byzantine Emperor who ruled from 457 to 474. He was known as Magnus Thrax (the "Great Thracian") by his supporters, and Macellus ("the
2 CONC  Butcher") by his enemies.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and children
2 CONT
2 CONT Leo and Verina had three children. Their eldest daughter Ariadne was born prior to the death of Marcian (reigned 450 - 457).[7]. Ariadne had a younger sister, Leontia. Leontia was first married to Patricius, a son of Aspar. Their 
2 CONC marriage was probably annulled when Aspar and another of his sons, Ardabur, were assassinated in 471. Leontia then married Marcian, a son of Anthemius and Marcia Euphemia. The couple led a failed revolt against Zeno in 478-479. Th
2 CONC ey were exiled to Isauria following their defeat.[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT An unknown son was born in 463. He died five months following his birth. The only sources about him are a horoscope by Rhetorius and a hagiography of Daniel the Stylite.[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT The Georgian Chronicle, a 13th century compilation drawing from earlier sources, reports a marriage of Vakhtang I of Iberia to Princess Helena of Byzantium, identifying her as a daughter of the predecessor of Zeno.[9]. This predec
2 CONC essor was probably Leo I, the tale attributing a third daughter to Leo. Cyril Toumanoff identified two children of this marriage. Mithridates of Iberia and Leo of Iberia. This younger Leo was father of Guaram I of Iberia. The accu
2 CONC racy of the descent is unknown.
2 CONT
