1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''
2 CONT =Appius Claudius Pulcher=
2 CONT '''
2 CONT
2 CONT Appius Claudius Pulcher was a Roman politician. An early supporter of Augustus, he was elected consul in 38 BC.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Biography'''
2 CONT
2 CONT A member of the patrician branch of the Claudii family, Pulcher originally bore the name Gaius Claudius Pulcher, and was the natural born son of Gaius Claudius Pulcher, a praetor in 56 BC. Upon his adoption by his uncle Appius Cla
2 CONC udius Pulcher, the consul of 54 BC, he took his adopted father’s praenomen. He, along with his natural born brother, also called Appius Claudius Pulcher, prosecuted Titus Annius Milo in 51 BC for the murder of Publius Clodius Pulc
2 CONC her, and managed to convict him with the help of Pompey.
2 CONT
2 CONT Originally a supporter of Mark Antony following the death of Julius Caesar, Pulcher had some sympathy towards the Liberatores, and showed some willingness to join Decimus Junius Brutus Albinus. However, by 38 BC, the year he was e
2 CONC lected consul, he had attached himself to the cause of Octavianus, one of the earliest patricians to publicly join the heir of Julius Caesar. This alliance came about as a result of the marriage of Octavianus to the aristocratic L
2 CONC ivia Drusilla, and by doing so, he attached his auctoritas to Octavianus’s rising power and fortune, and was greatly rewarded by Octavianus in the years to come.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 36 BC, during Octavianus’s war with Sextus Pompey, Pulcher was given command of the rear guard of Octavian’s fleet as it sailed from Puteoli, which then suffered some damage in a storm. Then in 34 BC, he was proconsul in one of
2 CONC  the provinces in Hispania where he remained for two years, during which time he was awarded the title imperator. In 32 BC, Pulcher was back in Rome where he celebrated a triumph on 1 June. After this, Pulcher was given no further
2 CONC  military commands, and by 31 BC he had been made a member of the Septemviri Epulones.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pulcher had at least one son, who may have been put to death by the emperor Augustus on charges of adultery with the emperor’s daughter Julia. He possibly had another son, Marcus Valerius Messalla Appianus, who was probably adopte
2 CONC d by Marcus Valerius Messalla, the suffect consul of 32 BC.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source : '''
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Appius_Claudius_Pulcher_(consul_38_BC)
