0 @N6000000002322297023@ NOTE Chlotar I married sisters,  Ingund first, then Aregund.
0 @N6000000002187665830@ NOTE Ingund, wife of Clotaire I
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Ingonde, Ingund, or Ingunda (born c.499, Thuringia) was the daughter of King Baderic of Thüringia (c.480 - c.529. She was the wife of Clotaire I and queen of the Franks. She was the mother of Chram, Charibert I, Guntram, and Sigeb
1 CONC ert I. She was the sister of Clotaire's wife Aregund.
0 @N6000000002187572289@ NOTE Aregund, wife of Clotaire I
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Arégonde, Aregund, or Aregunda (French: Arnegonde de Worms) (Worms, c.515-573) was the wife of Clotaire I, king of the Franks, and the mother of Chilperic I of Neustria. She was the sister of Ingund, one of Clotaire's other wives.
1 CONC  She may have been born sometime between 500 and 510 and may have died sometime between 580 and 590.
1 CONT
1 CONT Her sepulchre, among dozens of others, was discovered in 1959 in the Saint Denis Basilica by archaeologist Michel Fleury. It contained remarkably well-preserved clothing items and jewelery, which were used to identify her.
1 CONT
1 CONT In an episode of the television series "Digging for the Truth" (aired in May 2006), host Josh Bernstein arranged a DNA test of a sample of her remains, to see if it showed any Middle Eastern characteristics. It did not. It is not 
1 CONC known why Bernstein chose to test the DNA of the wife of a Merovingian king as opposed to a direct descendent of the Merovingian line, which logically would have been the only way to test the hypothetical Middle-Eastern origin of 
1 CONC the Merovingian bloodline.
0 @N6000000002187665871@ NOTE Radegund
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT  Radegund was born to King Berthar, one of the three kings of Thuringia (a kingdom located in present day Germany), some time in the first half of the 6th century.
1 CONT
1 CONT Radegund's uncle, Hermanfrid, killed Berthar in battle, orphaning her. Then, after allying with the Frankish King Theuderic, Hermanfrid defeated his other brother Baderic. However, having crushed his brothers and seized control of
1 CONC  Thuringia, Hermanfrid reneged on his deal with Theuderic to share sovereignty.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 531 Theuderic returned to Thuringia with his brother Clotaire I. Together they defeated Hermanfrid and conquered his kingdom. Clotaire I also took charge of Radegund, taking her back to Merovingian Gaul with him and making her 
1 CONC his wife.
1 CONT
1 CONT Radegund was one of Clotaire I’s four wives (the other three being Chunsina, Ingund and Ingund’s sister Aregund). She bore him no children, and, after Clotaire I had her brother assassinated, she turned to God, founding a nunnery 
1 CONC in Poitiers.
1 CONT
1 CONT Her chaplain was the poet Venantius Fortunatus and she was a friend of Gregory of Tours. She died on 13 August 586 and her funeral, which both men attended, was three days later.
1 CONT
1 CONT She was canonized as a saint in the ninth century. Five English parish churches are dedicated to her, and she had a chapel in the old St Paul's Cathedral, as well as in Gloucester, Lichfield, and Exeter Cathedrals. Saint Radegund'
1 CONC s Abbey, near Dover, was founded in her honour in 1191. She is also the patron saint of Jesus College, Cambridge, which was founded on the site of the twelfth-century nunnery of Saint Mary and Saint Radegund.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT Gregory of Tours, Glory of the Confessors, translation by R. Van Dam (Liverpool, 1988) 
1 CONT Gregory of Tours, History of the Franks, translation by L. Thorpe (Penguin, 1974: many reprints) 
1 CONT Venantius Fortunatus, The Life of the Holy Radegund, translation by J. McNamara and J. Halborg 
1 CONT History of St Ragegund from the Jesus College, Cambridge, web site 
1 CONT  Saints Portal 
1 CONT Retrieved from "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Radegund"
0 @N6000000002322297435@ NOTE Merovingians historically seemed to be of a violent and hostile nature.
