0 @N6000000002322297524@ NOTE He was the last of the Mervoingian dynasty to rule a realm united in more
1 CONT than name only. He moved his capital from Austrasia to Paris, a central
1 CONT location from which the kingdom could be governed more efficiently. Famed for
1 CONT his love of justice, he was nevertheless greedy and dissolute. The prosperity
1 CONT of his reign, and the revival of the arts during this period, can be judged
1 CONT by the rich contents of the tombs of the period and from the goldsmith's work
1 CONT at churches. He encouraged learning & founded Saint-Denis abbey.
0 @N6000000002187572209@ NOTE Dagobert I
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Merovingian Dynasty Kings of All the Franks 
1 CONT Kings of Neustria 
1 CONT Dagobert I (c. 603 - January 19, 639) was the king of the Franks from 629 to 639.
1 CONT
1 CONT The son of King Clotaire II, Dagobert became king of Austrasia and on the death of his father, the sole king of the Franks. By 632 he had Bourgogne and Aquitaine under his rule, becoming the most powerful of the Merovingian kings 
1 CONC and the most respected ruler in the West. He married five times.
1 CONT
1 CONT As king, Dagobert I made Paris his capital. During his reign, he built the Altes Schloss Castle in Meersburg, Germany which today is the oldest inhabited castle in that country. Devoutly religious, Dagobert was also responsible fo
1 CONC r the construction of the Saint Denis Basilica at the site of a Benedictine Monastery in Paris.
1 CONT
1 CONT Dagobert was the last of the Merovingian kings to wield any real royal power. In 632 the nobles of Austrasia revolted under Mayor of the Palace Pepin I, and Dagobert appeased the rebellious nobles by putting his three-year-old son
1 CONC  Sigebert III on the Austrasian throne, thereby ceding royal power in all but name. When Dagobert died in 639, another son, Clovis II, inherited the rest of his kingdom at age five.
1 CONT
1 CONT This pattern continued for the next century until Pippin III finally deposed the last Merovingian king in 751, establishing the Carolingian dynasty. The Merovingian boy-kings remained ineffective rulers who inherited the throne as
1 CONC  young children and lived only long enough to produce a male heir or two, while real power lay in the hands of the noble families (the Old Noblesse) who exercised feudal control over most of the land.
1 CONT
1 CONT Dagobert was the first of the French kings to be buried in the Royal tombs at Saint Denis Basilica.
1 CONT
1 CONT King Dagobert was immortalized by the song Le bon roi Dagobert (The good king Dagobert), a nursery rhyme featuring exchanges between the king and his chief adviser, St. Eligius (Eloi in the French text). The satirical rhymes place
1 CONC  Dagobert in various ridiculous positions, from which Eligius' good advice manages to extract him. The text, which probably originated in the 18th century, became extremely popular as an expression of the anti-monarchist sentiment
1 CONC  of the French Revolution. Other than placing Dagobert and Eligius in their respective roles, it has no historical accuracy.
0 @N6000000001744880909@ NOTE [s2.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT Funerary monument at St. Denis cathedral was carved in 13th century, and represents the legend of John the Hermit, who dreamed that the soul of Dagobert was stolen away by demons and finally snatched from their clutches by the int
1 CONC ercession of Sts. Denis, Maurice and Martin. The statues of Nanthild, his wife and Clovis II, his son, are 19th c. reproductions.  (Source: Alain Erlande-Brandenburg, The Abbey Church of St-Denis, Vol II, The Royal Tombs; Editions
1 CONC  de la Tourelle, Paris, 1984).
1 CONT
1 CONT Plaque at St. Denis reads "Premiere Dynasty: Dagobert I, Fondatuer de l'Abbaye Royale de s'Denis, [died 638, age 36]" and "Nanthilde, femme de Roi Dagobert [died 642]"Funerary monument at St. Denis cathedral was carved in 13th cen
1 CONC tury, and represents the legend of John the Hermit, who dreamed that the soul of Dagobert was stolen away by demons and finally snatched from their clutches by the intercession of Sts. Denis, Maurice and Martin. The statues of Nan
1 CONC thild, his wife and Clovis II, his son, are 19th c. reproductions.  (Source: Alain Erlande-Brandenburg, The Abbey Church of St-Denis, Vol II, The Royal Tombs; Editions de la Tourelle, Paris, 1984).
1 CONT
1 CONT Plaque at St. Denis reads "Premiere Dynasty: Dagobert I, Fondatuer de l'Abbaye Royale de s'Denis, [died 638, age 36]" and "Nanthilde, femme de Roi Dagobert [died 642]"
0 @N6000000002244390339@ NOTE He was the last of the Mervoingian dynasty to rule a realm united in more
1 CONT than name only. He moved his capital from Austrasia to Paris, a central
1 CONT location from which the kingdom could be governed more efficiently. Famed for
1 CONT his love of justice, he was nevertheless greedy and dissolute. The prosperity
1 CONT of his reign, and the revival of the arts during this period, can be judged
1 CONT by the rich contents of the tombs of the period and from the goldsmith's work
