1 CONT  Il est aussi connu sous le nom de Roi Dagobert Ier de Bourgogne 1 .
1 CONT  Il est aussi connu sous le nom de Roi Dagobert Ier de Neustrie 1 .
1 CONT  Il est couronné roi d'Austrasie en 0623.
1 CONT  Il est couronné roi de Neustrie et Bourgogne en 0629.
1 CONT  Il est couronné roi des Francs en 0632.
1 CONT  Il est inhumé à abbaye de Saint-Denis, France.
0 @N6000000002240782524@ NOTE He was the last of the Mervoingian dynasty to rule a realm united in more
1 CONT than name only. He moved his capital from Austrasia to Paris, a central
1 CONT location from which the kingdom could be governed more efficiently. Famed for
1 CONT his love of justice, he was nevertheless greedy and dissolute. The prosperity
1 CONT of his reign, and the revival of the arts during this period, can be judged
1 CONT by the rich contents of the tombs of the period and from the goldsmith's work
1 CONT at churches. He encouraged learning & founded Saint-Denis abbey.
0 @N6000000001355550421@ NOTE [blended.FTW]
1 CONT [mergebase.FTW]
1 CONT King of Austrasia 623-632
1 CONT King of the Franks 629-639
1 CONT In 623, Dagobert's father, Chlotar II, King of the Franks, made him king of Austrasia to please the leading Austrasian nobles: Mayor of thePalace Pepin I and Saint Arnulf, Bishop of Metz. When Chlotar died in629, Dagobert became s
1 CONC ole King of the Franks, and he moved his capital from Austrasia to Paris. Later, Dagobert left the council of Pepin for a more flexible Neustrian Mayor of the Palace. In 632, he was forced to put his three-year old son Sigebert on
1 CONC  the throne of Austrasia as the nobles were in revolt, however Pepin was not made his Mayor of the Palace. The Neustrian nobles then wished to unite with Burgundy, and so they urged Dagobert to put his son Clovis II as king of bot
1 CONC h those kingdoms, although he was only 5 years old and could be easily manipulated by the nobles. When Dagobert died in 639, the nobles of the kingdoms controlled both his sons, now puppet kings.
1 CONT [Fix.FTW]
