0 @N6000000002187572220@ NOTE Nanthild
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Nanthild (c.610 – 642) (also known as Nantéchilde, Nanthechilde, Nanthildis, Nanthilde, or Nantechildis) was the third of many wives of Dagobert I, king of the Franks (629-639).
1 CONT
1 CONT She was of Saxon lineage, born about 608 or 610. The Lexikon des Mittelalters calls her ein Mädchen aus dem Dienstpersonal ("a maiden of the royal [ Austrasian] household"). Her elevation to consort may have given importance to he
1 CONC r relatives: her brother Lanthegisel was an important landowner in the Limousin and a relation of Aldegisel. Dagobert set aside his wife Gomatrud to marry her, ca. 629; to her was born Clovis II, second eldest of Dagobert's surviv
1 CONC ing sons and the one who succeeded him in Neustria and Burgundy. After Dagobert's death in January 639, she was initially regent for her son, accompanied by Ega, mayor of the Neustrian palace and an opponent of the powerful contin
1 CONC gent of nobles headed by the Burgundofarones, whose seat was at Meaux.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the interest of reducing noble Burgundian independence of the Merovingian palace, she married her niece Ragnoberta to the Frank Flaochad and had the magnates and bishops of the realm of Burgundy acclaim him mayor of the palace 
1 CONC at Orléans in 642. Soon she died at Landry in Burgundy, where she had long resided; her body was translated to the Saint Denis Basilica. Her untimely death allowed her son to fall under the influence of the nobility, who abhorred 
1 CONC a strong royal hand.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Fredegar's Chronicle, book IV
1 CONT     * Geary, Patrick J., 1998. Before France and Germany: The Creation and Transformation of the Merovingian World (Oxford & New York: Oxford University Press)
1 CONT     * Wood, Ian, 1994. The Merovingian Kingdoms. 450-751 (London)
1 CONT     * Lexikon des Mittelalters vol. VI.1018
