0 @N6000000002188311737@ NOTE Ecgberht of Kent
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Ecgberht, or Egbert (d. July 4, 673) was a King of Kent who ruled from 664 to 673, succeeding his father Eorcenberht.
1 CONT
1 CONT He may have still been a child when he became king following his father's death on July 14, 664, because his mother Seaxburh was recorded as having been regent.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ecgberht's court seems to have had many diplomatic and ecclesiastic contacts. He hosted Wilfrid and Benedict Biscop, and provided escorts to Theodore and Hadrian for their travels in Gaul.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Mildrith legend reports that he had his cousins Aethelred and Aethelberht (sons of his uncle Eormenred) killed; this may reflect a dynastic struggle that ended in the success of Eorcenberht's line.
1 CONT
1 CONT A charter records his patronage of the monastery at Chertsey.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ecgberht was succeeded by his brother Hlothhere, who was in turn succeeded by Ecgberht's son Eadric and still later by his other son Wihtred.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] See also
1 CONT
1 CONT List of monarchs of Kent
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Bede, The Ecclesiastical History of the English People
1 CONT     * D. P. Kirby, The Earliest English Kings (London: Unwin Hyman, 1991), pp. 43-44
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Eorcenberht King of Kent
