0 @N6000000003635701586@ NOTE was murdered.
1 CONT
1 CONT [3963] "Holy Blood ... Holy Grail", chart 2 & 3, p 215 & 228; King of Austrasia in 674; assassinated by order of Pepin 'the Fat'
1 CONT
1 CONT "Bloodlines...", p 223, lanced to death by one of Pepin's men; impaled to a tree
1 CONT
1 CONT http://library.monterey.edu/merrill/family/dorsett6/d0052/I11030.html b 652 Ancestral roots p. 204
1 CONT Carolingeans table 1>dd
1 CONT Stuart p. 216>bd dd=678, King of Austrasia, 676-680
1 CONT
1 CONT per email 7-Apr-2001 from Susan Nesfield, Yorkshire, England (nesfield@lineone.net):
1 CONT ... information ... from an elderly man who had spent many years researching the Nesfield family name, ... according to records held in Bolton Abbey, Dagobert II had married Matilde of Irish descent, and later lived in York after 
1 CONC being expelled from France. While in York he is said to have married the daughter of an Anglo-Saxon landowner, called Imayne Naasfeld, by Ariano/Celtic Church Rites. He left her pregnant, and returned to France in 671, where he ma
1 CONC rried Giselle de Razes. The Nesfield family are supposed to descend from this York landowner.
0 @N6000000003949675423@ NOTE Dagobert II "the Young" (c.650 – December 23, 679) was the king of Austrasia (676 – 679), the son of Sigebert III, one of the rois-fainéants ("do-nothing kings") and the last Merovingian dynasty to rule independently in Austrasia,
1 CONC  with the exception of Charles Martel's dubious candidate Clotaire IV.
1 CONT The Arnulfing mayor of the Austrasian palace, Grimoald the Elder, the son of Pippin of Landen, and Dagobert's guardian, had had his son Childebert adopted by Sigebert, who was at the time still childless. When Sigebert died in 656
1 CONC , Grimoald seized the throne in order to secure it for his own son, and cut Dagobert's hair, thus marking him unfit for kingship, and exiled the boy, tonsured, to the care of Desiderius, Bishop of Poitiers, where there was a cathe
1 CONC dral school. The tale that Dagobert was ordered to be killed, that his death was published about, but that he was spirited out of the country and raised in an Irish monastery seems to be an embellishment, perhaps developed to expl
1 CONC ain the silence of Sigebert's queen Chimnechild, Dagobert's mother. She may have cooperated with Grimoald to set up Childebert the Adopted; later she hoped by marrying her daughter Bilichild to Childeric II to keep the eventual Au
1 CONC strasian heir in her bloodline[1]. It has also been hypothesised that Chimnechild was not Dagobert's mother, thus her reason for abandoning him.
1 CONT The boy was sent on to a monastery in Ireland, sometimes identified as Slane, and to be further polished as a page in an Anglo-Saxon court in England. An old tradition relates that he had married Mechthilde, an Anglo-Saxon princes
1 CONC s, during his exile, but the tradition that among his daughters was Saint Hermine, abbess of Oëren, and Saint Adula, abbess of Pfalzel, are fabrications, perhaps designed to link the saintly foundresses of these abbeys with the re
1 CONC vered Merovingian line.
1 CONT In the meantime the great nobles of Austrasia appealed to Clovis II, king of Neustria, who expelled the usurpers, executing Grimoald and Childebert, and added Austrasia to his own realm. The dating of these events is greatly confu
1 CONC sed, they occurred perhaps as soon after 656 as 657 or perhaps as late as 661, under Clotaire III, Clovis' son. The effective ruler however was the Neustrian major domo Ebroin, who was obliged in soon thereafter (in 660 or 662) to
1 CONC  give the Austrasian realm a king of its own once more: the choice was the child king Childeric II, brother of Clotaire III, with a mayor of the palace, Wulfoald, as regent. The young king was assassinated on a hunt near Maastrich
1 CONC t in 675, and in the chaotic power struggle that ensued, the Austrasian magnates, who wanted a king of Merovingian blood, pressed Wulfoald for the return of Dagobert, while opponents of Wulfoald acclaimed one Clovis III. Ebroin re
1 CONC turned from a monastic "retirement" to lead Clovis' partisans, but Wulfoald effected Dagobert's succession in 676, partly through the help of Wilfrid, Archbishop of York, on Clovis' untimely death. In spite of the continuing bitte
1 CONC r enmity of Ebroin and the party who had attempted to press Clovis as an alternate candidate (though he was perhaps an imposter), Dagobert was restored to a portion of his rightful lands, a territory along the Rhine, which pious t
1 CONC radition relates that he governed with the mildness and piety his childhood experience had taught him, but which history suggests he left largely to the mayor of the Austrasian palace, while he concerned himself more with the foun
1 CONC ding of cloisters and abbeys, including Surburg and Wissembourg in Alsace, where the Duke was his cousin. Nonetheless, he was undoubtedly an intelligent, educated man, an adult at the time of his succession, who could not be compl
1 CONC etely controlled by factions and mayors.
1 CONT The dynamics of Dagobert's career are largely a passive reflection of the competition between two sources of power, patronage and prestige, the palace institutions of Neustria on the one hand, and on the other, of Austrasia, firml
1 CONC y in the control of the Arnulfing dynasty that would become the Carolingians in the following century. In the chaos, the search for a consistent, rational pattern is hard to follow in the shifting loyalties.
1 CONT During revived conflict between Neustria and Austrasia, Dagobert in his turn was murdered in another hunting incident, December 23, 679, near Stenay-sur-Meuse in the Ardennes, probably on orders from Ebroin, still mayor of the pal
1 CONC ace in Neustria. Wilfrid must have remained in Austrasia until this time, because, according to his biographer, Wilfrid left Austrasia after the death of Dagobert, in mortal danger from the supporters of Ebroin. At the cloister of
1 CONC  Stenay afterwards there grew a cult of Dagobert, venerated as early as 1068 as "Saint Dagobert". The cult spread from there into Lotharingia and Alsace, and Saint Dagobert is recognized by the Roman Catholic Church, like his fath
1 CONC er and many royal Merovingians.
1 CONT After Dagobert's brief reign, leaving his lands without a male heir, the lords of the Rhineland divided the territory among themselves, while Pippin II, Mayor of the Palace of Austrasia (679-714) dominated Austrasia, and left the 
1 CONC throne empty until after the battle of Tertry (687), when he accepted Theuderic III.
1 CONT The by now (in)famous attempt to associate Dagobert II and his supposed descendants with a secret Merovingian line of legitimate royal succession, unjustly displaced by the Carolingian and Capetian monarchies but continuing into m
1 CONC odern times, is without historical foundation. It is one of the central myths in the historical fantasies associated with the town of Rennes-le-Château.
1 CONT Died    23 December 679 at Lorraine, France in a hunting accident by Ebroin, mayor of the palace; may have been murdered, and is considered a martyr 
0 @N6000000009304865550@ NOTE #Générale#note décès :  assassiné
1 CONT
1 CONT #Générale#assassiné par ordre de Pépin de Herstall
1 CONT s:ds01.2 ; dooghe.56 & 55 ; hg90.238
0 @N6000000002187700871@ NOTE Dagobert II
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Dagobert II "the Young" (c.650 – December 23, 679) was the king of Austrasia (676 – 679), the son of Sigebert III, one of the rois-fainéants ("do-nothing kings") and the last Merovingian dynasty to rule independently in Austrasia,
1 CONC  with the exception of Charles Martel's dubious candidate Clotaire IV.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Arnulfing mayor of the Austrasian palace, Grimoald the Elder, the son of Pippin of Landen, and Dagobert's guardian, had had his son Childebert adopted by Sigebert, who was at the time still childless. When Sigebert died in 656
1 CONC , Grimoald seized the throne in order to secure it for his own son, and cut Dagobert's hair, thus marking him unfit for kingship, and exiled the boy, tonsured, to the care of Desiderius, Bishop of Poitiers, where there was a cathe
1 CONC dral school. The tale that Dagobert was ordered to be killed, that his death was published about, but that he was spirited out of the country and raised in an Irish monastery seems to be an embellishment, perhaps developed to expl
1 CONC ain the silence of Sigebert's queen Chimnechild, Dagobert's mother. She may have cooperated with Grimoald to set up Childebert the Adopted; later she hoped by marrying her daughter Bilichild to Childeric II to keep the eventual Au
1 CONC strasian heir in her bloodline[1]. It has also been hypothesised that Chimnechild was not Dagobert's mother, thus her reason for abandoning him.
1 CONT
1 CONT The boy was sent on to a monastery in Ireland, sometimes identified as Slane, and to be further polished as a page in an Anglo-Saxon court in England. An old tradition relates that he had married Mechthilde, an Anglo-Saxon princes
1 CONC s, during his exile, but the tradition that among his daughters was Saint Hermine, abbess of Oëren, and Saint Adula, abbess of Pfalzel, are fabrications, perhaps designed to link the saintly foundresses of these abbeys with the re
1 CONC vered Merovingian line.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the meantime the great nobles of Austrasia appealed to Clovis II, king of Neustria, who expelled the usurpers, executing Grimoald and Childebert, and added Austrasia to his own realm. The dating of these events is greatly confu
1 CONC sed, they occurred perhaps as soon after 656 as 657 or perhaps as late as 661, under Clotaire III, Clovis' son. The effective ruler however was the Neustrian major domo Ebroin, who was obliged in soon thereafter (in 660 or 662) to
1 CONC  give the Austrasian realm a king of its own once more: the choice was the child king Childeric II, brother of Clotaire III, with a mayor of the palace, Wulfoald, as regent. The young king was assassinated on a hunt near Maastrich
1 CONC t in 675, and in the chaotic power struggle that ensued, the Austrasian magnates, who wanted a king of Merovingian blood, pressed Wulfoald for the return of Dagobert, while opponents of Wulfoald acclaimed one Clovis III. Ebroin re
1 CONC turned from a monastic "retirement" to lead Clovis' partisans, but Wulfoald effected Dagobert's succession in 676, partly through the help of Wilfrid, Archbishop of York, on Clovis' untimely death. In spite of the continuing bitte
1 CONC r enmity of Ebroin and the party who had attempted to press Clovis as an alternate candidate (though he was perhaps an imposter), Dagobert was restored to a portion of his rightful lands, a territory along the Rhine, which pious t
1 CONC radition relates that he governed with the mildness and piety his childhood experience had taught him, but which history suggests he left largely to the mayor of the Austrasian palace, while he concerned himself more with the foun
1 CONC ding of cloisters and abbeys, including Surburg and Wissembourg in Alsace, where the Duke was his cousin. Nonetheless, he was undoubtedly an intelligent, educated man, an adult at the time of his succession, who could not be compl
1 CONC etely controlled by factions and mayors.
1 CONT
1 CONT The dynamics of Dagobert's career are largely a passive reflection of the competition between two sources of power, patronage and prestige, the palace institutions of Neustria on the one hand, and on the other, of Austrasia, firml
1 CONC y in the control of the Arnulfing dynasty that would become the Carolingians in the following century. In the chaos, the search for a consistent, rational pattern is hard to follow in the shifting loyalties.
1 CONT
1 CONT During revived conflict between Neustria and Austrasia, Dagobert in his turn was murdered in another hunting incident, December 23, 679, near Stenay-sur-Meuse in the Ardennes, probably on orders from Ebroin, still mayor of the pal
1 CONC ace in Neustria. Wilfrid must have remained in Austrasia until this time, because, according to his biographer, Wilfrid left Austrasia after the death of Dagobert, in mortal danger from the supporters of Ebroin. At the cloister of
1 CONC  Stenay afterwards there grew a cult of Dagobert, venerated as early as 1068 as "Saint Dagobert". The cult spread from there into Lotharingia and Alsace, and Saint Dagobert is recognized by the Roman Catholic Church, like his fath
1 CONC er and many royal Merovingians.
1 CONT
1 CONT After Dagobert's brief reign, leaving his lands without a male heir, the lords of the Rhineland divided the territory among themselves, while Pippin II, Mayor of the Palace of Austrasia (679-714) dominated Austrasia, and left the 
1 CONC throne empty until after the battle of Tertry (687), when he accepted Theuderic III.
1 CONT
1 CONT The by now (in)famous attempt to associate Dagobert II and his supposed descendants with a secret Merovingian line of legitimate royal succession, unjustly displaced by the Carolingian and Capetian monarchies but continuing into m
1 CONC odern times, is without historical foundation. It is one of the central myths in the historical fantasies associated with the town of Rennes-le-Château.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT See also
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Franks (main history of Frankish kingdoms)
