0 @N6000000003051155189@ NOTE On his father's death, at age 5, he was kidnapped by the presiding mayor of the palace, Grimoald. It was not difficult to convince the court that he was dead. Balking at actually killing him, Grimoald had given him into the keepin
1 CONC g of the Bishop of Poitiers. The bishop was equally reluctant to murder the child, and confined him to permanent exile in Ireland. He grew to manhood in the monastery at Slane, not far from Dublin, and recieved an education unatta
1 CONC inable in France at the time.
1 CONT At some time during this period, he attended the court of the High Kings at Tara. He married his first wife while still in Ireland.
1 CONT Then he moved to York, England where he became the student of his mentor, Saint Wilfrid, Bishop of York.
1 CONT When his wife died, he returned to France and married again, and waited and watched for an opportuinity which came in 674 when he reclaimed his throne, in which endeavor he was assisted by Saint Wilfred and Saint Amatus, Bishop of
1 CONC  Sion.
1 CONT He did not become the wished for "arm of God" for the Church however. he curbed expansion of the Church within his realm, and thereby incurred ecclesiastical displeasure. By his marriage into the royal line of the Visigoths he acq
1 CONC uired considerable territroy in what is now the Languedoc.
1 CONT By the time he had ruled for 3 years, he had many powerful enemies.
1 CONT Near his palace at Stenay, there was a long stretch of heavy woods,considered "sacred" called the "Forest of Woevres", where. on Dec. 23,679, he went hunting. He lay down about noon to rest at the foot of a tree beside a stream. W
1 CONC hile he slept, one of his servents, acting under Pepin's orders, pierced him through the eye with a lance. The Church sided with the murderers.
1 CONT He was buried at Stenay, in the royal chapel of Saint Remy.
1 CONT In 872, he was moved to what became the Church of Saint Dagobert, but no-one seems willing to admit why he was canonized. Perhaps it was an attempt to make amends. His feast day is Dec. 23.
1 CONT During the French Revolution, the church was destroyed and the relics scattered. A ritually incised skull said to be his is in the custody of a convent in Mons.
1 CONT (HB,HG)
1 CONT
1 CONT [Wikipedia, "Dagobert II", retrieved 4 Oct 07]
1 CONT Dagobert II (c. 650 - December 23, 679) was the king of Austrasia (676 - 679), the son of Sigebert III and Chimnechild of Burgundy. He was the last of the Merovingian dynasty to rule independently in Austrasia, with the exception 
1 CONC of Charles Martel's dubious candidate Clotaire IV.
1 CONT
1 CONT Dagobert II was the son of Sigibert III (631 - 656), an Austrasian king of the Merovingian line. The Arnulfing mayor of the Austrasian palace, Grimoald the Elder, the son of Pippin of Landen, and Dagobert's guardian, had had his o
1 CONC wn son Childebert adopted by Sigebert III, when Sigebert was still childless. Then when Sigebert died in 656, Grimoald seized the throne for his own son and had Dagobert tonsured and exiled.
1 CONT
1 CONT The tale that Dagobert was ordered to be killed and his death published about, but that he was spirited out of the country, seems to be an embellishment, perhaps developed to explain the silence of Dagobert's mother Chimnechild. S
1 CONC he may have cooperated with Grimoald to set up Childebert the Adopted; later she hoped by marrying her daughter Bilichild to Childeric II to keep the eventual Austrasian heir in her bloodline. [1] It has also been hypothesised tha
1 CONC t Chimnechild was not Dagobert's mother, thus her reason for abandoning him.
1 CONT
1 CONT Dagobert was given to the care of Desiderius, Bishop of Poitiers, where there was a cathedral school. The boy was sent on to a monastery in Ireland, sometimes identified as Slane, to be further trained as a page at an Anglo-Saxon 
1 CONC court in England. An old tradition relates that he married Mechthilde, an Anglo-Saxon princess, during his exile, but the tradition that among his daughters was Saint Hermine, abbess of Oëren, and Saint Adula, abbess of Pfalzel, a
1 CONC re fabrications, perhaps designed to link the saintly foundresses of these abbeys with the revered Merovingian line.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the meantime the great nobles of Austrasia appealed to Clovis II, king of Neustria, who expelled the usurpers, executing Grimoald and Childebert, and added Austrasia to his own realm. The dating of these events is greatly confu
1 CONC sed, they occurred perhaps as early as 657 or as late as 661, under Clotaire III, Clovis' son. The effective ruler however was the Neustrian major domo Ebroin, who was obliged soon thereafter (in 660 or 662) to give the Austrasian
1 CONC  realm a king of its own once more: the choice was the child king Childeric II, brother of Clotaire III, with a mayor of the palace, Wulfoald, as regent. The young king was assassinated on a hunt near Maastricht in 675, and in the
1 CONC  chaotic power struggle that ensued, the Austrasian magnates, who wanted a king of Merovingian blood, pressed Wulfoald for the return of Dagobert, while opponents of Wulfoald acclaimed one Clovis III, possibly an impostor. Ebroin 
1 CONC returned from a monastic "retirement" to lead Clovis' partisans, but Wulfoald effected Dagobert's succession in 676, partly through the help of Wilfrid, Bishop of York, on Clovis' untimely death. In spite of the continuing bitter 
1 CONC enmity of Ebroin and the party who had attempted to press Clovis as an alternate candidate, Dagobert was restored to a portion of his rightful lands, a territory along the Rhine, which pious tradition relates that he governed with
1 CONC  the mildness and piety his childhood experience had taught him, but which history suggests he left largely to the mayor of the Austrasian palace, while he concerned himself more with the founding of cloisters and abbeys, includin
1 CONC g Surburg and Wissembourg in Alsace, where the Duke was his cousin. Nonetheless, he was undoubtedly an intelligent, educated man, an adult at the time of his succession, who could not be completely controlled by factions and mayor
1 CONC s.
1 CONT
1 CONT The dynamics of Dagobert's career are largely a passive reflection of the competition between two sources of power, patronage and prestige, the palace institutions of Neustria on the one hand, and on the other, of Austrasia, firml
1 CONC y in the control of the Arnulfing dynasty that would become the Carolingians in the following century. In the chaos, the search for a consistent, rational pattern is hard to follow in the shifting loyalties.
1 CONT
1 CONT During revived conflict between Neustria and Austrasia, Dagobert in his turn was murdered in another hunting incident, December 23, 679, near Stenay-sur-Meuse in the Ardennes, probably on orders from Ebroin, still mayor of the pal
1 CONC ace in Neustria. Wilfrid must have remained in Austrasia until this time, because, according to his biographer, Wilfrid left Austrasia after the death of Dagobert, in mortal danger from the supporters of Ebroin. At the cloister of
1 CONC  Stenay afterwards there grew a cult of Dagobert, venerated as early as 1068 as "Saint Dagobert". The cult spread from there into Lotharingia and Alsace, and Saint Dagobert is recognized by the Roman Catholic Church, like his fath
1 CONC er and many royal Merovingians.
1 CONT
1 CONT After Dagobert's brief reign, leaving his lands without a male heir, the lords of the Rhineland divided the territory among themselves, while Pippin II, Mayor of the Palace of Austrasia (679 ? 714) dominated Austrasia, and left th
1 CONC e throne empty until after the battle of Tertry (687), when he accepted Theuderic III.
0 @N6000000001354338722@ NOTE [blended.FTW]
1 CONT [mergebase.FTW]
1 CONT King of Austrasia 674-678
1 CONT When Sigebert III of Austrasia died in 656, the strong Mayor of the Palace, Grimoald, siezed power for himself. He sent away the 8-year oldDagobert II, son of Sigebert III, to be a monk in Ireland, and put his son Childebert on th
1 CONC e throne. The next year, Grimoald was killed byKing Clovis II of Neustria and Burgundy, but Dagobert was not recalled. Finally, in 674, the Austrasian Mayor of the Palace, Ebroin, recalled him. Ebroin enjoyed using the young Merov
1 CONC ingian kings as puppets through which he could rule himself, and this is what he sought to do with Dagobert. However, Dagobert, being an educated man of 26 years, began ruling for himself, and so four years later was killed by Ebr
1 CONC oin.
1 CONT [Fix.FTW]
