0 @N6000000002187705124@ NOTE Pharamond
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Merovingian Dynasty Kings of All the Franks 
1 CONT
1 CONT King PharamondPharamond (c. 370 - c. 430) is considered to be the first king of the Salian Franks, though he is possibly a legendary rather than historical figure. He was possibly a son or son-in-law of Marcomer.
1 CONT
1 CONT Only the later century historicians Prosper Tyron and Dom Bouquet give a report about his reign. In 420, he reportedly led his people in crossing the Rhine River and heading west. This movement would effectively separate his tribe
1 CONC  from the majority of the Rhinefranks that had settled near Cologne. Pharamond was eventually succeeded by his son Clodio. His wife was Argotta.
0 @N6000000001744974223@ NOTE Source: Church of JC of the LDS "Ancestral File" CD-Rom database, ver 4.17.
0 @N6000000001744974257@ NOTE Source: Church of JC of the LDS "Ancestral File" CD-Rom database, ver 4.17.
0 @N6000000003051295690@ NOTE [Wikipedia, "Pharamond", retrived 4 Oct 07]
1 CONT A figure mentioned for the first time in late Gallo-Roman sources, where he is said to be an early Frankish king. After the death of Sunno, his brother Marcomer, who was leader of the Ampsivarii and Chatti, proposed to the Franks 
1 CONC that they should have one single king, contrary to their tradition. Marcomer candidated his own son Pharamond for this occupation. This story can be read in the Liber Historiae Francorum and is generally believed to be a myth by m
1 CONC odern scholars. Nothing else is known about Pharamond. Pharamond is not attested by contemporary historians. No relation of him with the Salian Franks or Merovingian dynasty is accepted as historical. Ian Wood, for example, calls 
1 CONC all Merovingians before Childeric I "legendary".
1 CONT
1 CONT As a matter of fact in several sources, for example Gregory of Tours, multiple kings are attested to rule simultaneously in later times. So it is doubtful to assume that Pharamond (if he existed at all) ever got recognized as sole
1 CONC  king. The first king who may have been close to this position was Clovis I, but after his death his empire was divided again amongst his sons who ruled again simultaneously.
1 CONT
1 CONT The myth of Pharamond has led to new legends and romances in later times. Especially pseudo-historians are even today active in making up new stories about Pharamond. In past times this has led to attempts to falsely write Pharamo
1 CONC nd into Prosper Tiro, and for example Martin Bouquet at a much later date made up a whole history of Pharamond.
1 CONT
1 CONT A Pharamond appears as the king of France in the Prose Tristan and later Arthurian works.
0 @N6000000003319677298@ NOTE
1 CONT REIGNED: King of the Franks 
1 CONT
1 CONT FILE: Colombia Encyclopedia (c) 1944; The Royal Line (Adamic Genealogy) March 1980, Albert F. Schmuhl; Peter Grard Gentala 
1 CONT
1 CONT !Royal Ancestors of Some LDS Families by Michel L. Call, chart 804. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Ancestry and Progentry of Captain James Blount - Immigrant, by Robert F. Pfafman, p E-25 and 26.
0 @N6000000003495155412@ NOTE
1 CONT Pharamond (c. 370 - c. 430) is considered to be the first king of the Salian Franks, though he is possibly a legendary rather than historical figure. He was possibly a son or son-in-law of Marcomer.
1 CONT
1 CONT Only the later century historicians Prosper Tiro and Dom Bouquet give a report about his reign. In 420, he reportedly led his people in crossing the Rhine River and heading west. This movement would effectively separate his tribe 
1 CONC from the majority of the Ripuarian Franks that had settled near Cologne. Pharamond was eventually succeeded by his son Clodio. His wife was Argotta.
