0 @N6000000002188327778@ NOTE Nepotianus
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Nepotianus
1 CONT Usurper of the Roman Empire
1 CONT Nepotianus on a coin bearing
1 CONT his claimed title of Augustus
1 CONT Reign 3 - 30 June 350
1 CONT (in competition with Magnentius)
1 CONT Full name Flavius Iulius Popilius
1 CONT Nepotianus Constantinus
1 CONT Died 30 June 350
1 CONT Rome
1 CONT Dynasty Constantinian
1 CONT Father Virius Nepotianus
1 CONT Mother Eutropia
1 CONT
1 CONT Flavius Iulius Popilius Nepotianus Constantinus (died 30 June 350) was a member of the Constantinian dynasty, and a short-lived usurper of the Roman Empire. He ruled the city of Rome for twenty-eight days, before being killed by h
1 CONC is rival usurper Magnentius' general Marcellinus.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Background
1 CONT
1 CONT Nepotianus was the son of Eutropia, half-sister of Emperor Constantine I, and of Virius Nepotianus. On his mother's side, he was the grandson of Emperor Constantius Chlorus and Flavia Maximiana Theodora.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Events
1 CONT
1 CONT After the revolt of Magnentius, Nepotianus proclaimed himself "emperor" and entered Rome with a band of gladiators on 3 June 350. After attempting to resist Nepotianus with an undisciplined force of Roman citizens, the defeated Pr
1 CONC aefectus urbi Titianus (or Anicius, or Anicetus), a supporter of Magnentius, fled the city.
1 CONT
1 CONT Magnentius quickly dealt with this revolt by sending his trusted magister officiorum Marcellinus to Rome. According to Eutropius, Nepotianus was killed in the resulting struggle (on 30 June), his head put on a lance and brought ar
1 CONC ound the city. In the following days, his mother Eutropia was also killed, during the persecution of the supporters of Nepotianus, most of whom were senators.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * DiMaio, Michael, "Nepotian (350 A.D.)", DIR
1 CONT     * Aurelius Victor De Caesaribus 42.6, Epitome 42.3
1 CONT     * Eutropius x.11
1 CONT     * Zosimus, ii.59
