0 @N6000000002188289102@ NOTE Cornelia Pompeia Magna
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Cornelia Pompeia Magna also known as Pompeia Magna Minor (Minor Latin for the younger, born after 47 BC and before 35 BC-?) was the daughter and youngest child to Pompeia Magna and suffect consul Lucius Cornelius Cinna. Cornelia’s
1 CONC  maternal grandparents were triumvir Pompey and his third wife Mucia Tertia, while her paternal grandparents were politician an elder Lucius Cornelius Cinna and an unnamed Roman woman. Her full-blooded brother was Gnaeus Cornelius
1 CONC  Cinna Magnus; she also had two half-brothers and one half-sister from her mother’s first marriage to senator Faustus Cornelius Sulla. Her mother had died before 35 BC and was raised by her father. She along with her brother were 
1 CONC born and raised in Rome.
1 CONT
1 CONT Very little is known on the life of Cornelia. Before 16, Cornelia had married Lucius Scribonius Libo consul of 16 and like her came from a senatorial family. This nobleman was actually a distant relative to Cornelia. Libo was a de
1 CONC scendant of her mother’s first marriage; his paternal grandmother was Cornelia’s elder half-sister. The Roman Emperor Tiberius, who charged Libo in planning a revolt against the emperor, stabbed Libo to death in 16.
1 CONT
1 CONT Cornelia and Libo had one daughter and only child called Scribonia who married the consul Marcus Licinius Crassus Frugi and had children.
0 @N6000000002187712482@ NOTE Claudia Antonia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Antonia (30–66 AD) was Claudius' only child from his second marriage to Aelia Paetina. Until 37 AD, she was raised by her grandmother Antonia Minor. From then until 43 AD, she was raised by her father.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 43 AD, in an arranged marriage, she first married Gnaeus Pompeius Magnus, a man of the highest birth. According to Suetonius he was murdered several years later, because he was caught in bed with his favourite boyfriend. Howeve
1 CONC r Cassius Dio states that Messalina (out of her fear, of Pompeius being a rival to Britannicus) ordered his execution, so that Antonia could marry Messalina's half-brother to strengthen the Julian blood-line. His name was Faustus 
1 CONC Cornelius Sulla Felix.
1 CONT
1 CONT Faustus Sulla and Antonia married later that year. They had a son, who lived up to his second birthday. The child was a weak one of little strength.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 58 AD, Faustus Sulla was exiled and murdered in 62 AD on Nero's orders. Antonia in 65 AD, participated in the failed revolt of Gaius Calpurnius Piso. Tacitus stated that Antonia would marry Piso, if he became Emperor. However T
1 CONC acitus was not sure if this proposal was a rumour or fact.
1 CONT
1 CONT After the death of Empress Poppaea Sabina, Nero asked her to marry him. When Antonia refused, Nero had her charged with an attempt of rebellion and executed her.
1 CONT
1 CONT With her death, passed the last living grandchild of Nero Claudius Drusus and Antonia Minor.
0 @N6000000002188289354@ NOTE Marcus Plautius Silvanus
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Marcus Plautius Silvanus was an ordinary consul in 2 BC,[1] and proconsul of Asia in 4 AD. He also served in Pannonia in 9 AD[2], Dalmatia[3] and Illyricum.[4]
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT ^ Dio Cassius, Epitome of Roman History LV.0 
1 CONT ^ Dio LV.34 
1 CONT ^ Dio LVI.12 
1 CONT ^ Velleius Paterculus II.112 
0 @N6000000002187712451@ NOTE Plautia Urgulanilla
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Plautia Urgulanilla (fl. first century) was the first wife of the future Roman Emperor Claudius. They married around 9, when Claudius was 18 years old. According to Suetonius, they divorced in 24 on grounds of adultery by Plautia 
1 CONC and suspicion of her involvement in the murder of her sister-in-law.
1 CONT
1 CONT Her father was Marcus Plautius Silvanus, a general who was consul for the year 2 BC. Urgulanilla was named for her grandmother, Urgulania, a close friend of Livia.
1 CONT
1 CONT She gave birth to a son, Claudius Drusus (who died young), and a daughter, Claudia, who was born five months after the divorce. As Claudia was widely known to be the illegitimate daughter of the freedman Boter, Claudius repudiated
1 CONC  the child and had her laid at Urgulanilla's doorstep.[1] Her adopted nephew was Tiberius Plautius Silvanus Aelianus.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Notes
1 CONT ^ Suetonius. Claud. 27. 
0 @N6000000002188327475@ NOTE Elen
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Elen (known in Welsh tradition as Elen Luyddog; also known as Saint Helen of Caernarfon) was a late 4th century founder of churches in Wales who is remembered as a saint. Traditionally she is said to have been a Romano-British pri
1 CONC ncess and the wife of Macsen or Magnus Clemens Maximus, Emperor in Britain, Gaul and Spain, where he died seeking imperial recognition in 388.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Church tradition
1 CONT
1 CONT Elen was mother of five, including a boy named Constantine, but she lived about sixty years later than Helena of Constantinople, the mother of Constantine the Great with whom she has, in times past, been confused. She is patron of
1 CONC  Llanelan in West Gower and of the church at Penisa'r-waun near Caernarfon, where her feast day is May 22. Together with her sons, Cystennin (Constantine) and Peblig (Publicius, named in the calendar of the Church in Wales), she i
1 CONC s said to have introduced into Wales the Celtic form of monasticism from Gaul. Saint Gregory of Tours and Sulpicius Severus records that Maximus and Elen met Saint Martin of Tours while they were in Gaul.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Literary tradition
1 CONT
1 CONT Elen's story is told in The Dream of Macsen Wledig, one of the tales associated with the Mabinogion. Welsh mythology remembers her as the daughter of a chieftain of north Wales named Eudaf or Eudwy, who probably lived somewhere ne
1 CONC ar the Roman base of Segontium, now Caernarfon. She is remembered for having Macsen build roads across her country so that the soldiers could more easily defend it from attackers, thus earning her the name Elen Luyddog (Elen of th
1 CONC e Hosts). She is said to have ordered the making of Sarn Helen, the great Roman road running from Caernarfon to south Wales via Dolgellau, Pennal and Bremia (Llanddewi Brefi). Though this road bears her name it is considerably old
1 CONC er than Elen's accepted time period. Many other Roman roads in Wales bear her name (e.g. Llwybr Elen) and she is thus acknowledged as the patron saint of British roadbuilders[citation needed] and the protectoress of travellers.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Farmer, David (1997). Oxford Dictionary of Saints, 4th edition. Oxford. (Listed as Helen of Caernarvon).
1 CONT     * Pennick, Thorsons (1997). The Celtic Saints. Thorsons.
0 @N6000000000437030573@ NOTE Name Suffix:<NSFX> King Of Dyfed
0 @N6000000000437379683@ NOTE Name Suffix:<NSFX> King Of Dyfed
0 @N6000000003949917683@ NOTE Son of Magnus Maximus, Antonius Donatus Gregorius (Anwyn Dynod in Welsh although he was Roman) was King of South Wales (Demetia) from 382 to c. 400, according to the following:
1 CONT DEMETIA    The British kingdom based on the Celtic tribal territory of the Demetae, and its subsequent Roman civitatus designation, was apparently created in circa.382 by one of the many sons of Magnus Maximus. It certainly bore t
1 CONC he Romanised form of its name throughout the 4th and 5th centuries. Its early centre was at Castell Dwyan. Its first king, Antonius, was charged with protecting the southern half of Wales in much the same way as Cunedda was given 
1 CONC Gwynedd as King of North Wales. Some of the territory he controlled was passed to younger sons, but which territory is not clear. The core of his holdings, however, was Demetia.
1 CONT Irish raiders were causing the British widespread problems throughout the 4th and 5th centuries, and to combat this, The homeless Deisi tribe were settled in Demetia by Magnus Maximus. Existing evidence confirms this by suggesting
1 CONC  the Roman authorities asked for the help of Aed Brosc in keeping Irish pirates away from the western coast of Britain. When the last British king died without a male heir, the Deisi were on hand to fill the breach. [Post-Roman Ce
1 CONC ltic Kingdoms website, http://homepages.tesco.net/~plk33/plk33/History/KingListsBritain/CymruDeme tia.htm]
0 @N6000000000437038595@ NOTE Gradlon Mawr, 'King' of Brittany (born c.AD 330) (Welsh-Erbin, Latin-Urbanus,English-Urban)
1 CONT
1 CONT The eldest son of Conan Meriadoc  by his second wife, St. Darerca, though known as Gradlon the Great in Brittany, but was mostly called Urban outside his kingdom. In his youth, when he was a pagan, Gradlon is said to have fallen d
1 CONC eeply in love with a beautiful half-woman/half-fairy. Unfortunatelyhowever, he later offended this mysterious creature by converting to Christianity and seeking the counsel of St. Gwenole. The fairy fled from Gradlon acrossa torre
1 CONC ntial river, warning him not to follow. The brave Breton, however, plunged headlong into the swirling waters after her. The fairy was forced to save his life, but spurned him nevertheless and hated him all the more for having made
1 CONC her admit that she loved him still. The so-called ' King' - probably a governor - took quickly to the new religion. While out hunting, one day, he became separated from his entourage and found himself lost in the great Forest of M
1 CONC enez-Hom. Almost dead from exhaustion and hunger, he eventually stumbled across thehermitage of St. Cornetin (now built upon by Plomodiern). St. Corentin kept amarvellous fish from which he cut a slice to sustain him every day. Th
1 CONC e fish would be found miraculously whole again in the morning. The saint happily sharedhis meal with the Breton King and he was thus brought back to health. As a reward for his hospitality, Gradlon made St. Corentin the first Bish
1 CONC op of Cornouaille. The King's first fairy-love returned, in later years, to possess the mindof Gradlon's daughter, Dahut, who thence became the most wicked of Breton women. She built a castle over the River Argent in the Huelgoat 
1 CONC Forest, to which adifferent suitor was invited every night. Each was given a black mask to wear so he could not look upon Dahut's face. Then the revelries began. At the end ofa thoroughly enjoyable evening, however, hidden springs
1 CONC  would shoot out from the mask and strangle the wearer! Dahut would then have the body thrown into thenearby forest caves. Gradlon's main power-base was Cornouaille and his capital was the beautiful city of Caer Ys which stood in,
1 CONC  what is now, the Bay of Douarnenez. It was a low lying city with a large dyke to keep back the sea. Gradlon kept the key to the sluice gates hung around his neck at all times. One night, however, the evil Dahut stole the key whil
1 CONC e her father was sleeping and slipped away to open the gates in some sort of sadistic game. The raging sea immediately invaded the city. Gradlon awoke with barely enough time to jump on his horse and race ahead of the torrential w
1 CONC aves. Spying his daughter in the streets,and being unaware of her crimes, he just managed to pull her up behind him. Onthey rode ahead of the waves until they reached higher ground where St.Gwenolewas waiting. "Cast off the demon 
1 CONC that is riding behind you!" he cried, but Gradlon did not understand the saint. So Gwendole pushed Dahut into the sea himself, and immediately the waves were calm once more. Gradlon made his new capitalat Corspotium (Quimper), whe
1 CONC re his statue can be seen in front of the Cathedral. It is said that the bells of the churches of Ys can still be heard on stormynights at sea. Upon Gradlon's death, he was succeeded by his son, Salomon I; followed by his grandson
1 CONC , Aldrien . Generally considered legendary.
