0 @N6000000002188289060@ NOTE Lucius Cornelius Cinna (suffect consul 32 BC)
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Lucius Cornelius Cinna (flourished 1st century BC) was the son of an elder Lucius Cornelius Cinna, who was a supporter of politician Gaius Marius and his mother is an unnamed Roman woman. His sister was Cornelia Cinna minor, was t
1 CONC he first wife of dictator Gaius Julius Caesar and was the maternal uncle of their daughter Julia Caesaris. Cinna was a member of the Cornelius (gens), who were of patrician status.
1 CONT
1 CONT Cinna in 78 BC, allied himself with Marcus Aemilius Lepidus in overthrowing the Roman constitution of dictator Lucius Cornelius Sulla. After the defeat and death of Lepidus in Sardinia, Cinna accompanied Marcus Perperna to join ge
1 CONC neral Quintus Sertorius in Spain. Caesar used Cinna, against various Roman Senators in the Roman Senate and was able to recall Cinna from exile back to Rome.
1 CONT
1 CONT Due to his father’s association with Marius and Sulla’s constitutions reforms, he was unable to promote his career, until Caesar’s rise. When Caesar became dictator, Cinna promoted to the praetorship. Although Cinna, strongly disa
1 CONC pproved of Caesar’s way of governing, he wouldn’t associate himself with the senators who wanted Caesar killed.
1 CONT
1 CONT After Caesar was assassinated in March 44 BC, the senators were in such rage of Cinna, they accidentally murdered tribune of the plebs Helvius Cinna, thinking it was he. When the murder of the tribune took place, Cinna was walking
1 CONC  in Caesar’s funeral procession. Cinna during the political chaos didn’t promote his career, even not claiming a Roman province, for him to govern. Cicero praises him for this act. Cinna in 32 BC, served as a suffect consul.
1 CONT
1 CONT After 47 BC, Cinna married Pompeia Magna, the daughter of triumvir Pompey and from his third wife Mucia Tertia. Cinna married Pompeia has her second husband. Pompeia married Cinna as his first wife. Pompeia had become a widow, who
1 CONC  her husband Faustus Cornelius Sulla had died in battle. Cinna became a stepfather to Pompeia’s children from her first marriage. Pompeia bore Cinna two children who were: a son Gnaeus Cornelius Cinna Magnus and a daughter Corneli
1 CONC a Pompeia Magna. His wife died before 35 BC and beyond this no more is known on Cinna.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Sources
1 CONT Microsoft Encarta Encyclopaedia 2002 
1 CONT http://www.ancientlibrary.com/smith-bio/0763.html 
1 CONT http://www.ancientlibrary.com/smith-bio/0764.html 
1 CONT http://www.ancientlibrary.com/smith-bio/2806.html 
0 @N6000000002188289094@ NOTE Gnaeus Cornelius Cinna Magnus
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Gnaeus Cornelius Cinna Magnus (born after 47 BC and before 35 BC-?) was the son of suffect consul Lucius Cornelius Cinna and Pompeia Magna. His sister was Cornelia Pompeia Magna.
1 CONT
1 CONT His maternal grandparents were Roman triumvir Pompey and Mucia Tertia, while paternal grandparents were consul Lucius Cornelius Cinna and an unnamed Roman woman. Cinna is the only grandson of Pompey, that has the name ‘Magnus’ and
1 CONC  is the only grandson that has partly his name.
1 CONT
1 CONT Cinna became a supporter to Roman triumvir Mark Antony. He was promoted to a priesthood. In 4, Cinna and Aemilia Lepida, the granddaughter of triumvir Marcus Aemilius Lepidus were involved in a conspiracy against the Roman Emperor
1 CONC  Augustus. Cinna and Lepida were pardoned by the emperor. Cinna served as a consul in 5.
1 CONT
1 CONT Cinna is the main character of many Latin plays and of a tragedy called Cinna by the famous 17th century French playwright Corneille.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Sources:
1 CONT http://www.ancientlibrary.com/smith-bio/0763.html 
1 CONT http://www.ancientlibrary.com/smith-bio/0764.html 
1 CONT http://www.ancientlibrary.com/smith-bio/2806.html 
0 @N6000000002188289022@ NOTE Faustus (II) Cornelius Sulla
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Faustus Cornelius Sulla (49 BC-9) was a Roman Republic general and consul. The eldest son of the notable Faustus Cornelius Sulla by his wife Pompeia Magna daughter of Pompey and a was general of the Legio XVI Gallica and consul un
1 CONC der the Principitate of Gaius Julius Caesar Octavianus. Young Faustus Sulla was also the eldest grandson of the Dictator Lucius Cornelius Sulla Felix. He was born in Arrentium in 49 BC.
1 CONT
1 CONT Due to his ancestry, Faustus Sulla was in a strong position to succeed in the world of Roman politics. Although his father had supported Pompey in the Civil War, his uncle, the great General Barbatus Cornelius Sulla Albinus was on
1 CONC e of Octavian's most loyal adherents, and was instrumental in the Battle of Actium. As the ward of Barbatus, young Faustus was favoured by the Augustan court. From his youth, he was a close friend to the imperial step-son Tiberius
1 CONC  Claudius Nero, and served as his legate in Germania.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sulla was a natural soldier and, after his stint as Praetor, was given command of the prestigious XVI Legion stationed in Illyrica. He fell out of favour when it became known that he had fathered a child on his illegitimate half-b
1 CONC rother's daughter Ulpia, in return for giving his poor relation money. The child was not considered a member of the Patrician Cornelii, and was known as Gnaeus Ulpius Trajanus, after his grandfather and place of birth.
1 CONT
1 CONT After living in exile in Gades for seven years, Sulla was belatedly awarded the consulship at the age of 56, in partnership with Tiberius Nero. At this point in his life, Sulla's 40 year old son Lucius had already been consul, and
1 CONC  his lack of political recognition had been a source of great embarrassment. His elder son, born Faustus, had been adopted out to his uncle Barbatus, who was almost certain sterile, and was known as Barbatus Cornelius Sulla Albinu
1 CONC s Faustullus.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sulla eventually became a pontifex maximus, but could not be considered 'Augustified' as he tried to overthrow Octavian (future Roman Emperor Augustus) in 9. He was forced to commit suicide, and it is believed that his estate was 
1 CONC taken by Octavian.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Marriages and Children
1 CONT First Wife, Junia Albina 
1 CONT Faustus Cornelius Sulla, adopted as Barbatus Cornelius Sulla Albinus Faustullus 
1 CONT Lucius Cornelius Sulla Faustus 
1 CONT Fausta Sulla 
1 CONT Proculus Cornelius Sulla Faustus 
1 CONT Cossus Cornelius Sulla Faustus 
1 CONT Second Wife, Caecilia Nepos 
1 CONT Illyricus Cornelius Sulla 
1 CONT by half-niece Ulpia 
1 CONT Gnaeus Ulpius Trajanus
0 @N6000000000438256174@ NOTE Emperor
0 @N6000000003796585793@ NOTE Claudius 41 - 54 AD
1 CONT
1 CONT Polio in childhood had left Claudius I (AD 41-54) with a limp and a stammer, but he ruled well and added Britain to the Roman Empire. He showed both intelligence and compassion in his grants of citizenship, his admission of Gauls 
1 CONC to the Senate, and his humanitarian legislation on debt and the treatment of slaves. His fourth wife Agrippina (known as Agrippina the Younger) poisoned him to ensure that her son Nero would inherit the throne.
1 CONT
1 CONT Tiberius Claudius Caesar Augustus Germanicus was born in 41 AD to Nero Claudius Drusus and his wife Antonia. Although he came from a royal blood line, his family had a very low opinion of his abilities and often ignored him. Label
1 CONC ed an invalid from birth because of physical disabilities including partial paralysis, stammering, slobbering, and limping, he was the last person his family thought would inherit the throne and serve as Roman Emperor. An outcast 
1 CONC in his home environment, Claudius turned to the study of history to occupy his time. He authored various works about orthographic reform of the Roman alphabet and a work defending Cicero, a republican politician and orator. Claudi
1 CONC us also enjoyed playing dice games.
1 CONT
1 CONT Claudius' rise to power came after Emperor Gauis (Caligula), his nephew, was unexpectedly murdered on January 1, AD 41. Claudius became heir to the throne, to many a Roman's dismay. The soldiers, courtiers, freedman, and foreigner
1 CONC s were his main support although the senatorial aristocracy also offered to back the new emperor. Many Romans sought to have Claudius assassinated because of his cruel and ruthless discussions and actions with members of the senat
1 CONC e and knighthood. It is thought by some that he even executed senators on occasion. Despite this conflict Claudius did respect these agencies and gave new opportunities to them both.
1 CONT
1 CONT Claudius' reign is marked with an expansion of the Roman Empire. He invaded and conquered Britain in AD 43 and captured Camulodunum. There he started a colony of veterans and built client-kingdoms to protect the small populated la
1 CONC nd. Claudius also took over North Africa and annexed Mauretania, where he established two provinces as well. Around AD 49 he also annexed Iturea and allowed the province of Syria to control it, trying not to come into conflict wit
1 CONC h the Germans and the Parthians.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the area of civil administration he encouraged urbanization. The judicial system improved under his reign and he favored the modern extension by individual and collective grants in Noricum. Claudius also made many administrativ
1 CONC e innovations. He increased his control over finances and province administration and gave jurisdiction of fiscal matters to the governors under him in the senatorial provinces.
1 CONT
1 CONT Claudius' personal life was wrought with conflicts that ultimately led to his undoing. He married three times. His first wife, Boudicca, started a revolt, and his second wife had a strong sexual appetite that led her to conspiracy
1 CONC  and ultimately, her execution. Claudius' third time was not a charm either. He decided to stay within the family and married his niece, Aggripina. She was very influential over Claudius to the point where he adopted her son Nero.
1 CONC  Then she fed Claudius a dinner containing poisonous mushrooms which killed him. Her main motive was that her precious son, Nero, might inherit the throne.
1 CONT
1 CONT Although Claudius was generally thought of as a weak leader and was labeled, even by his own family, as someone not worthy to rule; he made notable contributions to the development of the Roman empire. He conquered and colonized B
1 CONC ritain, established provinces in North Africa, and he urbanized and innovated his civil administration. He died an unnecessary and tragic death at the hand of his own wife and was succeeded by his adopted son, Nero.
0 @N6000000003051222023@ NOTE [from "Claudius I," Microsoft(R) Encarta(R) 98 Encyclopedia. (c) 1993-1997 Microsoft Corporation.]
1 CONT Claudius was born TIBERIUS CLAUDIUS DRUSUS NERO GERMANICUS in Lugdunum (present-day Lyon, France). His father, Nero Claudius Drusus, was a younger brother of Tiberius Claudius Nero Caesar, later the Roman emperor Tiberius. Claudiu
1 CONC s held no important public office until the age of 47, when he became consul during the reign of his nephew, Emperor Caligula. When the latter was assassinated in AD 41, Claudius was proclaimed emperor by the Praetorian Guard, who
1 CONC  found him hiding in the palace. The first acts of his reign gave promise of mild and just government; but in 42, when a conspiracy against his life was uncovered, he went into semiretirement. His wife Messalina became largely res
1 CONC ponsible for administering the government for a time. She practiced cruelties and extortions without restraint. Aside from the excesses perpetrated under the influence of Messalina, Claudius's reign was that of an able administrat
1 CONC or, both in civil and military affairs. Mauretania (present-day northern Morocco and western Algeria) was made a Roman province; the conquest of Britain was begun; and the Roman armies fought successfully against the Germans. Jude
1 CONC a and Thrace also became Roman provinces during his rule. Claudius expended enormous sums in building, especially in the construction of the famous Claudian Aqueduct. His administration was characterized by a decline in the power 
1 CONC of the nobility and by the practice, later commonplace, of granting responsibility and wealth to the personal followers of the emperor, including former slaves.
1 CONT In 48 Claudius ordered the execution of Messalina, who had indicated her contempt for him by publicly staging a mock marriage with her lover. He then defied widespread disapproval by marrying his niece, Agrippina the Younger, unde
1 CONC r whose influence he deprived his son by Messalina, Britannicus, of his heritage, adopting instead Agrippina's son by a former marriage, Nero, later emperor of Rome. Shortly thereafter Claudius was poisoned, presumably by Agrippin
1 CONC a. Claudius is depicted by ancient historians as being neglected, sickly, and ridiculed before coming to power; his character during his reign is described as ignorant and malicious. Modern scholars, however, tend to discount thei
1 CONC r testimony and estimate him as shrewd and able.
