0 @N6000000002188329503@ NOTE Conan Meriadoc
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT
1 CONT Conan Meriadoc (modern Breton Konan Meriadeg, Welsh Cynan Meiriadog; Latin Conanus Meridiadocus; died ca. 426) was the legendary 4th century founder of the house of Rohan and legendary leader of the earliest Brythonic settlement i
1 CONC n Armorica.
1 CONT
1 CONT According to medieval Welsh sources (including vernacular texts and Geoffrey of Monmouth), Conan Meriadoc was the cousin (by marriage) of Macsen Wledig (Magnus Maximus) and nephew of Octavius. After he rebelled against Octavius, M
1 CONC acsen summoned him before departing to establish himself as Roman emperor, and gave Conan Armorica to rule as his own kingdom. Conan then led the settlement of Armorica by Welsh mercenaries and gave it the name of Llydaw (later Br
1 CONC ittany, or "Little Britain").
1 CONT
1 CONT In the Cornish miracle play Beunans Meriasek, Conan is a kinsman of St Meriasek who tries (unsuccessfully) to dissuade Meriasek from pursuing a religious life.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Fiction
1 CONT
1 CONT J.R.R. Tolkien alludes to the historical Meriadoc in his Lord of the Rings trilogy, where the hobbit Meriadoc ("Merry") becomes the adjutant of the king of the fictional kingdom Rohan.
1 CONT -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT Conan Meriadoc,
1 CONT 'King' of Dumnonia & Brittany
1 CONT (Born c.AD 305)
1 CONT (Welsh: Cynan; Latin: Conanus; English: Conan)
1 CONT
1 CONT Conan was the son of Octavius the Old's brother, Gerontius, (or Octavius himself according to the Dream of Macsen Wledig). He had expected to inherit his uncle's position of influence in what is now Wales, until his cousin, Helena
1 CONC , married the Roman citizen, Magnus Maximus. Originally, Conan was considerably put out by this man's rise to power and he organised a rebellion against him, aided by Picts & Scots. However, being defeated, Conan became great frie
1 CONC nds with his rival and travelled with Maximus to the continent to help him become Emperor of the West. He was given control over Armorica (modern Brittany) as a reward for killing the previous holder of the title. His new province
1 CONC , was well settled by his men-at-arms, however they lacked wives. Therefore, in order to populate the area, he sent to his uncle's old ally, Donaut of Dumnonia for numerous Cornish ladies. Conan proposed to cement the alliance of 
1 CONC their two peoples by marrying Donaut's daughter, Ursula. Though Donaut was delighted at the match, Ursula had her heart set on a life devoted only to God. She agreed to the marriage, on the condition that she first be allowed to g
1 CONC o on a pilgrimage throughout Europe. Conan appears to have joined her in Rome, where the two must have been married, probably by Pope Cyriacus himself. He apparently did not travel to Cologne where Ursula died. In later years, he 
1 CONC gained considerable influence in Dumnonia after the death of his father-in-law. The Governorship of Armorica & Dumnonia was united for only a short time. As his second wife, Conan married Dareca of Ireland, a supposed, though unli
1 CONC kely, sister of St. Patrick. Upon Conan's death, around AD 395, control of each region fell to the sons of his two marriages, Gadeon and Gradlon.
