1 CONT Anywhere beyond this point is solely based on the Bible and some archelogical evidence. I can not prove nor disprove the content in the Bible on what it holds on historical geneology. 
1 CONT -Bradley Kassian 
0 @N6000000002187718445@ NOTE Herod Antipas
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Herod Antipas (born 20 BC) was an ancient leader (Tetrarch) of Galilee and Peraea. A son of Herod the Great and Malthace, who was from Samaria, Herod Antipas became Tetrarch in 4 BC upon the death of his father. His first task was
1 CONC  to restore order caused by the rebellion of the Jewish Feast of Pentecost (Shavuot) in that year.
1 CONT
1 CONT Herod Antipas followed in his father's footsteps as a builder. He rebuilt Sepphoris in Galilee and Livia in Perea, but his most noted accomplishment was the construction of Tiberias as his capital on the western shore of the Sea o
1 CONC f Galilee. The city was named to honor his patron, Emperor Tiberius. The city gave its name to the sea, and was for a long time a great school and center of Jewish learning.
1 CONT
1 CONT He married Phasaelis, who was the daughter of Aretas IV, king in Arabia. He divorced her and married Herodias, the wife of his half-brother Herod Philip; for which he and Herodias were condemned by John the Baptist and blamed by F
1 CONC lavius Josephus (Jewish Antiquities, XVIII, v). The union with Herodias brought him to ruin, for it involved him in war with his original father-in-law, in which he lost an army. A later Christian interpolation inserted in Josephu
1 CONC s' Antiquities moralizes the calamity
1 CONT
1 CONT 'as a punishment for what he did against John that was called the Baptist; for Herod slew him, who was a good man, and commanded the Jews to exercise virtue, both as to righteousness towards one another, and piety towards God, and
1 CONC  so to come to baptism' (Antiquities, XVIII, v, 2). 
1 CONT Herod Antipas was exiled by the Roman Emperor Gaius Caesar Caligula to Lugdunum (modern Lyons), in Gaul in AD 39 according to Josephus (Antiquities) who says, however, in the Jewish Wars (II, ix, 6) "So Herod died in Spain whither
1 CONC  his wife had followed him".
1 CONT
1 CONT A much later spurious 'letter of Herod Antipas' is sometimes naively cited as being in 'records of the Roman senate.' The reference itself is equally spurious; there are no such records of the Roman Senate.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Herod Antipas in the New Testament
1 CONT Herod Antipas is featured in the New Testament for ordering the execution of John the Baptist and for his role in the trial of Jesus.
1 CONT
1 CONT Josephus simply attributes John the Baptist's execution to Herod's uneasy jealousy over John's influence. In the Gospels of Matthew and Mark, Herod's motives are more complex. In the Gospel accounts, there is a celebration for Her
1 CONC od's birthday. At the celebration, Herodias's daughter (never named but traditionally called Salomé), dances for Herod. The dance pleases Herod so much that he swears he will give the girl whatever she desires. Prompted by her mot
1 CONC her Herodias, the girl asks for the head of John the Baptist to be brought to her on a platter. Herod relucantly agrees to honor his oath and orders the execution of John the Baptist. (Matthew 14:3-12; Mark 6:17-29) This story ser
1 CONC ves as the inspiration for much legend, culminating in Richard Strauss' opera Salome, to a libretto based on the play by Oscar Wilde.
1 CONT
1 CONT Herod Antipas is also famous as the Tetrarch at the time of the ministry and trial of Jesus in the Gospel of Luke (Luke 23:7-13). When Judea's Roman governor, the prefect Pontius Pilate, learns that Jesus is a Galilean (and theref
1 CONC ore under Herod's juridiction), he sends Jesus to Herod, who is also in Jerusalem at that time. Herod hopes to see Jesus perform miracles but Jesus does not perform any. Herod asks Jesus many questions, but Jesus does not reply. H
1 CONC erod and his soldiers therefore mock and ridicule Jesus. After dressing Jesus in an elegant robe, Herod sends him back to Pilate
