0 @N6000000002187718049@ NOTE Claudia Marcella
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Claudia Marcella was the name of both daughters of Octavia the sister of Caesar Augustus, and her first husband, the consul Gaius Claudius Marcellus.
1 CONT
1 CONT The elder, Claudia Marcella Major (b. 44 BC), was married to Marcus Vipsanius Agrippa in 28 BC to cement a political alliance between Agrippa and her uncle. Their daughter Vipsania Marcella was born in Rome in 27 BC. In 21 BC Agri
1 CONC ppa divorced Claudia to marry Augustus' daughter Julia Caesaris. She then married Iullus Antonius, the son of Mark Antony, who was later exiled for adultery with Julia. Their son Lucius Antonius was born in 20 or 19 BC. After Iull
1 CONC us' exile, Claudia married Sextus Appuleius, the consul of AD 14. In AD 17 their daughter Appuleia Varilla was charged with adultery and speaking insultingly about the imperial family. Although she was acquitted, her loved Manlius
1 CONC  was banished from Italy and Africa.
1 CONT
1 CONT Her younger sister, Claudia Marcella Minor (b. 43 BC), was married first to the consul Aemilius Lepidus Paullus. Their daughter Claudia Pulchra was born in 14 BC. After Paullus' death in 13 BC, Claudia married the consul Marcus Va
1 CONC lerius Messalla Messallinus. Through their son, Marcus Valerius Messalla Barbatus, she was the grandmother of the emperor Claudius' wife Messalina.
0 @N6000000002187712560@ NOTE Fulvia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Fulvia (died 40 BC) was a Roman matron remembered by her ambition and political activity, in a time when women were expected to stay home and live with virtue and modesty, according to Roman morals. She was the first non mythologi
1 CONC cal woman represented in Roman coins.
1 CONT
1 CONT Fulvia was born in the 1st century BC in an uncertain date. She was daughter of Marcus Fulvius Flaccus Bambulus (a member of a senatorial family) and Sempronia, daughter of Gaius Gracchus. Her maternal great-grandmother was Cornel
1 CONC ia Africana, who lived her life according to all the traditions that Fulvia was about to break. As heiress to the Gracchi estate, after the death of her grandfather and Tiberius Gracchus, Fulvia was a very wealthy woman. Her famil
1 CONC y was not patrician but highly respected by Roman elite.
1 CONT
1 CONT Her first husband was Publius Clodius Pulcher, a demagogue politician famous for causing instability in Rome's internal affairs, often involved in conspiracies and known to resort to violence. It is said that Fulvia financially su
1 CONC pported her husband's career and inspired most of his actions. Clodius was (killed by Milo in a battle that erupted between these sworn enemies and their retainers in a chance encounter at Bovillae outside Rome) in 52 BC, leaving 
1 CONC Fulvia a widow. Not for long. Afterwards, she married Gaius Scribonius Curio, an influential and talented tribune whose defection to Caesar in exchange for an enormous bribe swung the balance in Caesar's favor in his struggle with
1 CONC  the Senate in 50 BC. At the outbreak of the Civil War Caesar entrusted Curio with an expedition to conquer Africa, but through overconfidence he allowed himself to be outmaneuvered by King Juba and he and his troops were annihila
1 CONC ted--the only serious defeat suffered by Caesar's forces during the Civil War.
1 CONT
1 CONT Fulvia's own political career started with her third marriage, to Mark Antony. Plutarch said that she needed husbands with an active political profile and the ambitious Antony was highly qualified. As Clodius had done previously, 
1 CONC Antony was happy to accept her money to boost his career.
1 CONT
1 CONT Following Julius Caesar's assassination in March 15, 44 BC, Antony formed the second triumvirate with Octavian and Lepidus and embarked on a savage proscription. To solidify the political alliance, Fulvia offered her daughter, Clo
1 CONC dia, to young Octavian as wife. Antony pursued his political enemies, chief among them being Marcus Tullius Cicero, who had criticized him openly for abusing his powers as consul after Caesar's assassination. In the proscription, 
1 CONC Antony dispatched search parties to Cicero's country homes to track him down. He was found and beheaded by a Roman centurion, Herennius, whom Cicero had previously defended successfully in a murder trial, after his whereabouts wer
1 CONC e revealed by a young slave to whom Cicero had shown special favor. Antony exhibited Cicero's head and hands at the rostra in the Forum.
1 CONT
1 CONT Fulvia was happy to take revenge against Cicero for Antony's sake, but also in revenge for Publius Clodius Pulcher, her first husband, also an earlier victim of Cicero's sharp rhetoric. Plutarch and other sources describe the joy 
1 CONC with which she pierced the tongue of the dead Cicero with her golden hairpins, as a final revenge against Cicero's power of speech.
1 CONT
1 CONT Shortly afterwards, triumvirs then distributed the provinces among them. Lepidus took the west and Antony went to the province of Egypt, where he met Cleopatra. Octavian remained in Italy, where he was busy taking lands from Itali
1 CONC ans and giving them to the triumvirate veterans.
1 CONT
1 CONT These actions caused political and social unrest, but when Octavian asked for a divorce from Clodia, Fulvia herself decided to take action. Together with Lucius Antonius, her brother-in-law, she raised eight legions in Italy to fi
1 CONC ght for Antony's rights against Octavian. The army occupied Rome for a short time, but eventually retreated to Perusia (modern Perugia). Octavian besieged Fulvia and Lucius Antonius in the winter of 41 - 40 BC, starving them into 
1 CONC surrender. Fulvia was exiled to Sicyon, where she died of a sudden illness, while Antony was en route to meet her.
1 CONT
1 CONT Her death opened a space for Octavian and Antony to reconcile. Now a widower, Antony married Octavian's sister Octavia. Later it would be Octavia who took care of Fulvia's children.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Fulvia's marriages and descendants
1 CONT First marriage, Publius Clodius Pulcher 
1 CONT Clodia Pulchra, briefly married to Octavian 
1 CONT Second marriage, Gaius Scribonius Curio 
1 CONT Third marriage, Mark Antony 
1 CONT Marcus Antonius Antyllus, executed by Octavian in 31 BC 
1 CONT Iullus Antonius 
1 CONT See also: Women in Rome - Scipio-Paullus-Gracchus family tree
1 CONT
1 CONT  This biographical article related to the military is a stub. You can help Wikipedia by expanding it. 
1 CONT
1 CONT   This feminism-related article is a stub. You can help Wikipedia by expanding it. 
