0 @N6000000002187718211@ NOTE Marcus Aemilius Lepidus (triumvir)
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT This page is about the contemporary of Caesar Augustus. For other generations of his family see Marcus Aemilius Lepidus.
1 CONT
1 CONT Marcus Aemilius Lepidus (Latin: M·AEMILIVS·M·F·Q·N·LEPIDVS¹), died 13 BC, was a patrician Roman politician of the 1st century BC. Known simply as Lepidus he was the son of Marcus Aemilius Lepidus and Appuleia, the daughter of the 
1 CONC rebellious Lucius Appuleius Saturninus. His father was also involved in a rebellion against the Republic and died because of it. He was a member of the second triumvirate and pontifex maximus. Lepidus was among Julius Caesar's gre
1 CONC atest supporters. He started his cursus honorum as a praetor in 49 BC, and was rewarded with the consulship in 47 BC, after the defeat of the Pompeians in the East. Following Caesar's assassination in March 15, 44 BC (the Ides of 
1 CONC March), Lepidus allied himself with Mark Antony in a joint bid for power. But Caesar had left an heir: Octavian, his great-nephew and adopted son, had a matching ambition and the popularity with which to climb the hierarchy. Toget
1 CONC her they formed the second triumvirate, legalized with the name of Triumvirs for the Organization of the People by the Lex Titia of 43 BC. The triumvirate had a legal life span of five years and was renewed in 38 BC with the Treat
1 CONC y of Tarentum for an equal period of time.
1 CONT
1 CONT After the pacification of the East and the defeat of the assassins faction, during which Lepidus remained in Rome, he went to rule the western provinces of Hispania and Africa. Somehow, Lepidus managed to remain aside the frequent
1 CONC  quarrels between his colleagues Antony and Octavian. However, in 36 BC, an ill-judged political move was the excuse that Octavian needed. Lepidus was accused of usurping power in Sicily and attempt of rebellion and was forced to 
1 CONC exile. All of his offices were taken away, except the one of pontifex maximus.
1 CONT
1 CONT Lepidus died peacefully in 13 BC. Lepidus was the brother of Lucius Paullus and father to Lepidus the Younger.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Notes
1 CONT 1- English: "Marcus Aemilius Lepidus, son of Marcus, nephew of Quintus".
0 @N6000000002188286927@ NOTE Drusus Caesar
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Drusus Julius Caesar, also referred to as Drusus III (7 – 33), was a member of a noble family of Ancient Rome. He was a son of Germanicus and Agrippina the Elder.
1 CONT
1 CONT Drusus married Aemilia Lepida, daughter of Marcus Aemilius Lepidus, his second cousin. Tacitus reports that during their marriage "she had pursued her husband with ceaseless accusations". In 36, she was charged with adultery with 
1 CONC a slave and committed suicide, "since there was no question about her guilt" (Tacitus, Annals 6.40).
1 CONT
1 CONT Drusus' paternal grandparents were Nero Claudius Drusus and Antonia Minor. His maternal grandparents were Marcus Vipsanius Agrippa and Julia the Elder. He was adopted by his paternal great-uncle Tiberius after Tiberius' own son, J
1 CONC ulius Caesar Drusus, had died.
1 CONT
1 CONT Drusus Caesar was later accused of plotting against Tiberius and was imprisoned in 30, a year after his mother Agrippina the Elder and his brother Nero Caesar were arrested. He starved to death in prison in 33, reduced to chewing 
1 CONC the stuffing of his bed (Annals 6.23).
1 CONT
1 CONT His siblings included four brothers (Tiberius and Gaius Julius, who died young; Nero, and Caligula) and three sisters (Julia Livilla, Julia Drusilla and Agrippina the younger).
0 @N6000000002188289081@ NOTE Julia (daughter of Julius Caesar)
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Julia Caesaris (Classical Latin: IVLIA•CAESARIS) was the daughter of Gaius Julius Caesar the dictator, by his first wife, Cornelia Cinna, and his only child in marriage.[1] Julia became the fourth wife of Pompey the Great and was 
1 CONC renowned for her beauty and virtue.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Life 
1 CONT 2 Cronology 
1 CONT 3 Cultural depictions of Julia 
1 CONT 3.1 Fiction 
1 CONT 3.2 Television 
1 CONT 4 Notes 
1 CONT 5 References 
1 CONT 5.1 Primary sources 
1 CONT 5.2 Secondary sources 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Life
1 CONT Julia was born around 83 BC–82 BC.[2] After her mother died in childbirth around 69 BC[3]–68 BC[2], she was raised by her paternal grandmother Aurelia Cotta. Her father wanted her to marry Faustus Cornelius Sulla, but she got enga
1 CONC ged to a certain Quintus Servilius Caepio.[4] Caesar broke off this engagement and married her to Pompey in April 59 BC, with whom Caesar sought a strong political alliance in forming the First Triumvirate. This family-alliance of
1 CONC  its two great chiefs was regarded as the firmest bond between Caesar and Pompey, and was accordingly viewed with much alarm by the oligarchal party in Rome, especially by Marcus Tullius Cicero and Cato the Younger.[5][6][7][8][9]
1 CONC [10][11]
1 CONT
1 CONT Pompey was supposedly infatuated with his bride. The personal charms of Julia were remarkable: she was a woman of beauty and virtue; and although policy prompted her union, and she was twenty-three years younger than her husband, 
1 CONC she pos­sessed in Pompey a devoted husband, to whom she was, in return, devotedly attached.[12] A rumor suggested that the aging conqueror was losing interest in politics in favor of domestic life with his young wife. In fact, Pom
1 CONC pey had been given the governorship of Hispania Ulterior, but had been permitted to remain in Rome to oversee the Roman grain supply as curator annonae, exercising his command through subordinates.[13]
1 CONT
1 CONT Julia died before a breach between her husband and father had become inevitable.[14][15][16][17] At the election of aediles in 55 BC, Pompey was surrounded by a tumultuous mob, and his gown was sprinkled with blood of the rioters.
1 CONC  A slave carried the stained toga to his house on the Carinae and was seen by Julia. Imagining that her husband was slain, she fell into premature labour,[18][19] and her constitution received an irreparable shock. In August of th
1 CONC e next year, 54 BC, she died in childbed,[20] and her infant —a son, according to some writers,[21][22][23] a daughter, according to others,[24][25]—survived her only a few days.[26] Caesar was in Britain, according to Seneca,[27]
1 CONC  when he received the tidings of Julia's death.[28]
1 CONT
1 CONT Pompey wished her ashes to repose in his favourite Alban villa, but the Roman people, who loved Julia, determined they should rest in the field of Mars. For permission a special decree of the senate was necessary, and Lucius Domit
1 CONC ius Ahenobarbus, one of the consuls of 54 BC, impelled by his hatred to Pompey and Caesar, procured an interdict from the tribunes. But the popular will prevailed, and, after listening to a funeral oration in the forum, the people
1 CONC  placed her urn in the Campus Martius.[29] Ten years later the official pyre for Caesar's cremation would be erected near the tomb of his daughter,[30][31] but the people intervened after the laudatio funebris by Marcus Antonius a
1 CONC nd cremated Caesar's body in the Forum.
1 CONT
1 CONT After Julia’s death Pompey and Caesar’s alliance began to fade which resulted in Caesar's civil war. It was remarked, as a singular omen, that on the day Augustus entered the city as Caesar's adoptive son, the monument of Julia wa
1 CONC s struck by lightning.[32] Caesar himself vowed a ceremony to her manes, which he exhibited in 46 BC as extensive funeral games including gladiatorial combats.[33][34][35] The date of the ceremony was chosen to coincide with the l
1 CONC udi Veneris Genetricis in September 26,[36] the festival in honor of Venus Genetrix, the divine ancestress of the Julians.[37]
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Cronology
1 CONT 83 BC–82 BC — Julia's birth 
1 CONT 69 BC–68 BC — Cornelia Cinna (Julia's mother and Caesar's first wife) dies in childbirth 
1 CONT 59 BC — marriage to Pompey 
1 CONT 55 BC — Julia has a miscarriage 
1 CONT 54 BC — Julia dies in childbed, her child only survives a few days 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Cultural depictions of Julia
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Fiction
1 CONT In Dante Alighieri's epic poem The Divine Comedy (14th century), Julia was encountered by Dante in the first circle of Hell, the Limbo (where rest souls who are not in torture, the pagans that lived righteous existences):[38] 
1 CONT [...] The foremost circle that surrounds the abyss. [...] 
1 CONT [...] I knew, who in that Limbo were suspended. [...] 
1 CONT [...] Lucretia, Julia, Marcia, and Cornelia, [...] 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Television
1 CONT In Julius Caesar (2002 television movie), the role of young Julia is played by Alexandra Morris,[39][40] while the role of grown-up Julia is played by Italian actress Nicole Grimaudo.[41] 
1 CONT Julia's death is portrayed in the premiere episode of HBO's 2005 television series Rome. However, Julia actually died in childbirth in 54 BC, at least 2 years before the events of this episode. 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Notes
